// Recall-mode questions — Biochemistry
// Format: {s: tag, q: stem, choices: [4], ans: 0-3 index into choices, exp: explanation}
const QB_BIOCHEMISTRY = [
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Transcription',q:'A _____-sense RNA viral genome has the same sequence and polarity as mRNA, so host ribosomes can translate it directly into protein',choices:['negative','ambisense','positive','antisense'],ans:2,exp:'**Positive-sense (+) RNA** is read like mRNA, so it can be translated immediately on entering the cell (e.g., poliovirus, coronaviruses). Negative-sense (antisense) RNA is complementary to mRNA and must first be copied by a viral RNA-dependent RNA polymerase; making DNA from RNA (reverse transcription) is done only by retroviruses.'},
  {s:'General',q:'Which amino acid is the only non-chiral standard amino acid?',choices:['Alanine','Glycine','Serine','Proline'],ans:1,exp:'**Glycine** has a hydrogen as its R group, so its α-carbon carries two identical H atoms and is not a stereocenter. Alanine, the next simplest, has a methyl group and is chiral like every other standard amino acid.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which amino acid has a methyl group (-CH3) as its side chain and is the simplest chiral amino acid?',choices:['Glycine','Valine','Alanine','Serine'],ans:2,exp:'**Alanine** has a -CH3 side chain, making it a small, nonpolar amino acid whose α-carbon bears four different groups. Glycine is simpler but achiral because its side chain is just H.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which branched-chain amino acid has an isopropyl side chain and is essential?',choices:['Leucine','Isoleucine','Valine','Alanine'],ans:2,exp:'**Valine** has an isopropyl (-CH(CH3)2) side chain and is one of the three essential branched-chain amino acids. Leucine has an isobutyl group and isoleucine a sec-butyl group; all three are catabolized mainly in muscle rather than liver.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which branched-chain amino acid is purely ketogenic?',choices:['Valine','Isoleucine','Lysine','Leucine'],ans:3,exp:'**Leucine** is degraded only to acetyl-CoA and acetoacetate, so it cannot contribute to net glucose synthesis. Lysine is also purely ketogenic but is not branched-chain; valine is glucogenic and isoleucine is both.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which branched-chain amino acid is both glucogenic and ketogenic and has two chiral centers?',choices:['Leucine','Valine','Threonine','Isoleucine'],ans:3,exp:'**Isoleucine** has a sec-butyl side chain with chiral α- and β-carbons and is degraded to both succinyl-CoA (glucogenic) and acetyl-CoA (ketogenic). Threonine also has two chiral centers but is not branched-chain; leucine is purely ketogenic and valine purely glucogenic.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which essential amino acid contains sulfur, serves as the universal start amino acid in translation, and is the precursor of the main methyl donor?',choices:['Cysteine','Methionine','Threonine','Serine'],ans:1,exp:'**Methionine** has a thioether (-CH2-CH2-S-CH3) side chain, is encoded by the start codon AUG, and is converted to S-adenosylmethionine (SAM), the universal methyl donor. Cysteine also contains sulfur but is nonessential and is not the start residue.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which amino acid has a rigid cyclic pyrrolidine ring that disrupts α-helices and is frequently found at β-turns?',choices:['Glycine','Histidine','Proline','Tryptophan'],ans:2,exp:'**Proline**\'s side chain loops back to bond its own backbone nitrogen, fixing the phi angle and removing the N-H needed for helix hydrogen bonding, so it is a helix breaker. Glycine also disrupts helices, but through excess flexibility rather than a rigid ring.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which aromatic amino acid is the precursor to tyrosine and accumulates in phenylketonuria (PKU)?',choices:['Tyrosine','Tryptophan','Phenylalanine','Histidine'],ans:2,exp:'**Phenylalanine** is hydroxylated to tyrosine by phenylalanine hydroxylase (BH4 cofactor). In PKU this enzyme is deficient, so phenylalanine and phenylketones build up and tyrosine becomes conditionally essential.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which amino acid contains an indole ring system and is the precursor to serotonin and melatonin?',choices:['Tyrosine','Phenylalanine','Histidine','Tryptophan'],ans:3,exp:'**Tryptophan** has a bicyclic indole side chain and is the precursor of serotonin, melatonin and niacin (vitamin B3). Tyrosine is the precursor of catecholamines instead, and histidine has an imidazole ring.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',idStem:"Which polar amino acid has a hydroxymethyl side chain, is the most commonly phosphorylated residue, and is the nucleophile in serine proteases?", q:"Which polar amino acid has a hydroxymethyl side chain and is the most commonly phosphorylated residue?",choices:['Threonine','Serine','Tyrosine','Cysteine'],ans:1,exp:'**Serine** has a -CH2OH side chain that kinases phosphorylate and that acts as the nucleophile in the Ser-His-Asp catalytic triad of proteases such as chymotrypsin. Threonine and tyrosine can also be phosphorylated, but threonine\'s side chain is -CH(OH)CH3 and tyrosine\'s is a phenol.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which essential polar amino acid has both a hydroxyl group and a methyl group on its β-carbon, giving it two chiral centers?',choices:['Serine','Valine','Threonine','Isoleucine'],ans:2,exp:'**Threonine** has a -CH(OH)-CH3 side chain, so its β-carbon is a second stereocenter. Isoleucine also has two chiral centers but its side chain is nonpolar, and serine has a hydroxyl but no extra methyl.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which amino acid is made from phenylalanine and is the precursor to catecholamines, thyroid hormones and melanin?',choices:['Phenylalanine','Tryptophan','Tyrosine','Histidine'],ans:2,exp:'**Tyrosine** is formed by hydroxylation of phenylalanine and feeds the pathway to dopamine, norepinephrine and epinephrine, as well as T3/T4 and melanin. Tryptophan instead gives serotonin and melatonin.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which amino acid contains a thiol (-SH) side chain, can form disulfide bonds, and has (R) configuration even though it is an L-amino acid?',choices:['Methionine','Serine','Cysteine','Threonine'],ans:2,exp:'**Cysteine**\'s thiol can be oxidized to a disulfide (cystine). Because sulfur outranks the carboxyl oxygen in Cahn-Ingold-Prelog priority, its L α-carbon is (R), whereas all other chiral L-amino acids are (S). Methionine contains sulfur as a thioether and cannot form disulfides.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which amino acid is the amide derivative of aspartate, is polar and uncharged, and is the attachment site for N-linked glycosylation?',choices:['Glutamine','Aspartate','Asparagine','Serine'],ans:2,exp:'**Asparagine** (-CH2-CONH2) carries N-linked oligosaccharides on its amide nitrogen, in the sequence Asn-X-Ser/Thr. Serine and threonine carry O-linked sugars instead, and glutamine is the amide of glutamate.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which amino acid is the most abundant free amino acid in blood, carries nitrogen to the liver for the urea cycle, and is the amide of glutamate?',choices:['Asparagine','Glutamate','Aspartate','Glutamine'],ans:3,exp:'**Glutamine** (-CH2-CH2-CONH2) is the main nontoxic carrier of ammonia between tissues; glutaminase releases its amide nitrogen for urea synthesis and it also donates nitrogen to purine and pyrimidine synthesis. Asparagine is the amide of aspartate, not glutamate.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which positively charged amino acid has an ε-amino group (pKa about 10.5), is commonly acetylated on histones, and is purely ketogenic?',choices:['Arginine','Histidine','Lysine','Ornithine'],ans:2,exp:'**Lysine** has a -(CH2)4-NH3+ side chain; acetylation of this ε-amine on histones neutralizes its charge and loosens chromatin to favor transcription. Lysine and leucine are the only purely ketogenic standard amino acids.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which amino acid has the side chain with the highest pKa (about 12.5), contains a guanidinium group, and is conditionally essential?',choices:['Lysine','Histidine','Arginine','Ornithine'],ans:2,exp:'**Arginine**\'s guanidinium group is resonance-stabilized when protonated, so its pKa is about 12.5 and it stays positively charged at physiological pH. It is a urea cycle intermediate and is conditionally essential; lysine is basic too, but less so (pKa about 10.5).'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which amino acid has an imidazole side chain with a pKa near 6, can donate or accept protons at physiological pH, and is found in enzyme catalytic triads?',choices:['Lysine','Arginine','Tryptophan','Histidine'],ans:3,exp:'**Histidine**\'s imidazole has a pKa near 6, close enough to physiological pH that it readily shuttles protons, making it ideal for acid-base catalysis (e.g., the Ser-His-Asp triad) and for buffering in hemoglobin. Lysine and arginine have much higher pKa values and remain protonated.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which amino acid donates the second nitrogen of urea by condensing with citrulline to form argininosuccinate?',choices:['Glutamate','Aspartate','Glutamine','Ornithine'],ans:1,exp:'**Aspartate** condenses with citrulline (argininosuccinate synthetase); argininosuccinate is then cleaved to arginine and fumarate, linking the urea and citric acid cycles. Ornithine is the carrier that condenses with carbamoyl phosphate to make citrulline, and glutamate supplies the first nitrogen as free ammonia.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which negatively charged amino acid is the central hub of amino acid metabolism, is the major excitatory CNS neurotransmitter, and is the precursor to GABA?',choices:['Aspartate','Glutamine','Asparagine','Glutamate'],ans:3,exp:'**Glutamate** collects amino groups from transamination (α-ketoglutarate → glutamate) and releases them as ammonia via glutamate dehydrogenase. Decarboxylation of glutamate makes GABA, the main inhibitory neurotransmitter; glutamine is its uncharged amide.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which functional group is the aromatic side chain of tryptophan?',choices:['Imidazole','Phenol','Indole','Guanidinium'],ans:2,exp:'**Indole** is a benzene ring fused to a pyrrole ring; it gives tryptophan its strong absorbance at 280 nm and its intrinsic fluorescence. Imidazole belongs to histidine and phenol to tyrosine.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which functional group is the side chain of histidine and has a pKa of approximately 6?',choices:['Guanidinium','Indole','Phenol','Imidazole'],ans:3,exp:'**Imidazole** is a five-membered aromatic ring with two nitrogens; with a pKa near 6 it can act as either an acid or a base near physiological pH. Guanidinium (arginine) is far more basic, with a pKa near 12.5.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Which functional group is the highly basic side chain of arginine with a pKa of approximately 12.5?',choices:['Imidazole','Indole','Guanidinium','Amide'],ans:2,exp:'The **guanidinium** group (-NH-C(=NH2+)-NH2) spreads its positive charge over three nitrogens by resonance, making arginine the most basic standard side chain. Imidazole (histidine) has a pKa near 6, and amides (asparagine, glutamine) are not basic.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Amino acids are organic compounds that contain both _____ and _____ groups on the same α-carbon',choices:['amino / carboxyl','amide / carboxyl','amino / hydroxyl','hydroxyl / carbonyl'],ans:0,exp:'Every amino acid has an α-carbon bonded to a basic **amino** group (-NH2), an acidic **carboxyl** group (-COOH), a hydrogen and a variable R group. An amide is the product of condensing an amine with a carboxyl, as in the peptide bond.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'_____ is the only standard amino acid that is not chiral',choices:['Alanine','Proline','Serine','Glycine'],ans:3,exp:'**Glycine**\'s R group is a hydrogen, so its α-carbon has two identical substituents and no stereocenter. Proline is unusual because its side chain is cyclic, but it is still chiral.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'In the D/L system, the α-carbon of every chiral amino acid used in eukaryotic proteins has _____ configuration',choices:['D','R','L','S'],ans:2,exp:'All proteinogenic chiral amino acids in eukaryotes are **L**, a relative configuration defined by comparison with L-glyceraldehyde. In the R/S system most are (S), but cysteine is (R), so (S) is not universal; D-amino acids appear mainly in bacterial cell walls.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'In Cahn-Ingold-Prelog terms, every chiral standard amino acid except cysteine has _____ configuration at the α-carbon',choices:['(R)','racemic','meso','(S)'],ans:3,exp:'L-amino acids are **(S)** at the α-carbon because the -NH2 > -COOH > R group priority order runs counterclockwise with H pointing back. Cysteine is the exception, being (R), because its sulfur-containing side chain outranks the carboxyl group.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'L and D indicate _____ configuration, whereas R and S indicate _____ configuration',choices:['absolute / relative','relative / absolute','relative / relative','absolute / absolute'],ans:1,exp:'D/L is a **relative** configuration assigned by comparison with glyceraldehyde, whereas R/S is an **absolute** configuration assigned from Cahn-Ingold-Prelog priorities. The two systems do not map onto each other: L-alanine is (S) but L-cysteine is (R).'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Amphoteric molecules such as amino acids and water can act as either _____ or _____, depending on the pH',choices:['acids / bases','oxidants / reductants','solutes / solvents','hydrophiles / hydrophobes'],ans:0,exp:'**Amphoteric** species can donate a proton (act as an acid) or accept one (act as a base): an amino acid\'s -COOH and -NH3+ groups both titrate. Do not confuse this with amphipathic, which describes molecules with both hydrophilic and hydrophobic regions.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'The pH at which half of an ionizable group is deprotonated is called the _____',choices:['pI','pKb','pKa','equivalence point'],ans:2,exp:'When [A-] = [HA], the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation gives pH = **pKa** + log(1) = pKa. The pI is instead the pH at which the whole molecule has no net charge.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'At a very low pH, amino acids are _____',choices:['fully protonated','fully deprotonated','zwitterionic','negatively charged'],ans:0,exp:'When pH is below every pKa, all ionizable groups carry their proton: -COOH (neutral) and -NH3+ (positive), so the amino acid is **fully protonated** with a net positive charge. At high pH it is fully deprotonated and negative; the zwitterion dominates between the two pKa values.'},
  {s:'General',q:'_____ is the pH at which an amino acid carries no net charge and exists mainly as the zwitterion',choices:['Carboxyl pKa','Isoelectric point','Amino pKa','Physiological pH'],ans:1,exp:'At the **isoelectric point (pI)** positive and negative charges balance; for a neutral amino acid pI = (pKa1 + pKa2)/2. At either pKa, half of that group is ionized, so the net charge is +1/2 or -1/2 rather than zero.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'_____ carry both a positive and a negative charge that cancel, so they have no net charge',choices:['Anions','Cations','Free radicals','Zwitterions'],ans:3,exp:'A **zwitterion** (e.g., H3N+-CHR-COO-) has separated formal charges that sum to zero; amino acids exist this way at physiological pH. An anion has a net negative charge and a cation a net positive one.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'At the half-equivalence point (midpoint of a buffering region) in a titration, pH = _____',choices:['pKb','pH 7','pI','pKa'],ans:3,exp:'At the half-equivalence point, [HA] = [A-], so by Henderson-Hasselbalch pH = **pKa**; the curve is flattest here because buffering is maximal. The pI lies at an equivalence point between two pKa values, not at a midpoint.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'For an amino acid with a neutral side chain titrated from low pH, the pH at the first equivalence point equals the _____',choices:['pKa1','pKa2','pI','pKb'],ans:2,exp:'For a neutral amino acid, the first equivalence point lies halfway between pKa1 (carboxyl) and pKa2 (amino), so pH = (pKa1 + pKa2)/2 = **pI**, where the zwitterion dominates. The pKa values themselves occur at the half-equivalence points.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'The _____ structure of proteins is the linear sequence of amino acids in a peptide',choices:['3°','4°','2°','1°'],ans:3,exp:'**Primary (1°)** structure is the amino acid sequence, held together by covalent peptide bonds; it ultimately determines all higher levels of folding. Secondary structure refers to local backbone hydrogen-bonded shapes such as α-helices.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'The _____ structure of proteins is the local folding of neighboring amino acids, including α-helices and β-pleated sheets',choices:['2°','1°','4°','3°'],ans:0,exp:'**Secondary (2°)** structure arises from hydrogen bonds between backbone C=O and N-H groups of nearby residues, not from side-chain interactions. Tertiary structure, by contrast, depends on R-group interactions across the whole chain.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'The _____ structure of proteins is the 3-D shape of a single polypeptide chain',choices:['4°','3°','2°','1°'],ans:1,exp:'**Tertiary (3°)** structure is the overall fold of one chain, stabilized by the hydrophobic effect, hydrogen bonds, salt bridges and disulfide bonds between R groups. Quaternary structure only exists when two or more chains associate.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'The _____ structure of proteins is the interaction between polypeptide chains in proteins that contain multiple subunits',choices:['1°','3°','2°','4°'],ans:3,exp:'**Quaternary (4°)** structure describes how separate subunits assemble, as in hemoglobin\'s two α and two β chains. Single-chain proteins such as myoglobin have no quaternary structure.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'_____ are right-handed (clockwise) coils around a central axis and are a common _____ protein structure',choices:['β-pleated sheets / 2°','α-helices / 3°','random coils / 3°','α-helices / 2°'],ans:3,exp:'**α-Helices** are stabilized by backbone hydrogen bonds between the C=O of residue i and the N-H of residue i+4, with side chains pointing outward. Because they arise from local backbone interactions, they are **secondary (2°)** structure, not tertiary.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'_____ are rippled strands that can be parallel or antiparallel and are a common _____ protein structure',choices:['β-pleated sheets / 2°','α-helices / 2°','β-pleated sheets / 3°','β-turns / 3°'],ans:0,exp:'**β-Pleated sheets** form when extended strands lie side by side and hydrogen bond through their backbones, giving a rippled shape. Like α-helices, they are **secondary (2°)** structure; antiparallel sheets have more linear, slightly stronger hydrogen bonds.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'_____ has a rigid cyclic structure and can interrupt 2° protein structure',choices:['Proline','Glycine','Alanine','Valine'],ans:0,exp:'**Proline**\'s ring locks its backbone and lacks an amide N-H to donate a hydrogen bond, so it kinks α-helices and often sits at β-turns. Glycine also breaks helices, but because it is too flexible, not rigid.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'_____ is when a protein (or nucleic acid) loses its quaternary, tertiary and secondary structure because its noncovalent interactions are disrupted',choices:['Denaturation','Proteolysis','Glycosylation','Renaturation'],ans:0,exp:'**Denaturation** by heat, extreme pH, urea or detergents unfolds the molecule but leaves the primary sequence intact, so function is lost while peptide bonds remain. Proteolysis, in contrast, cleaves covalent peptide bonds.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'The _____ pushes hydrophobic R groups to the interior of a protein, which _____ the entropy of the surrounding water molecules',choices:['hydrophobic effect / decreases','hydrogen bonding / increases','hydrophobic effect / increases','disulfide bonding / decreases'],ans:2,exp:'Water forms ordered solvation cages around exposed nonpolar groups; burying those groups through the **hydrophobic effect** releases that water and **increases** its entropy (ΔS > 0), making folding favorable. The drive is entropic, not new hydrogen or covalent bonds in the protein.'},
  {s:'General',q:'_____ form when the thiol groups of two cysteine residues are oxidized, creating a covalent bond between them',choices:['Peptide bonds','Ionic bonds','Hydrogen bonds','Disulfide bonds'],ans:3,exp:'Oxidation of two -SH groups forms a covalent S-S **disulfide bond** (cystine), which stabilizes tertiary and quaternary structure, especially in extracellular proteins. Reducing agents such as β-mercaptoethanol break them; peptide bonds instead join the amino and carboxyl groups of the backbone.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'_____ are proteins that contain a non-amino-acid component, such as the heme in hemoglobin',choices:['Fibrous proteins','Simple proteins','Conjugated proteins','Zymogens'],ans:2,exp:'**Conjugated proteins** consist of a polypeptide plus a prosthetic group, such as heme (hemoproteins), lipids (lipoproteins) or carbohydrates (glycoproteins). Simple proteins are made only of amino acids, and zymogens are inactive enzyme precursors.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'_____ are the tightly bound non-protein parts of conjugated proteins and can be metal ions, vitamin derivatives, lipids, carbohydrates or nucleic acids',choices:['Coenzymes','Prosthetic groups','Apoproteins','Zymogens'],ans:1,exp:'A **prosthetic group** is tightly, often covalently, attached and stays with the protein, like heme in hemoglobin or FAD in succinate dehydrogenase. A coenzyme is an organic helper that is usually loosely bound and can dissociate, and the apoprotein is the protein part alone.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'Peptide bonds link the _____ of one amino acid to the _____ of the next amino acid',choices:['α-amino group / α-amino group','α-carboxyl group / R group','α-carboxyl group / α-amino group','R group / α-amino group'],ans:2,exp:'A peptide (amide) bond forms by condensation between the **α-carboxyl group** of one residue and the **α-amino group** of the next, releasing water. This is why every chain has a free amino N-terminus and a free carboxyl C-terminus.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'_____ are biological catalysts that are unchanged by the reactions they catalyze and can be reused',choices:['Zymogens','Cofactors','Coenzymes','Enzymes'],ans:3,exp:'**Enzymes** lower activation energy without being consumed and without changing ΔG or the equilibrium constant. Coenzymes such as NAD+ are often chemically altered during the reaction and must be regenerated, and zymogens are inactive precursors.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'Exergonic reactions _____ free energy',choices:['require','absorb','release','store'],ans:2,exp:'Exergonic reactions have ΔG < 0, so they **release** free energy and are spontaneous, though not necessarily fast. Enzymes speed them up without changing ΔG; endergonic reactions are the opposite.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'Endergonic reactions _____ an input of free energy',choices:['release','produce','require','dissipate'],ans:2,exp:'Endergonic reactions have ΔG > 0, so they **require** energy and are nonspontaneous on their own. Cells drive them by coupling to exergonic reactions such as ATP hydrolysis.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'_____ catalyze redox reactions that involve the transfer of electrons',choices:['Oxidoreductases','Transferases','Hydrolases','Lyases'],ans:0,exp:'**Oxidoreductases** (dehydrogenases, oxidases, reductases) move electrons, often using NAD+ or FAD as carriers. Transferases move whole functional groups, such as phosphate or amino groups, instead.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'_____ move a functional group from one molecule to another',choices:['Hydrolases','Transferases','Lyases','Oxidoreductases'],ans:1,exp:'**Transferases** relocate groups such as phosphates (kinases), amino groups (aminotransferases) or methyl groups. Hydrolases also break bonds, but they use water to split a molecule rather than transfer a group to an acceptor.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'_____ catalyze cleavage of a bond with the addition of H₂O',choices:['Hydrolases','Isomerases','Lyases','Transferases'],ans:0,exp:'**Hydrolases**, such as proteases, lipases, phosphatases and nucleases, split bonds by adding water across them. Lyases also cleave molecules, but without water or redox, often forming a double bond or ring.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'_____ catalyze cleavage without the addition of H₂O and without the transfer of electrons',choices:['Lyases','Hydrolases','Isomerases','Ligases'],ans:0,exp:'**Lyases** break a single molecule into two, often forming a new double bond or ring (e.g., aldolase); run in reverse they act as synthases. Ligases also join molecules, but they require ATP to do so.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'_____ catalyze the interconversion of isomers',choices:['Lyases','Transferases','Isomerases','Ligases'],ans:2,exp:'**Isomerases** rearrange bonds within one molecule, interconverting constitutional isomers or stereoisomers (e.g., triose phosphate isomerase, racemases). Mutases are isomerases that shift a group to a new position in the same molecule.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'_____ join two large biomolecules, often of the same type, usually using ATP',choices:['Isomerases','Lyases','Ligases','Hydrolases'],ans:2,exp:'**Ligases** form new bonds between two molecules using energy from ATP hydrolysis, such as DNA ligase sealing Okazaki fragments. Lyases can also add groups, but they do so without ATP.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'Enzymes that hydrolyze the ester bonds of triacylglycerols (fats) are called _____',choices:['lipases','proteases','amylases','phosphatases'],ans:0,exp:'**Lipases** (e.g., pancreatic lipase, lipoprotein lipase) are hydrolases that cleave the ester bonds between glycerol and fatty acids. Proteases hydrolyze peptide bonds, and amylases hydrolyze the glycosidic bonds of starch.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'Enzymes that transfer a phosphate group from ATP (or another nucleoside triphosphate) to a substrate are called _____',choices:['phosphatases','kinases','phosphorylases','isomerases'],ans:1,exp:'**Kinases** (e.g., hexokinase, protein kinase A) move the terminal (γ) phosphate of ATP onto a substrate. Phosphorylases also add phosphate, but they use inorganic phosphate (Pi) rather than ATP.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'Enzymes that remove a phosphate group from a substrate by hydrolysis are called _____',choices:['kinases','phosphorylases','phosphatases','lipases'],ans:2,exp:'**Phosphatases** use water to cleave a phosphate ester and release Pi (e.g., glucose-6-phosphatase, and the protein phosphatases that reverse kinase signaling). Kinases do the opposite, adding phosphate from ATP.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'Enzymes that cleave a bond by adding inorganic phosphate (Pi) across it, as when glycogen is broken down to glucose-1-phosphate, are called _____',choices:['phosphatases','phosphorylases','transferases','kinases'],ans:1,exp:'**Phosphorylases** carry out phosphorolysis: Pi, not ATP, is the phosphate source, so glycogen phosphorylase releases glucose-1-phosphate without spending ATP. Kinases phosphorylate using ATP, and phosphatases remove phosphate by hydrolysis.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Kinetics',q:'A plot of initial velocity versus substrate concentration for an enzyme that obeys Michaelis-Menten kinetics has a _____ shape',choices:['exponential','sigmoidal','linear','hyperbolic'],ans:3,exp:'Michaelis-Menten enzymes give a **hyperbolic** curve, v = Vmax[S]/(Km + [S]): velocity rises almost linearly at low [S] and levels off toward Vmax as active sites saturate. A sigmoidal curve instead signals cooperative (allosteric) binding.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Kinetics',q:'The substrate concentration at which an enzyme reaches half of its maximum velocity is the _____',choices:['Keq','Vmax','Km','kcat'],ans:2,exp:'**Km** is the [S] at which v = Vmax/2; a lower Km means a higher apparent affinity for substrate. kcat is the turnover number (substrate molecules converted per active site per second), not a concentration.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Kinetics',q:'The reaction velocity reached when all of an enzyme\'s active sites are saturated with substrate is the _____',choices:['Vmax','Km','kcat','Keq'],ans:0,exp:'**Vmax** is the plateau of the Michaelis-Menten curve and equals kcat × [E]total, so it can be raised only by adding more enzyme. kcat is the per-active-site turnover number, and Km is the [S] at half of Vmax.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Kinetics',q:'Multisubunit enzymes that bind substrate cooperatively display a _____ plot of velocity versus substrate concentration',choices:['exponential','hyperbolic','linear','sigmoidal'],ans:3,exp:'Cooperativity gives a **sigmoidal** (S-shaped) curve: binding at one subunit shifts the others toward a higher-affinity state, so activity rises steeply over a narrow [S] range, as in hemoglobin binding O₂. Non-cooperative Michaelis-Menten enzymes give a hyperbolic curve.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Kinetics',q:'When binding of one ligand to a subunit of a multisubunit protein changes the affinity of the other subunits for that same ligand, the protein shows _____',choices:['feedback inhibition','cooperative binding','heterotropic regulation','competitive inhibition'],ans:1,exp:'In **cooperative binding**, each bound ligand changes subunit conformation and alters affinity for the next one (positive cooperativity in hemoglobin; Hill coefficient > 1). Heterotropic regulation involves a different molecule, such as 2,3-BPG, rather than the ligand itself.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'The region of an enzyme where substrate binds and the chemical reaction is catalyzed is the _____',choices:['allosteric site','active site','regulatory subunit','signal sequence'],ans:1,exp:'The **active site** is a pocket whose side chains and cofactors position the substrate and stabilize the transition state. Allosteric sites are separate regulatory sites where effectors bind to change the enzyme\'s conformation.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'The model proposing that an enzyme\'s active site is rigid and already exactly complementary in shape to its substrate is the _____',choices:['allosteric model','lock and key theory','collision theory','induced fit theory'],ans:1,exp:'The **lock and key theory** (Emil Fischer) treats the active site as a fixed shape that the substrate simply slots into. It has largely been replaced by the induced fit theory, in which binding itself reshapes the active site.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'The model proposing that substrate binding causes conformational changes in the enzyme that optimize their interaction is the _____',choices:['transition state theory','lock and key theory','allosteric model','induced fit theory'],ans:3,exp:'The **induced fit theory** (Koshland) holds that the active site is flexible and molds around the substrate on binding, which helps stabilize the transition state; hexokinase closing around glucose is a classic example. The lock and key theory assumes a rigid, pre-formed fit.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'Metal ions such as Mg²⁺ or Zn²⁺ that some enzymes require for activity are classified as _____',choices:['coenzymes','zymogens','cofactors','apoenzymes'],ans:2,exp:'**Cofactors** are non-protein helpers, and the inorganic ones are metal ions (e.g., Mg²⁺ for kinases, Zn²⁺ for carbonic anhydrase). Coenzymes are the organic, often vitamin-derived subset of helpers (e.g., NAD⁺, FAD, CoA).'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'Small organic molecules, often derived from vitamins, that some enzymes require for activity are classified as _____',choices:['zymogens','coenzymes','apoenzymes','holoenzymes'],ans:1,exp:'**Coenzymes** are organic cofactors such as NAD⁺ (from niacin), FAD (from riboflavin) and coenzyme A (from pantothenic acid). An apoenzyme is an enzyme lacking its cofactor, a holoenzyme is the complete active enzyme, and a zymogen is an inactive precursor.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'_____ occurs when the end product of a metabolic pathway inhibits an enzyme that acts earlier in the same pathway',choices:['Feedforward activation','Competitive inhibition','Allosteric activation','Feedback inhibition'],ans:3,exp:'**Feedback inhibition** (negative feedback) prevents overproduction; for example, CTP inhibits aspartate transcarbamoylase, the first committed step of pyrimidine synthesis. It is usually allosteric, so the end product need not resemble the substrate of the enzyme it inhibits.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'An inhibitor that binds the active site of free enzyme and thus prevents substrate from binding is a _____',choices:['mixed inhibitor','noncompetitive inhibitor','uncompetitive inhibitor','competitive inhibitor'],ans:3,exp:'A **competitive inhibitor** resembles the substrate and occupies the active site, so raising [S] overcomes it: Km rises while Vmax is unchanged. Noncompetitive and uncompetitive inhibitors bind elsewhere and cannot be outcompeted by substrate.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'An inhibitor that binds only to the enzyme-substrate complex, not to free enzyme, is a _____',choices:['competitive inhibitor','mixed inhibitor','noncompetitive inhibitor','uncompetitive inhibitor'],ans:3,exp:'An **uncompetitive inhibitor** binds a site that forms only after substrate binds, locking substrate in place; it lowers both Vmax and Km. A mixed inhibitor can bind either free enzyme or the enzyme-substrate complex, with different affinities.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'An inhibitor that binds an allosteric site with equal affinity for free enzyme and the enzyme-substrate complex is a _____',choices:['noncompetitive inhibitor','mixed inhibitor','uncompetitive inhibitor','competitive inhibitor'],ans:0,exp:'A **noncompetitive inhibitor** binds away from the active site and binds E and ES equally well, so it lowers Vmax without changing Km. If it preferred one form over the other, it would be a mixed inhibitor.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'In competitive inhibition, Vmax _____ and Km _____',choices:['is unchanged / increases','is unchanged / decreases','decreases / is unchanged','decreases / decreases'],ans:0,exp:'A competitive inhibitor competes for the active site, so more substrate is needed to reach half-maximal velocity and apparent **Km increases**. At saturating [S], substrate outcompetes the inhibitor, so **Vmax is unchanged**.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'In uncompetitive inhibition, Vmax _____ and Km _____',choices:['decreases / is unchanged','is unchanged / increases','decreases / decreases','increases / decreases'],ans:2,exp:'An uncompetitive inhibitor binds only the ES complex, so it **lowers Vmax** and also **lowers apparent Km**, because removing ES pulls E + S toward ES and raises apparent affinity. Both fall by the same factor, giving parallel lines on a Lineweaver-Burk plot.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'In noncompetitive inhibition, Vmax _____ and Km _____',choices:['decreases / decreases','is unchanged / increases','increases / is unchanged','decreases / is unchanged'],ans:3,exp:'A noncompetitive inhibitor binds an allosteric site with equal affinity for E and ES. It removes functional enzyme, so **Vmax decreases**, but it does not change substrate affinity, so **Km is unchanged**.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'Lineweaver-Burk plot: x-intercept = _____; y-intercept = _____; slope = _____',choices:['-1/Km / 1/Vmax / Km/Vmax','1/Km / -1/Vmax / Vmax/Km','-1/Vmax / 1/Km / Km/Vmax','-1/Km / 1/Vmax / Vmax/Km'],ans:0,exp:'Taking the reciprocal of the Michaelis-Menten equation gives 1/v = (Km/Vmax)(1/[S]) + 1/Vmax, a straight line with slope **Km/Vmax**, y-intercept **1/Vmax** and x-intercept **-1/Km**. A common error is inverting the slope to Vmax/Km.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'On a Lineweaver-Burk plot, an inhibitor that leaves the y-intercept unchanged but makes the slope steeper (moving the x-intercept toward the origin) is a _____',choices:['noncompetitive inhibitor','uncompetitive inhibitor','mixed inhibitor','competitive inhibitor'],ans:3,exp:'An unchanged y-intercept (1/Vmax) means Vmax is unchanged, and an x-intercept (-1/Km) moving toward zero means apparent Km increased: the signature of a **competitive inhibitor**. A noncompetitive inhibitor does the reverse, raising the y-intercept while the lines meet on the x-axis.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'A Lineweaver-Burk (double-reciprocal) plot graphs 1/v against 1/[S], so its x-intercept is _____ and its y-intercept is _____',choices:['1/Km / -1/Vmax','-1/Km / 1/Vmax','-1/Vmax / 1/Km','1/Vmax / -1/Km'],ans:1,exp:'Because both axes are reciprocals, both intercepts are too: the x-intercept is **-1/Km** and the y-intercept is **1/Vmax**. Remember that Vmax sits on the velocity (y) axis and Km on the substrate (x) axis.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'An irreversible inhibitor typically _____ the enzyme, often at its active site, permanently eliminating its activity',choices:['noncovalently binds','covalently modifies','allosterically binds','competitively binds'],ans:1,exp:'Irreversible inhibitors **covalently modify** the enzyme, usually an active-site residue (e.g., aspirin acetylates a serine in COX; organophosphates modify acetylcholinesterase), so activity returns only when new enzyme is made. Noncovalent binding, whether competitive or allosteric, is reversible.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'_____ is irreversible inhibition in which an enzyme converts a substrate analog into a reactive intermediate that covalently inactivates the enzyme itself',choices:['Competitive inhibition','Allosteric inhibition','Suicide inhibition','Feedback inhibition'],ans:2,exp:'In **suicide (mechanism-based) inhibition**, the enzyme\'s own catalytic mechanism activates the inhibitor, which makes the inactivation highly specific; examples include 5-fluorouracil (as FdUMP) on thymidylate synthase and clavulanic acid on β-lactamase. Competitive inhibition, by contrast, is reversible and overcome by excess substrate.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'Molecules that bind a site other than the active site and shift the enzyme into a conformation that cannot bind substrate are _____',choices:['allosteric inhibitors','competitive inhibitors','uncompetitive inhibitors','allosteric activators'],ans:0,exp:'**Allosteric inhibitors** stabilize a low-affinity (T) conformation of the enzyme from a separate regulatory site. Uncompetitive inhibitors also bind away from the active site, but only after substrate is already bound.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'An allosteric regulator that is the same molecule as the protein\'s own ligand, such as O₂ for hemoglobin, is a _____',choices:['heterotropic effector','homotropic effector','competitive inhibitor','feedback inhibitor'],ans:1,exp:'A **homotropic effector** regulates its own binding: O₂ binding at one heme raises the affinity of the remaining hemes, producing cooperativity. Heterotropic effectors are different molecules, such as 2,3-BPG, H⁺ and CO₂ (homo = same, hetero = different).'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'An allosteric regulator that is a different molecule from the substrate, such as 2,3-BPG acting on hemoglobin, is a _____',choices:['competitive inhibitor','homotropic effector','heterotropic effector','coenzyme'],ans:2,exp:'A **heterotropic effector** is a molecule other than the ligand that binds an allosteric site; 2,3-BPG, H⁺ and CO₂ all lower hemoglobin\'s O₂ affinity (right shift). A homotropic effector is the ligand itself, as with O₂ on hemoglobin.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'_____ is the addition of a phosphoryl group (–PO₃²⁻) to an organic molecule, a common way to switch enzyme activity on or off',choices:['Glycosylation','Ubiquitination','Phosphorylation','Acetylation'],ans:2,exp:'**Phosphorylation**, catalyzed by kinases and reversed by phosphatases, typically targets serine, threonine or tyrosine residues and changes protein conformation and activity. Ubiquitination instead tags proteins for degradation by the proteasome.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'_____ is the covalent addition of a carbohydrate group to a protein or lipid',choices:['Methylation','Phosphorylation','Ubiquitination','Glycosylation'],ans:3,exp:'**Glycosylation** occurs mainly in the ER (N-linked, on asparagine) and Golgi (O-linked, on serine or threonine) and affects protein folding, sorting and cell recognition. Methylation adds a –CH₃ group, as in DNA and histone regulation.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'_____ are inactive enzyme precursors that must be cleaved to become active, such as trypsinogen and pepsinogen',choices:['Zymogens','Apoenzymes','Coenzymes','Isozymes'],ans:0,exp:'**Zymogens** (proenzymes, often named with the suffix -ogen) let cells store dangerous enzymes such as digestive proteases safely until cleavage activates them. An apoenzyme is also inactive, but because it lacks its cofactor, not because it needs cleavage.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'_____ make up the cytoskeleton, anchoring proteins and much of the extracellular matrix; examples include actin, tubulin, keratin and collagen',choices:['Motor proteins','Structural proteins','Binding proteins','Receptor proteins'],ans:1,exp:'**Structural proteins** are typically fibrous and provide shape and support (collagen gives tensile strength, elastin gives stretch, keratin forms hair and nails). Motor proteins such as myosin and kinesin interact with structural filaments but generate movement rather than form them.'},
  {s:'General',q:'_____ convert chemical energy into mechanical work by hydrolyzing ATP',choices:['Structural proteins','Motor proteins','Binding proteins','Cell adhesion molecules'],ans:1,exp:'**Motor proteins** such as myosin, kinesin and dynein use ATP hydrolysis to change conformation, generating force along actin filaments (myosin) or microtubules (kinesin toward the plus end, dynein toward the minus end). Structural proteins form those tracks but do not move along them.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'Nonenzymatic proteins such as hemoglobin, calmodulin and transcription factors, whose job is to carry or sequester a specific molecule or ion, are classified as _____',choices:['motor proteins','binding proteins','structural proteins','cell adhesion molecules'],ans:1,exp:'**Binding proteins** hold a specific target with a characteristic affinity, e.g., hemoglobin for O₂ and calmodulin for Ca²⁺. Cell adhesion molecules also bind, but specifically to other cells or to the extracellular matrix.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'_____ are membrane proteins that allow cells to attach to other cells or to the extracellular matrix',choices:['Cytoskeletal proteins','Receptor tyrosine kinases','Cell adhesion molecules','Motor proteins'],ans:2,exp:'**Cell adhesion molecules (CAMs)** are integral membrane proteins in three main classes: cadherins (cell–cell, Ca²⁺-dependent), integrins (cell–matrix) and selectins (bind carbohydrates, important for leukocytes). Receptor tyrosine kinases also span the membrane, but they transduce signals rather than anchor cells.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'_____ are calcium-dependent glycoproteins that hold cells of the same type together',choices:['Selectins','Cadherins','Claudins','Integrins'],ans:1,exp:'**Cadherins** (C for calcium) form adherens junctions and desmosomes between like cells; loss of E-cadherin in epithelia promotes cancer metastasis. Integrins bind the extracellular matrix rather than neighboring cells, and claudins seal tight junctions without requiring Ca²⁺.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'_____ have two membrane-spanning chains (α and β) and let cells adhere to extracellular matrix proteins such as fibronectin',choices:['Integrins','Claudins','Cadherins','Selectins'],ans:0,exp:'**Integrins** are α/β heterodimers that link the extracellular matrix to the actin cytoskeleton and relay signals affecting migration, division and survival; platelet integrins binding fibrinogen during clotting are a classic example. Cadherins, by contrast, bind neighboring cells of the same type.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'_____ bind carbohydrates on the surfaces of other cells and are best known for mediating leukocyte rolling along blood vessel walls',choices:['Integrins','Claudins','Cadherins','Selectins'],ans:3,exp:'**Selectins** (on endothelium, platelets and leukocytes) weakly bind specific carbohydrate groups, slowing white blood cells so they roll along the vessel wall during inflammation. Integrins then mediate firm adhesion and migration out of the vessel.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'_____ are Y-shaped proteins made of two heavy and two light chains, secreted by plasma cells, that bind a specific antigen on a pathogen or toxin',choices:['T cell receptors','Complement proteins','MHC molecules','Antibodies'],ans:3,exp:'**Antibodies** (immunoglobulins) bind antigen with their variable regions and can neutralize the target, opsonize it for phagocytosis or activate complement. T cell receptors also recognize specific antigens, but they stay membrane-bound and recognize only peptides presented on MHC.'},
  {s:'Biosignaling',q:'_____ are transmembrane proteins that form pores through which ions diffuse down their electrochemical gradients',choices:['G protein-coupled receptors','Enzyme-linked receptors','Ion pumps','Ion channels'],ans:3,exp:'**Ion channels** provide a hydrophilic pore, so ion movement through them is passive (facilitated diffusion); they can be ungated, voltage-gated or ligand-gated. Ion pumps such as the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase instead use ATP to move ions against their gradients.'},
  {s:'Biosignaling',q:'Ungated (leak) ion channels are _____ open',choices:['always','never','only during depolarization','only when ligand-bound'],ans:0,exp:'Ungated channels have no gate, so they are **always** open; K⁺ leak channels are a major contributor to the negative resting membrane potential. Voltage-gated and ligand-gated channels open only in response to a stimulus.'},
  {s:'Biosignaling',q:'_____ open only within a particular range of membrane potentials',choices:['Ungated channels','Voltage-gated channels','Gap junctions','Ligand-gated channels'],ans:1,exp:'**Voltage-gated channels** open in response to changes in membrane potential, like the Na⁺ channels that open at threshold to fire an action potential. Ligand-gated channels instead open when a specific molecule binds.'},
  {s:'Biosignaling',q:'_____ open when a specific signaling molecule, usually a neurotransmitter or hormone, binds to them',choices:['Ungated channels','Aquaporins','Voltage-gated channels','Ligand-gated channels'],ans:3,exp:'**Ligand-gated channels** (ionotropic receptors) open when their ligand binds; the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor at the neuromuscular junction is the classic example. Voltage-gated channels respond to membrane potential rather than to a bound molecule.'},
  {s:'Biosignaling',q:'Enzyme-linked receptors bind an extracellular ligand and respond by initiating _____ inside the cell',choices:['second messenger cascades','direct DNA binding','ion channel opening','proteolysis'],ans:0,exp:'Enzyme-linked receptors have a ligand-binding domain, a membrane-spanning domain and an intracellular catalytic domain; ligand binding activates the catalytic domain (often by dimerization and autophosphorylation, as in receptor tyrosine kinases) and launches **second messenger cascades**. Direct DNA binding is a property of intracellular receptors for steroid hormones.'},
  {s:'Biosignaling',q:'_____ span the membrane seven times and, when ligand binds, activate an associated heterotrimeric protein that exchanges GDP for GTP to trigger intracellular signaling',choices:['G protein-coupled receptors','Nuclear receptors','Enzyme-linked receptors','Ligand-gated ion channels'],ans:0,exp:'**GPCRs** are 7-transmembrane receptors: ligand binding makes the α subunit of the coupled G protein swap GDP for GTP, and it then regulates effectors such as adenylyl cyclase (cAMP) or phospholipase C (IP₃/DAG). Enzyme-linked receptors also relay external signals, but through their own catalytic domains.'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'The _____ is the convention used for sugars that assigns configuration by comparing the chiral carbon farthest from the carbonyl with glyceraldehyde',choices:['E and Z system','cis and trans system','D and L system','R and S system'],ans:2,exp:'In the **D and L system**, a sugar is D if the –OH on its highest-numbered chiral carbon points right in a Fischer projection (like D-glyceraldehyde) and L if it points left; this is a relative configuration. The R and S system instead gives the absolute configuration of each individual stereocenter.'},
  {s:'General',q:'_____ are biomolecules with the general formula Cn(H₂O)n, which is why they were named hydrates of carbon',choices:['Nucleic acids','Proteins','Carbohydrates','Lipids'],ans:2,exp:'**Carbohydrates** such as glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆ = C₆(H₂O)₆) are polyhydroxy aldehydes or ketones; the name reflects the formula, not water molecules actually bound to carbon. Lipids also contain C, H and O but have far fewer oxygens relative to carbon.'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'_____ are stereoisomers that are non-superimposable mirror images of each other',choices:['Diastereomers','Anomers','Enantiomers','Epimers'],ans:2,exp:'**Enantiomers** have opposite configurations at every chiral center (e.g., D- and L-glucose); they share physical properties except that they rotate plane-polarized light in opposite directions. Diastereomers are stereoisomers that are not mirror images.'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'A chiral molecule _____ an internal plane of symmetry and has a _____ mirror image',choices:['has / superimposable','lacks / non-superimposable','lacks / superimposable','has / non-superimposable'],ans:1,exp:'A chiral molecule **lacks** an internal plane of symmetry, so its mirror image is **non-superimposable**, like left and right hands. A molecule with stereocenters but a symmetry plane is achiral (meso).'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'Configurational stereoisomers that are not mirror images of each other are _____',choices:['Enantiomers','Constitutional isomers','Conformational isomers','Diastereomers'],ans:3,exp:'**Diastereomers** differ at some, but not all, of their chiral centers, so unlike enantiomers they have different physical properties; cis/trans isomers are also diastereomers. Epimers and anomers are special cases of diastereomers.'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'_____ always come in pairs because a molecule has only one mirror image, while a molecule can have many _____ depending on its number of stereocenters',choices:['Enantiomers / diastereomers','Diastereomers / enantiomers','Epimers / enantiomers','Diastereomers / epimers'],ans:0,exp:'A molecule has exactly one mirror image, so **enantiomers** come in pairs. With n stereocenters there are up to 2ⁿ stereoisomers, so a molecule can have many **diastereomers** (e.g., D-glucose has 15 stereoisomers besides itself: one enantiomer and 14 diastereomers).'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'Sugars that differ in configuration at exactly one chiral carbon, such as D-glucose and D-galactose at C-4, are _____',choices:['Enantiomers','Epimers','Anomers','Constitutional isomers'],ans:1,exp:'**Epimers** are diastereomers that differ at a single stereocenter; D-mannose is the C-2 epimer and D-galactose the C-4 epimer of D-glucose. Anomers are the special case of epimers that differ at the anomeric carbon (C-1 of an aldose), which C-4 is not.'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'Cyclic sugars that differ only in configuration at the former carbonyl carbon, such as α- and β-D-glucopyranose, are _____',choices:['Enantiomers','Anomers','Constitutional isomers','Conformational isomers'],ans:1,exp:'**Anomers** are epimers that differ at the anomeric carbon (C-1 of aldoses, C-2 of ketoses), the carbon that becomes chiral on ring closure. They interconvert in solution through the open-chain form (mutarotation), whereas enantiomers would differ at every chiral center.'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'The carbon that is the carbonyl carbon in a sugar\'s open-chain form and becomes a new stereocenter when the ring closes is the _____',choices:['epimeric carbon','alpha carbon','anomeric carbon','hydroxymethyl carbon'],ans:2,exp:'The **anomeric carbon** (C-1 of glucose) is planar and achiral as a carbonyl, but becomes chiral when a hydroxyl attacks it to form a hemiacetal, giving α and β anomers. It is also the carbon that forms glycosidic bonds.'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'_____ is the process in which a monosaccharide\'s own hydroxyl group attacks its carbonyl carbon, converting the straight-chain form into a ring',choices:['Glycosylation','Mutarotation','Cyclization','Epimerization'],ans:2,exp:'**Cyclization** forms a cyclic hemiacetal (aldoses) or hemiketal (ketoses), usually a five-membered furanose or six-membered pyranose ring. Mutarotation is the related process of the ring opening and reclosing to interconvert α and β anomers.'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'In the Haworth projection of a D-sugar, the cyclic form whose anomeric –OH points down (trans to the CH₂OH group) is the _____',choices:['α-anomer','β-anomer','L-enantiomer','open-chain form'],ans:0,exp:'In the **α-anomer** the anomeric –OH is trans to the CH₂OH (down in a Haworth projection), and in the β-anomer it is cis (up); mnemonic: birds (β) fly up. α-1,4 links build starch and glycogen, while β-1,4 links build cellulose.'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'In the Haworth projection of a D-sugar, the cyclic form whose anomeric –OH points up (cis to the CH₂OH group) is the _____',choices:['open-chain form','L-enantiomer','β-anomer','α-anomer'],ans:2,exp:'In the **β-anomer** the anomeric –OH is cis to the CH₂OH, pointing up like a bird (β) flying up; the β-anomer of glucose is slightly favored at equilibrium because its –OH is equatorial in the chair. The α-anomer has the –OH pointing down.'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'A cyclic sugar drawn as a flat ring viewed edge-on, with each substituent shown pointing above or below the ring plane, is a _____',choices:['Fischer projection','Haworth projection','Newman projection','chair conformation'],ans:1,exp:'A **Haworth projection** shows a cyclic sugar as a planar ring with groups pointing up or down; for D-sugars, groups on the right of the Fischer projection point down. A Fischer projection instead shows the open-chain form as a vertical carbon backbone, and a Newman projection looks down a single C–C bond.'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'_____ is the spontaneous interconversion of the α and β anomers of a cyclic sugar in solution',choices:['Glycosylation','Cyclization','Epimerization','Mutarotation'],ans:3,exp:'In **mutarotation**, the ring opens to the straight-chain form and recloses with the anomeric -OH on the other face, so a pure α or β solution drifts to an equilibrium mixture. Epimerization changes configuration at a non-anomeric carbon and is not spontaneous; it requires an enzyme or base.'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'_____ are single carbohydrate units that cannot be hydrolyzed into smaller sugars',choices:['Disaccharides','Oligosaccharides','Polysaccharides','Monosaccharides'],ans:3,exp:'**Monosaccharides** (e.g., glucose, fructose, ribose) are the monomers of carbohydrates. Two joined by a glycosidic bond make a disaccharide, a few make an oligosaccharide, and many make a polysaccharide.'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'Reducing sugars have a free _____ group on the _____ carbon',choices:['methoxy / anomeric','hydroxyl / C-6','hydroxyl / anomeric','phosphate / anomeric'],ans:2,exp:'A free **hydroxyl** on the **anomeric** carbon (a hemiacetal or hemiketal) lets the ring open to an aldehyde, which reduces Tollens\' or Benedict\'s reagent. When the anomeric -OH is replaced, as in a methyl glycoside or sucrose, the ring is locked shut and the sugar is non-reducing.'},
  {s:'General',q:'_____ are sugars in which a hydroxyl group has been replaced by a hydrogen atom',choices:['Amino sugars','Sugar alcohols','Deoxy sugars','Uronic acids'],ans:2,exp:'**Deoxy sugars** lack an -OH; 2-deoxyribose in DNA has H instead of OH at C-2\', which makes DNA more chemically stable than RNA. Amino sugars instead replace an -OH with an amino group (e.g., glucosamine).'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'When the hydroxyl groups of a sugar react with a carboxylic acid or acid anhydride, the reaction is called _____',choices:['esterification','glycoside formation','saponification','mutarotation'],ans:0,exp:'Sugar -OH groups behave like alcohols, so they undergo **esterification** with carboxylic acids and their derivatives; phosphorylation of glucose to glucose 6-phosphate is the biological analog (a phosphate ester). Saponification is the reverse type of reaction: base-driven hydrolysis of an ester.'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'_____ is the basis for building complex carbohydrates and requires the anomeric carbon to link to another sugar',choices:['Esterification','Glycoside formation','Mutarotation','Oxidation'],ans:1,exp:'In **glycoside formation**, the hemiacetal at the anomeric carbon reacts with an -OH of another sugar (or alcohol) to form an acetal, the glycosidic bond. Chains of these bonds build disaccharides and polysaccharides; esterification links an -OH to an acyl group instead.'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'_____ form when a glycosidic bond joins exactly two monosaccharide subunits',choices:['Polysaccharides','Disaccharides','Oligosaccharides','Monosaccharides'],ans:1,exp:'**Disaccharides** such as sucrose (glucose + fructose), lactose (galactose + glucose) and maltose (glucose + glucose) contain one glycosidic bond. Oligosaccharides have roughly 3–10 units and polysaccharides many more.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'Lipids are _____ in water and _____ in nonpolar organic solvents',choices:['soluble / insoluble','insoluble / soluble','soluble / soluble','insoluble / insoluble'],ans:1,exp:'Lipids are mostly nonpolar hydrocarbon, so they are **insoluble** in polar water but **soluble** in nonpolar solvents such as ether or chloroform (like dissolves like). This is why dietary lipids need bile-salt micelles and lipoproteins to move through aqueous environments.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'Phospholipids contain a _____ head and _____ tails',choices:['hydrophobic / hydrophilic','hydrophilic / hydrophilic','hydrophilic / hydrophobic','hydrophobic / hydrophobic'],ans:2,exp:'Phospholipids are amphipathic: a polar **hydrophilic** phosphate head group and two nonpolar **hydrophobic** fatty acid tails. In water they spontaneously form bilayers, heads facing out and tails buried inside.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'Compared with an unsaturated fatty acid, a saturated fatty acid has _____ double bonds in its tail and is _____ fluid',choices:['no / more','one or more / less','no / less','one or more / more'],ans:2,exp:'Saturated fatty acids have **no** C=C double bonds, so their straight tails pack tightly with strong van der Waals contacts, making them **less** fluid (solid at room temperature, like butter). Cis double bonds in unsaturated tails create kinks that loosen packing and increase fluidity.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'_____ are phospholipids built on a glycerol backbone',choices:['Cerebrosides','Sphingolipids','Glycerophospholipids','Gangliosides'],ans:2,exp:'**Glycerophospholipids** (e.g., phosphatidylcholine) have two fatty acids esterified to glycerol plus a phosphate-linked head group. Sphingolipids, including cerebrosides and gangliosides, use a sphingosine backbone instead.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'_____ are lipids built on a sphingosine backbone rather than glycerol',choices:['Glycerophospholipids','Sphingolipids','Triacylglycerols','Waxes'],ans:1,exp:'**Sphingolipids** use sphingosine, an amino alcohol with a long hydrocarbon chain; adding a fatty acid by an amide bond gives ceramide, the parent of sphingomyelins, cerebrosides and gangliosides. Glycerophospholipids and triacylglycerols are built on glycerol.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'A _____ is a sphingolipid with a phosphocholine or phosphoethanolamine head group that is abundant in the myelin sheath around nerve axons',choices:['ceramide','cerebroside','ganglioside','sphingomyelin'],ans:3,exp:'**Sphingomyelins** are the major sphingophospholipids, and their phosphate-containing head group makes them phospholipids as well. Cerebrosides and gangliosides are glycosphingolipids with sugar head groups, and ceramide is the bare sphingosine + fatty acid core with only an -OH head.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'_____ are glycolipids built from sphingosine, a fatty acid, and one or more sugar units in place of a phosphate head group',choices:['Glycerophospholipids','Triacylglycerols','Glycosphingolipids','Sphingomyelins'],ans:2,exp:'**Glycosphingolipids** are ceramide (sphingosine + fatty acid) plus sugar head groups; they include cerebrosides (one sugar) and globosides and gangliosides (oligosaccharides). Sphingomyelins share the ceramide core but carry a phosphate head group, so they are phospholipids rather than glycolipids.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'A _____ is a glycosphingolipid with one or more sialic acid (NANA) residues on its oligosaccharide chain',choices:['ceramide','cerebroside','ganglioside','sphingomyelin'],ans:2,exp:'**Gangliosides** carry oligosaccharides ending in sialic acid (N-acetylneuraminic acid), which gives the head group a negative charge; they are abundant in neuronal membranes and act in cell recognition. A cerebroside has just a single sugar, and buildup of the ganglioside GM2 causes Tay-Sachs disease.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'_____ are lipids built from isoprene units that serve as metabolic precursors to steroids',choices:['Terpenes','Eicosanoids','Sphingolipids','Waxes'],ans:0,exp:'**Terpenes** are made of five-carbon isoprene units; squalene, a 30-carbon triterpene, cyclizes to form cholesterol and the steroid nucleus. Eicosanoids such as prostaglandins come from 20-carbon arachidonic acid, not from isoprene.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'The steroid nucleus contains _____ cyclohexane ring(s) and _____ cyclopentane ring(s)',choices:['2 / 2','3 / 1','1 / 3','4 / 0'],ans:1,exp:'The steroid nucleus has four fused rings: **3** six-membered (cyclohexane) rings and **1** five-membered (cyclopentane) ring. This 6-6-6-5 skeleton is shared by cholesterol, cortisol, aldosterone and the sex hormones.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'The precursor to all steroid hormones is _____',choices:['cortisol','testosterone','progesterone','cholesterol'],ans:3,exp:'**Cholesterol** is converted to pregnenolone (side-chain cleavage), which then gives rise to progesterone, cortisol, aldosterone, testosterone and estrogens. Progesterone is an intermediate on the path to other steroids, but it is itself made from cholesterol.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'_____ are 20-carbon signaling lipids made from arachidonic acid that act locally to regulate cAMP levels and mediate inflammation, pain, and fever',choices:['Leukotrienes','Terpenes','Ceramides','Prostaglandins'],ans:3,exp:'**Prostaglandins** are autocrine/paracrine eicosanoids made by cyclooxygenase (COX); they regulate cAMP synthesis and promote inflammation, pain and fever, which is why NSAIDs such as aspirin (COX inhibitors) relieve these symptoms. Leukotrienes are also eicosanoids but are made by lipoxygenase and mainly cause bronchoconstriction.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'The four fat-soluble vitamins, in alphabetical order, are _____, _____, _____, and _____',choices:['A / B / C / D','A / D / E / K','B / C / D / E','A / C / E / K'],ans:1,exp:'The fat-soluble vitamins are **A, D, E and K**; they are absorbed with dietary fat in micelles and chylomicrons and stored in liver and adipose, so they can accumulate to toxic levels. The B vitamins and vitamin C are water-soluble and are not stored to a large extent.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'_____ are the main storage form of fatty acids in the body',choices:['Waxes','Triacylglycerols','Phospholipids','Sphingolipids'],ans:1,exp:'**Triacylglycerols** (triglycerides) are three fatty acids esterified to glycerol. They are highly reduced and anhydrous, so they store about 9 kcal/g, more than twice the energy of carbohydrate or protein.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'_____ are cells specialized for storing triacylglycerol deposits',choices:['Hepatocytes','Chondrocytes','Adipocytes','Myocytes'],ans:2,exp:'**Adipocytes** store triacylglycerols in large lipid droplets and release fatty acids through hormone-sensitive lipase when glucagon or epinephrine is high. Hepatocytes make and export triacylglycerols in VLDL but are not the main storage site.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'_____ are unesterified fatty acids that travel in the bloodstream bound to albumin',choices:['Cholesterol esters','Phospholipids','Triacylglycerols','Free fatty acids'],ans:3,exp:'**Free fatty acids** have a free carboxyl group (not esterified) and are carried by serum albumin to tissues for beta oxidation. Triacylglycerols and cholesterol esters are esterified and must travel inside lipoproteins.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'_____ is the hydrolysis of the ester bonds of triacylglycerols using a strong base such as NaOH or KOH',choices:['Esterification','Transesterification','Saponification','Hydrogenation'],ans:2,exp:'**Saponification** cleaves triacylglycerols into glycerol and fatty acid salts, which are soaps. Esterification is the reverse reaction that forms the ester bonds, and hydrogenation adds H2 across C=C double bonds.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'A _____ is a single-layer sphere of amphipathic lipids that can dissolve a lipid-soluble molecule in its hydrophobic core',choices:['vesicle','micelle','lipid bilayer','liposome'],ans:1,exp:'A **micelle** has polar heads facing water and fatty acid tails pointing inward, forming a nonpolar core; bile-salt micelles carry dietary lipids to the intestinal wall. A liposome is a bilayer sphere with an aqueous center, so its interior holds water-soluble cargo.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Nucleic Acids',q:'_____ are polymers of nucleotides',choices:['Nucleic acids','Carbohydrates','Lipids','Proteins'],ans:0,exp:'**Nucleic acids** (DNA and RNA) are chains of nucleotides joined by 3\'–5\' phosphodiester bonds. Proteins are polymers of amino acids and polysaccharides are polymers of monosaccharides.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Nucleic Acids',q:'The primary structure of a nucleic acid is its _____',choices:['three-dimensional fold','sequence of nucleotides','base-pairing interactions','binding to histones'],ans:1,exp:'Primary structure is the linear **sequence of nucleotides**, read 5\' to 3\'. Higher levels build on it: base pairing gives secondary structure, the 3D fold gives tertiary structure, and interactions with other molecules give quaternary structure.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Nucleic Acids',q:'The secondary structure of a nucleic acid is made up of its _____',choices:['sequence of nucleotides','binding to histones','base-pairing interactions','three-dimensional fold'],ans:2,exp:'Secondary structure comes from **base-pairing interactions**, such as the Watson-Crick pairs of the double helix or the stem-loops (hairpins) of tRNA and other RNAs. The nucleotide sequence alone is primary structure.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Nucleic Acids',q:'The tertiary structure of a nucleic acid is defined by its _____',choices:['base-pairing interactions','sequence of nucleotides','three-dimensional arrangement of atoms','interactions with other molecules'],ans:2,exp:'Tertiary structure is the overall **three-dimensional arrangement of atoms**, such as the L-shape of folded tRNA or supercoiling of DNA. Interactions with other molecules, such as histones, are quaternary structure.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Nucleic Acids',q:'The quaternary structure of a nucleic acid is defined by its _____',choices:['sequence of nucleotides','base-pairing interactions','three-dimensional arrangement of atoms','interactions with other molecules'],ans:3,exp:'Quaternary structure covers a nucleic acid\'s **interactions with other molecules**, such as DNA wrapped around histones in nucleosomes or rRNA assembled with proteins in the ribosome. The 3D fold of a single chain is tertiary structure.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Structure',q:'_____ is a polymer of nucleotides whose two long strands wind into an antiparallel double helix',choices:['mRNA','DNA','Chromatin','Polypeptide'],ans:1,exp:'**DNA** (deoxyribonucleic acid) has two strands running in opposite directions (5\'→3\' paired with 3\'→5\') held together by complementary base pairing. mRNA is normally single-stranded, and chromatin is DNA packaged with histone proteins rather than the polymer itself.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Structure',q:'DNA is _____ charged at physiological pH because of its phosphate backbone',choices:['variably','neutrally','negatively','positively'],ans:2,exp:'Each phosphodiester linkage carries an ionized phosphate, so DNA is **negatively** charged. This is why it migrates toward the anode in gel electrophoresis and binds tightly to positively charged (lysine- and arginine-rich) histones.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Structure',q:'A _____ is a five-carbon sugar bonded to a nitrogenous base, with no phosphate groups',choices:['nucleoside','nucleobase','nucleotide','polynucleotide'],ans:0,exp:'A **nucleoside** is a base attached by a glycosidic bond to C-1\' of ribose or deoxyribose (e.g., adenosine). Adding one to three phosphates on C-5\' makes a nucleotide, and the base alone is a nucleobase.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Structure',q:'A _____ is a nucleoside with one to three phosphate groups attached',choices:['polynucleotide','nucleobase','nucleoside','nucleotide'],ans:3,exp:'A **nucleotide** is a nucleoside plus phosphate(s) on C-5\', such as AMP, ADP or ATP. Nucleotides are the monomers of DNA and RNA, and high-energy nucleoside triphosphates supply the energy for polymerization.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Structure',q:'The _____ (1953) describes DNA as two antiparallel strands wound into a right-handed double helix with complementary base pairing',choices:['Watson-Crick model','Pauling-Corey model','Meselson-Stahl model','Chargaff model'],ans:0,exp:'The **Watson-Crick model**, built using Franklin\'s X-ray data and Chargaff\'s base ratios, has a sugar-phosphate backbone outside and paired bases inside. Pauling and Corey had proposed an incorrect triple helix, and Meselson and Stahl later showed that replication is semiconservative.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Structure',q:'List the five nitrogenous bases of DNA and RNA, purines first and then pyrimidines, each group in alphabetical order: _____, _____, _____, _____, _____',choices:['adenine / cytosine / guanine / thymine / uracil','adenine / guanine / cytosine / thymine / uracil','adenine / thymine / cytosine / guanine / uracil','adenine / guanine / cytosine / thymine / xanthine'],ans:1,exp:'The purines are **adenine and guanine** (two rings); the pyrimidines are **cytosine, thymine (DNA only) and uracil (RNA only)** (one ring). Mnemonic: "Pure As Gold" for purines, and "CUT the Py" for pyrimidines.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Structure',q:'The pyrimidines in DNA and RNA have _____ ring(s)',choices:['1','4','3','2'],ans:0,exp:'Pyrimidines (cytosine, thymine, uracil) have a single six-membered ring, so **1** ring. Purines (adenine, guanine) have 2 fused rings, so each base pair always joins one purine to one pyrimidine, keeping the helix width constant.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Structure',q:'The purines in DNA and RNA have _____ ring(s)',choices:['3','1','4','2'],ans:3,exp:'Purines (adenine and guanine) have **2** fused rings, a six-membered ring joined to a five-membered imidazole ring. Pyrimidines have only one ring; remember the longer word, pyrimidine, names the smaller molecule.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Structure',q:'The B-DNA double helix has a diameter of about _____ angstroms',choices:['20','10','24','34'],ans:0,exp:'B-DNA is about **20** Å (2 nm) wide, a constant width that purine–pyrimidine pairing makes possible. 34 Å is a common trap: it is the length of one helical turn (about 10 base pairs at 3.4 Å each).'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Structure',q:'The sugar-phosphate backbone of DNA is held together by _____',choices:['hydrogen bonds','phosphodiester bonds','peptide bonds','glycosidic bonds'],ans:1,exp:'**Phosphodiester bonds** link the 3\'-OH of one sugar to the 5\'-phosphate of the next. Hydrogen bonds hold the two strands together through base pairs, and N-glycosidic bonds attach each base to its sugar.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Structure',q:'Adenine and thymine are held together by _____ hydrogen bonds, and guanine and cytosine by _____ hydrogen bonds',choices:['3 / 2','2 / 3','2 / 2','3 / 3'],ans:1,exp:'A–T pairs form **2** hydrogen bonds and G–C pairs form **3**. That is why GC-rich DNA has a higher melting temperature and is harder to denature.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Structure',q:'_____ state that in double-stranded DNA the amount of purines equals the amount of pyrimidines',choices:['The wobble hypothesis','Chargaff’s rules','The central dogma','The semiconservative model'],ans:1,exp:'**Chargaff\'s rules**: in double-stranded DNA, %A = %T and %G = %C, so purines (A + G) equal pyrimidines (C + T). This follows from complementary base pairing; for example, 30% A means 30% T, leaving 20% G and 20% C.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Replication',q:'DNA _____ is the process of producing an identical copy of a DNA molecule',choices:['transcription','recombination','transposition','replication'],ans:3,exp:'DNA **replication** happens in S phase and is semiconservative: each daughter helix has one parental and one new strand. Transcription instead copies a DNA segment into RNA.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Replication',q:'_____ relieves the positive supercoiling (torsional strain) that builds up ahead of the replication fork by nicking and resealing the DNA backbone',choices:['DNA polymerase','Topoisomerase','Helicase','Primase'],ans:1,exp:'Helicase unwinds the strands at the fork, which overwinds the DNA ahead of it; **topoisomerases** (e.g., topoisomerase I, II and bacterial DNA gyrase) relieve that strain by transiently cutting one or both strands and resealing them. Fluoroquinolone antibiotics work by inhibiting bacterial DNA gyrase.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Replication',q:'_____ breaks the hydrogen bonds between base pairs to separate the two DNA strands at the replication fork',choices:['Helicase','Topoisomerase','DNA ligase','Primase'],ans:0,exp:'**Helicase** uses ATP to unzip the double helix by breaking the hydrogen bonds between bases. Topoisomerase does not separate the strands; it relieves the supercoiling that unwinding creates.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Replication',q:'Single-strand binding protein coats single-stranded DNA during replication in order to _____',choices:['proofread newly made DNA','lay down RNA primers','join Okazaki fragments','keep the strands from reannealing'],ans:3,exp:'**Single-strand binding proteins** keep separated strands from reannealing and protect them from nucleases until polymerase copies them. Joining Okazaki fragments is the job of DNA ligase, and laying down primers is the job of primase.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Replication',q:'_____ synthesizes the RNA primer that starts each new DNA strand',choices:['Primase','DNA polymerase','Helicase','DNA ligase'],ans:0,exp:'**Primase** is an RNA polymerase that lays down a short RNA primer, since it can start a chain from scratch. DNA polymerase cannot; it can only extend an existing 3\'-OH.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Replication',q:'During cellular DNA replication, _____ are short nucleotide sequences complementary to the template that provide a 3\'-OH for synthesis to begin',choices:['RNA primers','Okazaki fragments','DNA primers','Telomeres'],ans:0,exp:'In cells, **RNA primers** made by primase start every leading strand and every Okazaki fragment, and they are later removed and replaced with DNA (by DNA polymerase I in prokaryotes). DNA primers are a laboratory tool, used in PCR and sequencing.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Replication',q:'_____ adds deoxyribonucleotides to the 3\' end of the growing strand during DNA replication',choices:['RNA polymerase','Primase','DNA polymerase','DNA ligase'],ans:2,exp:'**DNA polymerase** reads the template 3\'→5\' and synthesizes the new strand 5\'→3\', adding dNTPs to a free 3\'-OH. DNA ligase forms a phosphodiester bond only to seal nicks between existing fragments.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Replication',q:'_____ are short, newly synthesized DNA segments formed discontinuously on the lagging strand during DNA replication',choices:['RNA primers','Okazaki fragments','Replication bubbles','Telomeres'],ans:1,exp:'The lagging strand is made away from the fork in short **Okazaki fragments**, each started by its own RNA primer and later joined by DNA ligase. This is necessary because polymerase works only 5\'→3\'.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Replication',q:'_____ seals nicks between adjacent DNA fragments, such as Okazaki fragments, by forming phosphodiester bonds',choices:['Topoisomerase','DNA ligase','DNA polymerase','Primase'],ans:1,exp:'**DNA ligase** joins the 3\'-OH of one fragment to the 5\'-phosphate of the next, sealing the backbone after primers are replaced. DNA polymerase also makes phosphodiester bonds, but only by adding free nucleotides, not by joining two existing strands.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Repair',q:'_____ arise from mutations of proto-oncogenes, promote cell cycling, and may lead to cancer',choices:['Pseudogenes','Oncogenes','Tumor suppressor genes','Proto-oncogenes'],ans:1,exp:'**Oncogenes** are gain-of-function mutants of proto-oncogenes (e.g., RAS, MYC); one mutated allele is enough to drive proliferation, like a stuck accelerator. Tumor suppressor genes, by contrast, usually need both alleles lost (two-hit hypothesis).'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Repair',q:'_____ code for proteins that slow cell cycling or promote DNA repair',choices:['Tumor suppressor genes','Oncogenes','Proto-oncogenes','Housekeeping genes'],ans:0,exp:'**Tumor suppressor genes** such as p53 and Rb act as the brakes of the cell cycle; cancer develops when both copies are lost (loss of function). Proto-oncogenes instead promote growth and become oncogenes when overactivated.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Repair',q:'_____ proofreads its own work during replication and excises incorrectly paired bases',choices:['DNA polymerase','RNA polymerase','DNA ligase','Primase'],ans:0,exp:'Replicative **DNA polymerase** has 3\'→5\' exonuclease activity that removes a mismatched nucleotide right after it is added. RNA polymerase and primase lack this robust proofreading, which is one reason their error rates are higher.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Repair',q:'According to standard MCAT review sources, mismatch repair of DNA using the genes MSH2 and MLH1 occurs during _____',choices:['G2 phase','M phase','G1 phase','G0 phase'],ans:0,exp:'**G2 phase** is where MCAT review sources (Kaplan) place mismatch repair: MSH2 and MLH1 fix mismatches that polymerase proofreading missed, using the parental strand as the template. Strictly, mismatch repair is coupled to the replication fork and acts mostly during S phase (the new strand is recognized by methylation in E. coli and by strand nicks in eukaryotes), but G2 is the answer review books test. Inherited defects in these genes cause Lynch syndrome (HNPCC).'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Repair',q:'_____ fixes helix-deforming lesions of DNA, such as thymine dimers',choices:['Homologous recombination','Nucleotide excision repair','Mismatch repair','Base excision repair'],ans:1,exp:'**Nucleotide excision repair** cuts out a short oligonucleotide around a bulky lesion, such as a UV-induced thymine dimer, and refills the gap. Defects cause xeroderma pigmentosum (extreme UV sensitivity); base excision repair handles small, non-distorting lesions instead.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Repair',q:'_____ fixes small, non-helix-deforming lesions such as deaminated cytosine by removing the damaged base, leaving an apurinic/apyrimidinic (AP) site',choices:['Base excision repair','Nucleotide excision repair','Homologous recombination','Mismatch repair'],ans:0,exp:'In **base excision repair**, a glycosylase removes the damaged base (e.g., uracil from deaminated cytosine), an AP endonuclease cuts the backbone, and polymerase and ligase fill the gap. Nucleotide excision repair instead removes a whole stretch of nucleotides around bulky, helix-distorting damage.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Chromosomes',q:'Human somatic cells have _____ pairs of chromosomes, for a total of _____ chromosomes',choices:['46 / 92','22 / 44','23 / 46','24 / 48'],ans:2,exp:'Human somatic cells are diploid: **22 pairs of autosomes plus 1 pair of sex chromosomes** gives 23 pairs, or 46 chromosomes. Gametes are haploid with 23 chromosomes; 22 is only the number of autosome pairs.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Chromosomes',q:'_____ is the general term for the DNA–protein material of the eukaryotic nucleus, which exists as loosely packed euchromatin or densely packed heterochromatin',choices:['Nucleosome','Chromosome','Chromatid','Chromatin'],ans:3,exp:'**Chromatin** is DNA complexed with histones and other proteins, which compacts the genome to fit in the nucleus. Euchromatin is open and transcriptionally active, while heterochromatin is condensed and mostly silent; a nucleosome is only the repeating unit of chromatin.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Chromosomes',q:'_____ are repetitive TTAGGG sequences at the ends of linear chromosomes that protect coding DNA from being lost during replication and can be extended by a reverse transcriptase enzyme',choices:['Centromeres','Telomeres','Nucleosomes','Origins of replication'],ans:1,exp:'**Telomeres** cap chromosome ends; their G-rich repeats form tight structures that resist unraveling, and some are lost each round because lagging-strand synthesis cannot finish the very end. Telomerase, a reverse transcriptase with its own RNA template, extends them in germ cells, stem cells and most cancer cells.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Chromosomes',q:'_____ are constricted, heterochromatin-rich regions where sister chromatids are held together and where kinetochores attach spindle fibers until anaphase',choices:['Telomeres','Origins of replication','Centromeres','Nucleosomes'],ans:2,exp:'**Centromeres** join sister chromatids and anchor the kinetochore, where spindle microtubules attach; cohesin there is cleaved at anaphase so the chromatids separate. Telomeres are at the chromosome ends, not the attachment point.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Chromosomes',q:'A chromosome whose centromere lies very close to, but not at, one end, giving a very short p arm, is _____',choices:['metacentric','acrocentric','submetacentric','telocentric'],ans:1,exp:'**Acrocentric** chromosomes (human 13, 14, 15, 21, 22) have the centromere near one end, which is why they take part in Robertsonian translocations. Telocentric means the centromere is exactly at the end (not found in normal human cells), and metacentric means it is in the middle.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Replication',q:'In order, prokaryotes have _____ origin(s) of replication per chromosome, and eukaryotes have _____ origin(s) of replication per chromosome',choices:['1 / multiple','multiple / 1','1 / 1','multiple / multiple'],ans:0,exp:'A circular prokaryotic chromosome has a **single origin** (oriC), from which two forks proceed around the circle. Each long linear eukaryotic chromosome has **many origins**, so replication can finish within S phase.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'_____ is DNA made in the laboratory by joining sequences from two or more different sources, such as a human gene inserted into a bacterial plasmid',choices:['Complementary DNA','Genomic DNA','Mitochondrial DNA','Recombinant DNA'],ans:3,exp:'**Recombinant DNA** is built by cutting DNA with restriction enzymes and joining the pieces with DNA ligase, often in a vector that is then cloned in bacteria. Complementary DNA (cDNA) is different: it is copied from mRNA by reverse transcriptase and lacks introns.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'_____ describes the usual flow of genetic information, in which DNA is transcribed to RNA and RNA is translated to protein',choices:['The wobble hypothesis','The central dogma','Chargaff\'s rules','The Watson–Crick model'],ans:1,exp:'**The central dogma** (Crick) states that information flows DNA → RNA → protein. Reverse transcription in retroviruses is a known exception; Chargaff\'s rules describe base ratios, not information flow.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'The genetic code is described as _____ because most amino acids are specified by more than one codon',choices:['ambiguous','universal','degenerate','non-overlapping'],ans:2,exp:'The code is **degenerate**: 61 sense codons encode only 20 amino acids, and synonymous codons usually differ at the third (wobble) position. "Ambiguous" would mean the opposite problem, one codon encoding several amino acids, which does not occur.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'In the standard genetic code, the start codon is _____ and the stop codons are _____',choices:['AUG / UAA, UGG, UGA','AUG / UAA, UAG, UGA','UGA / UAA, UAG, AUG','AUG / UUA, UAG, UGA'],ans:1,exp:'**AUG** starts translation, and **UAA, UAG and UGA** are stop codons recognized by release factors rather than tRNAs (mnemonic: "U Are Away, U Are Gone, U Go Away"). UGG is a sense codon for tryptophan and UUA codes for leucine.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'The start codon _____ codes for the amino acid _____',choices:['AUG / tryptophan','UAG / methionine','AUG / methionine','UGG / methionine'],ans:2,exp:'**AUG** is the start codon and encodes **methionine** (N-formylmethionine in prokaryotes), so new polypeptides begin with Met. UGG is the single codon for tryptophan, and UAG is a stop codon.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'The _____ of a codon can pair with the tRNA anticodon by non-Watson–Crick wobble pairing, usually without changing the amino acid',choices:['first base','second base','third base','first and second bases'],ans:2,exp:'The **third (3\') base** of the codon pairs with the first (5\') base of the anticodon loosely, for example G with U or inosine with U, C or A. This wobble lets one tRNA read several synonymous codons and explains why third-position mutations are often silent.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'A mutation that substitutes one nucleotide for another at a single position is a _____',choices:['point mutation','frameshift mutation','chromosomal deletion','translocation'],ans:0,exp:'A **point mutation** changes a single base and can be silent, missense or nonsense depending on the new codon. Insertions or deletions that are not multiples of three cause frameshift mutations, while deletions and translocations of large segments are chromosomal mutations.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'A _____ changes a codon to another codon for the same amino acid, so the protein sequence is unchanged',choices:['nonsense mutation','missense mutation','frameshift mutation','silent mutation'],ans:3,exp:'A **silent mutation** exploits the degeneracy of the code, usually through a change at the third (wobble) position. A missense mutation, by contrast, does change the amino acid.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'A _____ converts a codon for an amino acid into a premature stop codon',choices:['silent mutation','missense mutation','nonsense mutation','frameshift mutation'],ans:2,exp:'A **nonsense mutation** creates UAA, UAG or UGA early in the reading frame, giving a truncated protein that is often nonfunctional (and the mRNA is often destroyed by nonsense-mediated decay). Mnemonic: nonsense means the protein "stops making sense".'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'A _____ changes a codon so that it specifies a different amino acid',choices:['missense mutation','nonsense mutation','silent mutation','frameshift mutation'],ans:0,exp:'A **missense mutation** substitutes one amino acid for another, as in sickle cell disease (Glu → Val in β-globin). It is conservative if the new residue has similar properties and nonconservative if not.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'A _____ results from inserting or deleting a number of nucleotides that is not a multiple of three, which changes every downstream codon',choices:['nonsense mutation','frameshift mutation','silent mutation','missense mutation'],ans:1,exp:'Because codons are read in non-overlapping triplets, a **frameshift mutation** shifts the reading frame from that point on, usually scrambling the protein and creating an early stop codon. An in-frame insertion or deletion of three bases adds or removes one amino acid instead.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Structure',q:'In order, RNA uses _____ as its sugar and uses _____ in place of _____',choices:['deoxyribose / uracil / thymine','ribose / thymine / uracil','ribose / cytosine / thymine','ribose / uracil / thymine'],ans:3,exp:'RNA contains **ribose** (with a 2\'-OH) and the base **uracil** in place of **thymine**. DNA has deoxyribose and thymine; the 2\'-OH makes RNA more reactive and less stable than DNA.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Transcription',q:'_____ is transcribed from DNA in the nucleus, processed, and exported to the cytoplasm, where its codons are read during translation',choices:['Messenger RNA','Ribosomal RNA','Small nuclear RNA','Transfer RNA'],ans:0,exp:'**Messenger RNA** carries the coding sequence as codons that ribosomes read. Transfer RNA carries the matching anticodons, and ribosomal RNA forms the ribosome itself.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'_____ carries amino acids to the ribosome and recognizes each mRNA codon by base pairing with its anticodon',choices:['Messenger RNA','Small nuclear RNA','Transfer RNA','Ribosomal RNA'],ans:2,exp:'**Transfer RNA** is charged with its amino acid by an aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase (using ATP), and its anticodon pairs antiparallel with the codon. Small nuclear RNA works in splicing, not translation.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'_____ is the main structural component of the ribosome and catalyzes peptide bond formation',choices:['Small nuclear RNA','Messenger RNA','Transfer RNA','Ribosomal RNA'],ans:3,exp:'**Ribosomal RNA** is a ribozyme: rRNA in the large subunit provides the peptidyl transferase activity. It is transcribed mainly in the nucleolus.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Transcription',q:'_____ is the first step of gene expression, in which RNA polymerase copies a segment of DNA into RNA',choices:['Translation','Reverse transcription','Transcription','Replication'],ans:2,exp:'In **transcription**, RNA polymerase reads the template (antisense) strand 3\'→5\' and builds RNA 5\'→3\', with no primer needed. Reverse transcription runs the other way, from RNA to DNA.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Structure',q:'About 147 base pairs of DNA wrapped around an octamer of histone proteins forms a _____',choices:['chromosome','nucleosome','chromatid','chromatin fiber'],ans:1,exp:'A **nucleosome** is DNA wrapped around two copies each of histones H2A, H2B, H3 and H4, with H1 sealing the linker DNA, giving a "beads on a string" look. Nucleosomes are the repeating units that coil into chromatin fibers.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: RNA Processing',q:'_____ is the set of nuclear processing steps, including 5\' capping, splicing, and 3\' polyadenylation, that converts a eukaryotic primary transcript (hnRNA) into mature mRNA',choices:['Post-translational modification','Post-transcriptional modification','Transcription','RNA interference'],ans:1,exp:'**Post-transcriptional modification** happens in the nucleus before export and occurs only in eukaryotes, since prokaryotic mRNA is translated as it is made. Post-translational modification acts on proteins, not RNA.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: RNA Processing',q:'_____ are the segments of a eukaryotic gene that remain in the mature mRNA after splicing and are expressed',choices:['Introns','Exons','Operons','Transposons'],ans:1,exp:'**Exons** are joined by the spliceosome and exported (mnemonic: exons exit the nucleus and are expressed). Introns are removed as lariats and stay in the nucleus.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: RNA Processing',q:'_____ are noncoding segments of a eukaryotic pre-mRNA that the spliceosome removes as lariat structures',choices:['Exons','Operons','Introns','Transposons'],ans:2,exp:'**Introns** (intervening sequences) are cut out by snRNPs, which recognize the 5\' GU and 3\' AG splice sites and a branch-point A that forms the lariat. Exons are the segments kept in mature mRNA.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: RNA Processing',q:'_____ joins different combinations of exons from the same pre-mRNA, allowing a single gene to encode multiple protein isoforms',choices:['RNA editing','Alternative splicing','Post-translational modification','Epigenetic silencing'],ans:1,exp:'**Alternative splicing** includes or skips particular exons, which is why humans make far more proteins than they have genes. RNA editing changes individual bases in the transcript instead of rearranging exons.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'The 5\' cap and poly-A tail of eukaryotic mRNA mainly _____',choices:['mark it for rapid degradation in the cytoplasm','target it to the rough ER for cotranslational import','define the intron–exon boundaries for splicing','protect it from exonucleases and promote translation'],ans:3,exp:'The **7-methylguanosine cap** and **poly-A tail** shield the ends from exonucleases, aid nuclear export, and help ribosomes bind; the tail shortens over time and acts as a lifespan timer. Targeting to the rough ER is done by an N-terminal signal sequence on the protein, not by the cap or tail.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'Prokaryotic mRNA that encodes several different proteins in a single transcript is described as _____',choices:['monocistronic','polycistronic','polyadenylated','alternatively spliced'],ans:1,exp:'**Polycistronic** mRNAs come from operons and carry several coding regions, each with its own Shine-Dalgarno sequence and start codon. Eukaryotic mRNAs are typically monocistronic, encoding one protein.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'Ribosomes are the sites where _____ occurs',choices:['transcription','translation','replication','RNA splicing'],ans:1,exp:'Ribosomes carry out **translation**, reading mRNA codons and joining amino acids into a polypeptide. Transcription and splicing happen in the nucleus, carried out by RNA polymerase and spliceosomes.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'In translation, a succession of _____ add their amino acids to the growing polypeptide as the mRNA moves through the _____ one codon at a time',choices:['mRNAs / ribosome','tRNAs / ribosome','tRNAs / spliceosome','snRNAs / proteasome'],ans:1,exp:'Charged **tRNAs** enter the **ribosome** in turn; each new peptide bond moves the growing chain onto the newest tRNA, and the ribosome then translocates one codon (3 nucleotides) along the mRNA. Spliceosomes process pre-mRNA in the nucleus and play no part in translation.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'Translation occurs in the _____, at the _____',choices:['nucleus / ribosome','cytoplasm / spliceosome','cytoplasm / ribosome','nucleus / nucleolus'],ans:2,exp:'Translation takes place in the **cytoplasm** on **ribosomes**, which may be free or bound to the rough ER. The nucleolus makes rRNA and assembles ribosomal subunits but does not translate.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'In order, the three stages of translation are _____, _____, and _____',choices:['elongation / initiation / termination','initiation / translocation / termination','initiation / elongation / termination','transcription / elongation / termination'],ans:2,exp:'**Initiation** assembles the ribosome on the start codon with Met-tRNA in the P site; **elongation** repeats A-site entry, peptide bond formation and translocation; **termination** begins when a release factor recognizes a stop codon. Translocation is one step within elongation, not a separate stage.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'In prokaryotic translation initiation, the 30S ribosomal subunit binds the _____, a purine-rich sequence just upstream of the start codon',choices:["TATA promoter sequence","Kozak initiation sequence","7-methylguanosine cap","Shine-Dalgarno sequence"],ans:3,exp:'The **Shine-Dalgarno sequence** base-pairs with 16S rRNA, which positions the AUG in the P site. Eukaryotic 40S subunits instead bind the 5\' cap and scan for the first AUG (often in a Kozak sequence); the TATA box is a promoter element in DNA.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'A Svedberg (S) unit measures a particle\'s _____ during ultracentrifugation',choices:['sedimentation rate','electrophoretic mobility','molecular weight','buoyant density'],ans:0,exp:'The **Svedberg** is a sedimentation coefficient: how fast a particle settles, which depends on both mass and shape. That is why S values are not additive (30S + 50S = 70S).'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'Complete prokaryotic ribosomes sediment at _____',choices:['60S','70S','50S','80S'],ans:1,exp:'Prokaryotic ribosomes are **70S**, made of a 30S small subunit (16S rRNA) and a 50S large subunit. Their differences from eukaryotic ribosomes let antibiotics such as tetracyclines and aminoglycosides target bacteria selectively.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'Complete eukaryotic cytoplasmic ribosomes sediment at _____',choices:['80S','70S','60S','40S'],ans:0,exp:'Eukaryotic ribosomes are **80S**, made of a 40S small and a 60S large subunit. Mitochondria keep bacteria-like 70S-type ribosomes, consistent with endosymbiotic theory.'},
  {s:'General',q:'During translation elongation, each incoming aminoacyl-tRNA first enters the ribosome at the _____',choices:['P site','A site','E site','exit tunnel'],ans:1,exp:'The ribosome moves tRNAs in the order **A → P → E**: aminoacyl-tRNAs enter at the A site, the peptidyl-tRNA sits in the P site, and uncharged tRNAs leave from the E site. The only exception is the initiator Met-tRNA, which goes directly to the P site.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'In translation, _____ begins when the codon in the A site is a stop codon',choices:['translocation','termination','initiation','elongation'],ans:1,exp:'In **termination**, a release factor binds the stop codon in the A site and causes peptidyl transferase to add water, hydrolyzing the bond that links the polypeptide to the tRNA. The finished protein is released and the ribosome comes apart.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Translation',q:'Changes made to a protein after it is synthesized, such as phosphorylation, glycosylation, and cleavage of a signal peptide, are called _____',choices:['Epigenetic modifications','Post-transcriptional modifications','Post-translational modifications','Allosteric modifications'],ans:2,exp:'**Post-translational modifications** happen in the cytosol, ER and Golgi and control a protein\'s activity, location and lifespan (e.g., zymogen cleavage, ubiquitination). Post-transcriptional modifications act on RNA, not protein.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Chromosomes',q:'Eukaryotes have _____ chromosome(s), and prokaryotes typically have _____ chromosome(s)',choices:['a single circular / multiple linear','multiple linear / a single circular','multiple circular / a single linear','multiple linear / multiple circular'],ans:1,exp:'Eukaryotic genomes are split among **multiple linear** chromosomes with telomeres. Most prokaryotes have **a single circular** chromosome in the nucleoid, often with extra plasmids.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Chromosomes',q:'A _____ is one of the two identical copies of a replicated chromosome, joined to its partner at the centromere',choices:['chromatin fiber','nucleosome','chromosome','chromatid'],ans:3,exp:'After S phase, each chromosome consists of two sister **chromatids** held together by cohesin at the centromere. They separate at anaphase, and each one then counts as its own chromosome.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Chromosomes',q:'A _____ is a single long DNA molecule, with its associated proteins, that carries part of an organism\'s genome; human somatic cells have 46 of them',choices:['chromatid','nucleosome','chromatin fiber','chromosome'],ans:3,exp:'Each **chromosome** is one continuous DNA double helix packaged with proteins. A replicated chromosome has two chromatids, so a G2 cell still has 46 chromosomes but 92 chromatids.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'_____ are clusters of prokaryotic genes transcribed together from a single promoter into one polycistronic mRNA',choices:['Regulons','Promoters','Enhancers','Operons'],ans:3,exp:'An **operon** contains a promoter, an operator, and structural genes that are controlled as a unit, such as the lac and trp operons. A regulon is a larger set of genes or operons, each with its own promoter, that share a common regulator.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'In an operon, the _____ is the DNA sequence where RNA polymerase binds to begin transcription',choices:['operator','silencer','promoter','enhancer'],ans:2,exp:'The **promoter** is where RNA polymerase (with its sigma factor in bacteria) binds and starts transcription. The operator, next to or overlapping the promoter, is where the repressor binds.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'In an operon, the _____ is a short DNA sequence, overlapping or next to the promoter, that binds a repressor protein to block transcription',choices:['enhancer','operator','silencer','promoter'],ans:1,exp:'When a repressor sits on the **operator**, it physically blocks RNA polymerase from transcribing the structural genes. Enhancers and silencers are mainly eukaryotic regulatory elements that can act from a distance.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'The regulatory protein that binds an operon\'s operator and prevents RNA polymerase from transcribing the genes is the _____',choices:['activator','corepressor','repressor','inducer'],ans:2,exp:'The **repressor** is encoded by a separate regulator gene and binds the operator to switch the operon off. A corepressor is a small molecule (e.g., tryptophan) that activates the repressor, not the DNA-binding protein itself.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'The regulatory protein that binds near an operon\'s promoter and increases RNA polymerase binding, as CAP–cAMP does for the lac operon, is the _____',choices:['activator','repressor','inducer','corepressor'],ans:0,exp:'An **activator** gives positive control by helping RNA polymerase bind the promoter; CAP binds only when cAMP is high, which happens when glucose is low. An inducer is a small molecule, not a DNA-binding protein.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'The small molecule that binds a repressor and releases it from the operator, switching the operon on (e.g., allolactose), is the _____',choices:['repressor','corepressor','activator','inducer'],ans:3,exp:'An **inducer** changes the repressor\'s shape so it can no longer bind the operator, so transcription proceeds. A corepressor does the opposite: it binds the repressor so that it can bind the operator.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'In the absence of its inducer, an inducible operon is _____',choices:['on, with the repressor bound to the inducer','off, with the repressor bound to the operator','on, with an activator bound to the promoter','off, with the promoter silenced by methylation'],ans:1,exp:'Inducible operons such as lac are **off by default**: the active repressor sits on the operator until an inducer (allolactose) removes it. They are used for pathways needed only when their substrate is present.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'In the absence of its corepressor, a repressible operon is _____',choices:['off, with the active repressor bound to the operator','off, with the promoter silenced by methylation','on, with the corepressor bound to the promoter','on, with the inactive repressor unable to bind the operator'],ans:3,exp:'Repressible operons such as trp are **on by default** because their repressor is inactive on its own. When the end product (tryptophan) builds up, it acts as a corepressor, activating the repressor so it binds the operator and stops transcription.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'_____ are proteins that bind specific DNA sequences, such as promoters and enhancers, to turn particular genes on or off',choices:['RNA polymerases','Transcription factors','Histone deacetylases','DNA methyltransferases'],ans:1,exp:'**Transcription factors** have a DNA-binding domain (e.g., zinc finger, leucine zipper, helix-turn-helix) and an activation domain that recruits or blocks RNA polymerase. Histone deacetylases and DNA methyltransferases change chromatin generally rather than recognizing a gene\'s specific regulatory sequence.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'_____ are DNA sequences located just upstream of a gene, such as the TATA box, where RNA polymerase and general transcription factors assemble to start transcription',choices:['Enhancers','Silencers','Operators','Promoters'],ans:3,exp:'**Promoters** set where and in which direction transcription starts; in eukaryotes, TATA-binding protein (part of TFIID) recognizes the TATA box about 25 bp upstream. Enhancers can work from far away, while promoters sit right next to the start site.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'_____ are DNA sequences that bind activator proteins and can raise transcription of a gene even when located thousands of base pairs upstream or downstream of it',choices:['Silencers','Promoters','Enhancers','Operators'],ans:2,exp:'**Enhancers** work by DNA looping, which brings bound activators close to the promoter and its transcription machinery. Silencers act the same way but bind repressors and lower transcription.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'_____ loosens chromatin, increasing the access of transcription factors and other DNA-binding proteins',choices:['DNA methylation','Histone deacetylation','Histone acetylation','Histone methylation'],ans:2,exp:'**Histone acetylation** by histone acetyltransferases adds acetyl groups to lysines, neutralizing their positive charge so the histones hold the negatively charged DNA less tightly (euchromatin). Deacetylation and DNA methylation have the opposite, silencing effect.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'Heavy methylation of the CpG islands in a gene\'s promoter _____ the access of transcription machinery to that DNA',choices:['increases','does not affect','transiently increases','decreases'],ans:3,exp:'**DNA methylation** of cytosines in CpG islands recruits methyl-binding proteins and deacetylases that compact chromatin into heterochromatin, silencing the gene. It underlies genomic imprinting and X-inactivation.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'The _____ encodes proteins that import lactose into the bacterial cell and hydrolyze it into glucose and galactose',choices:['trp operon','his operon','lac operon','ara operon'],ans:2,exp:'The **lac operon** encodes β-galactosidase (lacZ), permease (lacY) and transacetylase (lacA). The trp operon instead encodes the enzymes that synthesize tryptophan, and the ara operon handles arabinose.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'The lac operon is classified as _____',choices:['repressible','constitutive','inducible','silenced'],ans:2,exp:'The lac operon is **inducible**: it stays off until allolactose inactivates the lac repressor, and it is fully on only when glucose is also low (CAP–cAMP). Repressible operons such as trp are on by default and are switched off by their end product.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'The lac operon is strongly transcribed only when lactose is _____ and glucose is _____',choices:['present / high','absent / low','present / low','absent / high'],ans:2,exp:'Lactose (as allolactose) removes the repressor from the operator, and low glucose raises cAMP so the **cAMP–CAP complex** can recruit RNA polymerase. With lactose present but glucose high, the repressor is off yet CAP is inactive, so transcription stays low (catabolite repression).'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'_____ binds to the lac repressor and changes its shape so that the repressor detaches from the operator',choices:['Lactose','Allolactose','Glucose','cAMP'],ans:1,exp:'**Allolactose**, an isomer formed from lactose by β-galactosidase, is the true inducer: it binds the repressor allosterically and lowers its affinity for the operator. cAMP acts on a different protein, CAP, which is an activator rather than the repressor.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'In a Haworth projection of a D-sugar, a glycosidic bond whose anomeric substituent points down (trans to the CH2OH on C-5) is _____, the linkage type found in _____',choices:['beta / glycogen','alpha / cellulose','alpha / glycogen','beta / cellulose'],ans:2,exp:'For D-sugars in a Haworth projection, an **alpha** anomeric bond points down (trans to the CH2OH) and a beta bond points up (cis to it). Glycogen, starch and maltose use alpha linkages, whereas cellulose and lactose use beta linkages, which humans cannot digest except for lactose via lactase.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'The _____ describes the plasma membrane as a dynamic, semisolid phospholipid bilayer in which lipids, proteins and carbohydrates can move laterally',choices:['lipid raft model','fluid mosaic model','Davson-Danielli model','unit membrane model'],ans:1,exp:'The **fluid mosaic model** (Singer and Nicolson, 1972) pictures a fluid bilayer studded with a mosaic of proteins, cholesterol and carbohydrates that diffuse in the plane of the membrane. The older Davson-Danielli model wrongly placed proteins as static sheets sandwiching the bilayer.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'Lipids move freely in the plane of the membrane and can cluster into cholesterol- and sphingolipid-rich microdomains called _____',choices:['liposomes','vesicles','micelles','lipid rafts'],ans:3,exp:'**Lipid rafts** are ordered patches of cholesterol and sphingolipids within the bilayer that concentrate signaling proteins. Micelles and liposomes are separate lipid assemblies that form in water, not domains within an existing membrane.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'_____ are ATP-dependent membrane enzymes that move specific phospholipids from the outer (extracellular) leaflet to the inner (cytoplasmic) leaflet of the bilayer',choices:['Flippases','Floppases','Scramblases','Translocons'],ans:0,exp:'Spontaneous flip-flop between leaflets is very slow, so **flippases** use ATP to move lipids such as phosphatidylserine inward, keeping the membrane asymmetric. Floppases use ATP to move lipids outward, and scramblases are ATP-independent and move lipids in both directions, erasing the asymmetry (e.g., phosphatidylserine exposure in apoptosis).'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'Compared with lipids, membrane proteins and their attached carbohydrates diffuse laterally much more slowly, mainly because of their _____',choices:['net charge','glycosylation','larger size','hydrophobicity'],ans:2,exp:'Lateral diffusion slows as the diffusing unit gets bigger, so large proteins (and glycoproteins) move far more slowly than individual phospholipids; anchoring to the cytoskeleton can slow them further. Hydrophobic surfaces are what keep integral proteins in the bilayer, not what slows them.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'Phosphatidic acid and diacylglycerol, made from fatty acids and glycerol 3-phosphate on the same pathway that builds triacylglycerols, are the precursors of _____',choices:['cholesterol','glycerophospholipids','sphingolipids','prostaglandins'],ans:1,exp:'Triacylglycerol and **glycerophospholipid** synthesis share early steps: fatty acids are esterified to glycerol 3-phosphate to form phosphatidic acid, which is then converted either to triacylglycerol or to phospholipids with polar head groups. Sphingolipids are built on a sphingosine backbone, not glycerol.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'_____ have the same glycerol backbone as triacylglycerols, except that one fatty acid is replaced by a phosphate group linked to a polar head group',choices:['Glycerophospholipids','Gangliosides','Cerebrosides','Sphingolipids'],ans:0,exp:'**Glycerophospholipids** (e.g., phosphatidylcholine) have two fatty acid tails and a phosphate-containing head on glycerol, which makes them amphipathic and ideal for bilayers. Cerebrosides and gangliosides are sphingolipids, built on sphingosine rather than glycerol.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'_____ buffers membrane fluidity in animal cells, raising it at low temperatures and lowering it at high temperatures',choices:['Cholesterol','Sphingomyelin','Phosphatidylcholine','Glycolipids'],ans:0,exp:'**Cholesterol** inserts between phospholipids: its rigid ring system restricts tail movement when the membrane is warm, and it disrupts tight tail packing when the membrane is cold. It can make up a large fraction of membrane lipid by mass, which also adds mechanical stability.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'At low temperatures, cholesterol will _____ membrane fluidity',choices:['decrease','eliminate','have no effect on','increase'],ans:3,exp:'In the cold, phospholipid tails tend to pack into a rigid gel, and cholesterol wedges between them to prevent crystallization, so it **increases** fluidity. At high temperatures the effect reverses and cholesterol decreases fluidity.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'At high temperatures, cholesterol will _____ membrane fluidity',choices:['have no effect on','increase','eliminate','decrease'],ans:3,exp:'When the membrane is warm and the tails move freely, cholesterol\'s rigid steroid rings restrict their motion, so it **decreases** fluidity. This is the opposite of its effect in the cold, which is why cholesterol is called a fluidity buffer.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'_____ are esters of long-chain fatty acids and long-chain alcohols that provide waterproofing and protection, as on the surfaces of plant leaves',choices:['Cholesterol esters','Waxes','Phospholipids','Triacylglycerols'],ans:1,exp:'**Waxes** pair a long fatty acid with a long monohydroxy alcohol, making them extremely hydrophobic with high melting points; their main role is as protective surface coatings (plant cuticles, beeswax, earwax), and review books note only small amounts in some membranes. Triacylglycerols are also fatty acid esters, but of glycerol, and serve as energy storage.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'Integral proteins that span the entire bilayer, with domains exposed on both faces of the membrane, are called _____',choices:['transmembrane proteins','embedded proteins','peripheral proteins','lipid-anchored proteins'],ans:0,exp:'**Transmembrane proteins** cross the whole bilayer and often act as channels, carriers or receptors. Embedded proteins are also integral but sit in only one leaflet, and peripheral proteins attach to the surface by noncovalent interactions.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'Unlike transmembrane proteins, embedded membrane proteins are inserted into _____',choices:['both leaflets','one leaflet only','the glycocalyx only','neither leaflet'],ans:1,exp:'Embedded proteins are integral proteins associated with **only one leaflet** (the cytoplasmic or the extracellular face) and often form catalytic or signaling complexes. Transmembrane proteins pass through both leaflets.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'_____ is the umbrella term for membrane proteins with one or more segments buried in the hydrophobic core of the bilayer, including both transmembrane and embedded proteins',choices:['Glycoproteins','Peripheral proteins','Integral proteins','Cytoskeletal proteins'],ans:2,exp:'**Integral proteins** have hydrophobic regions that interact with the lipid tails, so they can be removed only with detergents. Peripheral proteins bind the membrane surface through electrostatic and hydrogen-bond interactions and can be removed with salt or pH changes.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'The protective, carbohydrate-rich layer on the outer surface of the plasma membrane, formed by sugars attached to membrane proteins and lipids, is the _____',choices:['glycocalyx','basement membrane','cell wall','extracellular matrix'],ans:0,exp:'The **glycocalyx** is made of the oligosaccharide chains of glycoproteins and glycolipids; it protects the cell and takes part in cell recognition (e.g., ABO blood group antigens). The extracellular matrix is a separate network of secreted proteins such as collagen outside the cell.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'A hydrophilic peptide hormone changes the activity of its target cell by _____',choices:['diffusing across the bilayer to a nuclear receptor','binding a surface receptor that signals inward','entering through a channel to act in the cytosol','passing through gap junctions to the nucleus'],ans:1,exp:'Polar ligands cannot cross the hydrophobic core, so they **bind membrane receptors** (e.g., GPCRs or receptor tyrosine kinases) that relay the signal through second messengers. Diffusing through the bilayer to an intracellular receptor is how lipid-soluble steroid hormones act.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'_____ seal adjacent epithelial cells together, preventing solutes from leaking between cells by the paracellular route, but do not allow exchange between the cells',choices:['Tight junctions','Gap junctions','Desmosomes','Adherens junctions'],ans:0,exp:'**Tight junctions** (claudins, occludins) form a watertight seal that forces solutes to cross epithelia through cells, as in the blood-brain barrier and renal tubules. Gap junctions are the junctions that connect cytoplasm between cells.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'_____ allow rapid exchange of ions and other small molecules directly between the cytoplasm of adjacent cells',choices:['Gap junctions','Tight junctions','Adherens junctions','Desmosomes'],ans:0,exp:'**Gap junctions** are channels made of connexins that link neighboring cells, letting ions pass so that, for example, cardiac muscle cells depolarize together. Tight junctions seal the space between cells but do not connect their cytoplasm.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'_____ bind adjacent cells together by anchoring to their intermediate filament cytoskeletons',choices:['Desmosomes','Adherens junctions','Gap junctions','Tight junctions'],ans:0,exp:'**Desmosomes** use cadherins linked to intermediate filaments (keratin in epithelia) to resist mechanical stress, as in skin. Adherens junctions also use cadherins but connect to actin filaments rather than intermediate filaments.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'_____ is the pressure that must be applied to a solution to stop the net flow of water into it across a semipermeable membrane',choices:['Turgor pressure','Hydrostatic pressure','Osmotic pressure','Oncotic pressure'],ans:2,exp:'**Osmotic pressure** is a colligative property, Π = iMRT, so it depends on the number of dissolved particles. Oncotic pressure is the specific part of osmotic pressure created by plasma proteins such as albumin.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'_____ is the general term for any movement of a solute down its concentration gradient that needs no energy input',choices:['Active transport','Endocytosis','Passive transport','Secondary active transport'],ans:2,exp:'**Passive transport** (simple diffusion, osmosis and facilitated diffusion) is driven by the increase in entropy as a solute moves down its gradient, so ΔG < 0 and no ATP is needed. Secondary active transport moves its solute against a gradient, using the energy of another ion\'s gradient.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'In simple diffusion, molecules move passively across the bilayer, without a transport protein, from an area of _____ concentration to an area of _____ concentration until equilibrium is reached',choices:['high / low','low / high','high / equal','equal / high'],ans:0,exp:'Simple diffusion moves solute **down** its gradient, from **high to low** concentration, for small nonpolar molecules such as O2 and CO2 that dissolve in the bilayer. The rate is proportional to the gradient and never saturates, unlike carrier-mediated transport.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'_____ is a form of passive transport describing the diffusion of water across a selectively permeable membrane',choices:['Osmosis','Facilitated diffusion','Endocytosis','Active transport'],ans:0,exp:'In **osmosis**, water moves toward the side with higher solute concentration (lower water concentration), often through aquaporins. It continues until osmotic pressure balances the gradient.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'_____ is a form of passive transport that uses carrier or channel proteins to move solutes that cannot cross the bilayer on their own',choices:['Active transport','Simple diffusion','Osmosis','Facilitated diffusion'],ans:3,exp:'**Facilitated diffusion** still moves solute down its gradient without energy, but polar or charged solutes (glucose through GLUT, ions through channels) need a protein pathway. Because carriers can saturate, it shows Michaelis-Menten-like kinetics, unlike simple diffusion.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'_____ moves a solute against its gradient and requires energy, either from ATP or from an existing favorable gradient of a different ion',choices:['Facilitated diffusion','Active transport','Osmosis','Passive transport'],ans:1,exp:'**Active transport** has ΔG > 0 for the solute moved, so it must be coupled to an energy source. Using ATP directly is primary active transport, and using another ion\'s gradient is secondary active transport.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'In _____, the energy for transport comes directly from ATP hydrolysis',choices:['secondary active transport','simple diffusion','primary active transport','facilitated diffusion'],ans:2,exp:'**Primary active transport** pumps, such as the Na+/K+ ATPase (3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in) and the Ca2+ ATPase, hydrolyze ATP themselves. Secondary active transporters use no ATP directly; they use gradients that primary pumps built.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'_____ harnesses the energy released by one particle moving down its electrochemical gradient to drive a different particle up its gradient',choices:['Primary active transport','Simple diffusion','Secondary active transport','Facilitated diffusion'],ans:2,exp:'**Secondary active transport** (coupled transport) relies on gradients made by primary pumps; for example, SGLT uses the inward Na+ gradient to pull glucose into intestinal and renal cells. It can be a symport or an antiport.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'In membrane transport, the term antiport is used when two particles flow in _____',choices:['the same direction','opposite directions','random directions','alternating directions'],ans:1,exp:'An **antiporter** (exchanger) moves two particles in **opposite directions**, as the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger does in cardiac cells. A symporter moves both in the same direction.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'In membrane transport, the term symport is used when two particles flow in _____',choices:['opposite directions','alternating directions','the same direction','random directions'],ans:2,exp:'A **symporter** (cotransporter) moves both particles in the **same direction**, as SGLT carries Na+ and glucose into the cell together. Antiporters move them in opposite directions.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'_____ is the general process of bringing material into the cell by invaginating the membrane to form a vesicle; pinocytosis and phagocytosis are specific forms of it',choices:['Endocytosis','Exocytosis','Transcytosis','Autophagy'],ans:0,exp:'**Endocytosis** includes pinocytosis (fluid), phagocytosis (large particles) and receptor-mediated endocytosis (e.g., LDL via clathrin-coated pits). Exocytosis is the reverse: vesicles fuse with the membrane to release contents outside.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'Pinocytosis is the endocytic uptake of _____ in vesicles',choices:['gases','liquids','large solids','single ions'],ans:1,exp:'**Pinocytosis** (cell drinking) takes in extracellular fluid along with whatever is dissolved in it. Phagocytosis (cell eating) is the uptake of large solid particles such as bacteria.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'Phagocytosis is the endocytic uptake of _____',choices:['dissolved ions','small gases','extracellular fluid','large solid particles'],ans:3,exp:'In **phagocytosis**, cells such as macrophages and neutrophils extend pseudopods around **large solid particles** (bacteria, debris) to form a phagosome, which fuses with a lysosome. Uptake of extracellular fluid is pinocytosis.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'_____ is the process by which a secretory vesicle fuses with the plasma membrane and releases its contents outside the cell',choices:['Pinocytosis','Endocytosis','Phagocytosis','Exocytosis'],ans:3,exp:'In **exocytosis**, vesicle fusion (driven by SNARE proteins and often triggered by Ca2+) releases contents such as neurotransmitters or hormones and adds membrane to the cell surface. Endocytosis, pinocytosis and phagocytosis all bring material in.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'The resting membrane potential is maintained mainly by the _____ and by _____',choices:['Na+/K+ ATPase / leak channels','Na+/K+ ATPase / ligand-gated channels','voltage-gated Na+ channels / leak channels','Ca2+ ATPase / gap junctions'],ans:0,exp:'The **Na+/K+ ATPase** (3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in) builds the ion gradients, and **leak channels**, mostly for K+, let K+ flow out, which makes the inside negative. Voltage-gated Na+ channels are closed at rest and open only during an action potential.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'The resting potential of most cells is between _____ and _____ mV',choices:['+40 / +80','-10 / -30','-40 / -80','-90 / -120'],ans:2,exp:'Most cells rest between about **-40 and -80 mV** (neurons about -70 mV), inside negative. This mainly reflects K+ efflux through leak channels toward the K+ equilibrium potential of about -90 mV.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'In membrane physiology, the Nernst equation calculates the _____ of a single ion from its concentrations on the two sides of the membrane',choices:['resting membrane potential','equilibrium potential','osmotic pressure','membrane conductance'],ans:1,exp:'The Nernst equation, E = (61.5 mV/z) log([ion]out/[ion]in) at body temperature, gives the **equilibrium potential** at which the electrical gradient balances one ion\'s concentration gradient. The resting potential, which reflects several ions weighted by permeability, needs the Goldman equation.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'The outer mitochondrial membrane is _____ permeable to ions and small molecules because it contains _____',choices:['poorly / porins','highly / cardiolipin','highly / porins','poorly / cardiolipin'],ans:2,exp:'The outer membrane is **highly permeable** because of large **porin** channels that pass ions, metabolites and small proteins. The inner membrane, rich in cardiolipin and lacking porins, is highly impermeable, which is what allows it to hold the proton gradient.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'The _____ mitochondrial membrane surrounds the matrix, where the citric acid cycle produces the high-energy electron carriers used in the ETC',choices:['inner','nuclear','outer','plasma'],ans:0,exp:'The **inner** membrane encloses the matrix (site of the citric acid cycle and beta oxidation), is folded into cristae, and holds the ETC complexes and ATP synthase. The intermembrane space lies between it and the outer membrane.'},
  {s:'Metabolism',q:'_____ are uniporter carriers that move glucose across the plasma membrane down its concentration gradient by facilitated diffusion',choices:['GLUT transporters','Sodium-glucose cotransporters','Aquaporins','Na+/K+ ATPases'],ans:0,exp:'**GLUT transporters** (e.g., GLUT-2 in liver and pancreas, GLUT-4 in muscle and fat) carry glucose passively. Sodium-glucose cotransporters (SGLTs) also move glucose, but by Na+-coupled secondary active transport in the intestine and kidney, so they can move it against its gradient.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glucose Transport',q:'GLUT-2, a low-affinity, high-capacity glucose transporter, is found mainly in the _____ and pancreatic beta cells',choices:['muscle','liver','brain','adipose tissue'],ans:1,exp:'**GLUT-2** has a high Km, so glucose uptake in the **liver** and beta cells rises in proportion to blood glucose, letting these tissues act as glucose sensors after a meal. Muscle and adipose tissue use insulin-responsive GLUT-4.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glucose Transport',q:'GLUT-4 is found in _____ and _____',choices:['liver / skeletal muscle','adipose tissue / skeletal muscle','adipose tissue / brain','liver / pancreatic beta cells'],ans:1,exp:'**GLUT-4** is in **adipose tissue and skeletal muscle**, where insulin moves it from intracellular vesicles to the plasma membrane, so glucose uptake in these tissues depends on insulin. Liver and beta cells use GLUT-2, and the brain relies mainly on insulin-independent GLUT-1 and GLUT-3.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'_____ is the process in which one glucose molecule is broken down to form two molecules of pyruvic acid',choices:['Gluconeogenesis','Glycolysis','Beta oxidation','The citric acid cycle'],ans:1,exp:'**Glycolysis** runs in the cytosol of all cells, needs no oxygen, and nets 2 ATP and 2 NADH per glucose. Gluconeogenesis is the reverse direction, making glucose from pyruvate and other precursors.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'_____ converts glucose to pyruvate and _____ converts pyruvate to glucose',choices:['gluconeogenesis / glycolysis','glycolysis / gluconeogenesis','glycogenolysis / gluconeogenesis','glycolysis / glycogenesis'],ans:1,exp:'**Glycolysis** breaks glucose into 2 pyruvate, and **gluconeogenesis** rebuilds glucose from pyruvate, using bypass enzymes for glycolysis\'s three irreversible steps. Glycogenesis and glycogenolysis instead make and break down glycogen.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycogen',q:'_____ converts glucose to glycogen and _____ converts glycogen to glucose',choices:['glycogenolysis / glycogenesis','glycogenesis / gluconeogenesis','glycolysis / glycogenolysis','glycogenesis / glycogenolysis'],ans:3,exp:'**Glycogenesis** (glycogen synthase and branching enzyme) builds glycogen, and **glycogenolysis** (glycogen phosphorylase and debranching enzyme) releases glucose-1-phosphate from it. Remember "-lysis" means splitting and "-genesis" means making.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Three common fates of pyruvate are conversion to _____ (alcohol fermentation in yeast), _____ (anaerobic glycolysis in animals), and _____ (aerobic oxidation by pyruvate dehydrogenase)',choices:['lactate / acetaldehyde / acetyl-CoA','acetaldehyde / lactate / oxaloacetate','acetaldehyde / lactate / acetyl-CoA','oxaloacetate / lactate / acetyl-CoA'],ans:2,exp:'Yeast decarboxylate pyruvate to **acetaldehyde** and then reduce it to ethanol; animal cells reduce pyruvate to **lactate** with lactate dehydrogenase; and PDH oxidizes it to **acetyl-CoA**. The first two regenerate NAD+ so glycolysis can continue, while carboxylation to oxaloacetate (pyruvate carboxylase) serves gluconeogenesis.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'In alcohol fermentation, pyruvate is first decarboxylated to acetaldehyde, and the next step converts acetaldehyde to _____',choices:['lactate','CO2','ethanol','acetyl-CoA'],ans:2,exp:'Alcohol dehydrogenase reduces acetaldehyde to **ethanol**, oxidizing NADH back to NAD+ so glycolysis can continue without oxygen. CO2 is released in the earlier step, when pyruvate decarboxylase converts pyruvate to acetaldehyde.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Acetyl-CoA',q:'The multienzyme complex that oxidatively decarboxylates pyruvate to acetyl-CoA in the mitochondrial matrix is the _____',choices:['pyruvate dehydrogenase complex','pyruvate kinase','pyruvate decarboxylase','pyruvate carboxylase'],ans:0,exp:'The **pyruvate dehydrogenase complex** uses thiamine pyrophosphate, lipoic acid, CoA, FAD and NAD+ to make acetyl-CoA, CO2 and NADH, and the reaction is irreversible. Pyruvate decarboxylase is the yeast enzyme that makes acetaldehyde, and pyruvate carboxylase makes oxaloacetate for gluconeogenesis.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Acetyl-CoA',q:'In the mitochondrial matrix, oxidative decarboxylation of pyruvate produces _____',choices:['acetaldehyde','oxaloacetate','acetyl-CoA','malonyl-CoA'],ans:2,exp:'Pyruvate (3 carbons) loses one carbon as CO2, and the remaining acetyl group is attached to coenzyme A as **acetyl-CoA**, which enters the citric acid cycle. Acetaldehyde comes from the non-oxidative decarboxylation in yeast fermentation.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Acetyl-CoA',q:'Two major fates of acetyl-CoA are oxidation in _____ and use as the carbon source for _____',choices:['the citric acid cycle / gluconeogenesis','glycolysis / fatty acid synthesis','the citric acid cycle / fatty acid synthesis','beta oxidation / gluconeogenesis'],ans:2,exp:'Acetyl-CoA is oxidized in **the citric acid cycle** or exported (as citrate) for **fatty acid synthesis**; it also feeds ketogenesis and cholesterol synthesis. It cannot supply net glucose because the PDH reaction is irreversible and both of its carbons are lost as CO2 in the cycle.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'The cytosolic pathway that builds palmitate from acetyl-CoA, using malonyl-CoA and NADPH, is _____',choices:['beta oxidation','ketogenesis','fatty acid synthesis','fatty acid activation'],ans:2,exp:'In **fatty acid synthesis**, acetyl-CoA carboxylase makes malonyl-CoA (the committed step), and fatty acid synthase adds 2-carbon units using NADPH until it reaches palmitate (C16). Ketogenesis also uses acetyl-CoA, but it makes ketone bodies in liver mitochondria during fasting.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'The mitochondrial process that breaks fatty acids down into acetyl-CoA is _____',choices:['lipolysis','beta oxidation','ketogenesis','fatty acid synthesis'],ans:1,exp:'**Beta oxidation** removes 2 carbons as acetyl-CoA per round, producing 1 FADH2 and 1 NADH each time. Lipolysis is the earlier step in adipose tissue that releases free fatty acids from triacylglycerols, not the breakdown of the fatty acids themselves.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Urea Cycle',q:'When amino acids are degraded, their amino groups are released as _____, which the liver converts into urea',choices:['nitrate','uric acid','creatinine','ammonia'],ans:3,exp:'Transamination and oxidative deamination release nitrogen as **ammonia** (NH3/NH4+), which is toxic, so the liver fixes it into carbamoyl phosphate and the urea cycle. Uric acid is the end product of purine breakdown, not of amino acid nitrogen.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'The NADH and FADH2 produced by the citric acid cycle deliver their electrons to the _____',choices:['electron transport chain','pentose phosphate pathway','glycolytic pathway','fatty acid synthase'],ans:0,exp:'NADH donates electrons at Complex I and FADH2 at Complex II of the **electron transport chain**, where electron flow pumps protons that drive ATP synthase. Fatty acid synthase uses NADPH (from the pentose phosphate pathway), not NADH.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'For each glucose molecule, the carbon end product of glycolysis is _____',choices:['two acetyl-CoA','one pyruvate','two pyruvate','four pyruvate'],ans:2,exp:'Glucose (6 carbons) is split into **two pyruvate** (3 carbons each), so no carbon is lost in glycolysis. Acetyl-CoA forms only afterward, when the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex removes one carbon from each pyruvate as CO2.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Glucose has _____ carbons and pyruvate has _____ carbons',choices:['6 / 2','5 / 3','6 / 3','3 / 6'],ans:2,exp:'Glucose (C6H12O6) has **6** carbons and is split into two **3**-carbon pyruvate molecules. A 2-carbon unit (the acetyl group of acetyl-CoA) appears only after pyruvate dehydrogenase releases CO2.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'The three major regulatory (irreversible) enzymes of glycolysis are _____, _____, and _____',choices:['hexokinase / PFK-1 / pyruvate carboxylase','glucose-6-phosphatase / PFK-1 / pyruvate kinase','hexokinase / aldolase / pyruvate kinase','hexokinase / PFK-1 / pyruvate kinase'],ans:3,exp:'**Hexokinase** (glucokinase in liver), **PFK-1** (the rate-limiting, committed step) and **pyruvate kinase** catalyze the three irreversible steps, which is why gluconeogenesis needs bypass enzymes. Pyruvate carboxylase and glucose-6-phosphatase are gluconeogenic bypass enzymes, and aldolase catalyzes a reversible step.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Per glucose, glycolysis consumes _____ ATP and produces _____ ATP (gross)',choices:['2 / 4','2 / 2','4 / 2','1 / 3'],ans:0,exp:'The investment phase uses **2 ATP** (hexokinase and PFK-1), and the payoff phase makes **4 ATP** by substrate-level phosphorylation (phosphoglycerate kinase and pyruvate kinase, 2 each). The net yield is therefore 2 ATP per glucose, which is why "2 / 2" is a tempting but wrong answer: 2 is the net, not the gross.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Per glucose, glycolysis consumes _____ NAD+ and produces _____ NADH',choices:['2 / 2','1 / 1','2 / 4','4 / 4'],ans:0,exp:'Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase reduces one NAD+ for each of the two triose phosphates, so glycolysis uses **2 NAD+** and makes **2 NADH** per glucose. NAD+ must be regenerated (by the ETC aerobically or by lactate dehydrogenase anaerobically) for glycolysis to continue.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Gluconeogenesis',q:'Gluconeogenesis is the process in which _____ is converted to _____',choices:['pyruvic acid / glucose','glucose / pyruvic acid','acetyl-CoA / glucose','glycogen / glucose'],ans:0,exp:'**Gluconeogenesis** builds new glucose from pyruvate and other 3-carbon precursors (lactate, glycerol, glucogenic amino acids), mainly in the liver. Acetyl-CoA cannot give net glucose because pyruvate dehydrogenase is irreversible, and glycogen to glucose is glycogenolysis.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Gluconeogenesis',q:'The two major regulated enzymes of gluconeogenesis are _____ and _____',choices:['pyruvate carboxylase / fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase','pyruvate kinase / phosphofructokinase-1','hexokinase / glucose-6-phosphatase','pyruvate dehydrogenase / citrate synthase'],ans:0,exp:'**Pyruvate carboxylase** (activated by acetyl-CoA) and **fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase** (the rate-limiting step; inhibited by AMP and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate) are the key control points. Pyruvate kinase and PFK-1 are their glycolytic counterparts, which are switched off when gluconeogenesis is on.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Gluconeogenesis',q:'In gluconeogenesis, the combination of _____ and _____ is used to bypass the irreversible pyruvate kinase step',choices:['pyruvate carboxylase / PEP carboxykinase','pyruvate dehydrogenase / citrate synthase','glucose-6-phosphatase / fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase','pyruvate kinase / lactate dehydrogenase'],ans:0,exp:'**Pyruvate carboxylase** (mitochondrial, needs biotin and ATP) turns pyruvate into oxaloacetate, and **PEP carboxykinase** (needs GTP) turns oxaloacetate into phosphoenolpyruvate. Glucose-6-phosphatase and fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase are the other two bypass enzymes, but they bypass hexokinase and PFK-1.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Acetyl-CoA',q:'The _____ is a series of reactions that completely oxidizes the acetyl group of acetyl-CoA to CO2',choices:['citric acid cycle','urea cycle','Cori cycle','glyoxylate cycle'],ans:0,exp:'The **citric acid cycle** joins acetyl-CoA to oxaloacetate and releases its two carbons as 2 CO2, capturing the energy as NADH, FADH2 and GTP. The glyoxylate cycle (plants and bacteria) uses acetyl-CoA but skips the decarboxylation steps, and humans lack it.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'In pathway order, the three regulated, irreversible enzymes of the citric acid cycle are _____, _____ and _____',choices:['citrate synthase / isocitrate dehydrogenase / α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase','aconitase / fumarase / malate dehydrogenase','citrate synthase / succinate dehydrogenase / fumarase','pyruvate dehydrogenase / aconitase / succinyl-CoA synthetase'],ans:0,exp:'**Citrate synthase**, **isocitrate dehydrogenase** (rate-limiting) and the **α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase** complex catalyze the large negative ΔG steps and are inhibited by high ATP and NADH. Pyruvate dehydrogenase feeds the cycle but is not part of it.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'The rate-limiting enzyme of the citric acid cycle is _____',choices:['aconitase','citrate synthase','isocitrate dehydrogenase','malate dehydrogenase'],ans:2,exp:'**Isocitrate dehydrogenase** oxidatively decarboxylates isocitrate to α-ketoglutarate, making the first NADH and CO2; it is activated by ADP and NAD+ and inhibited by ATP and NADH. Citrate synthase is also regulated, but it is not the rate-limiting step.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'Per turn (per acetyl-CoA), the citric acid cycle consumes _____ NAD+ and produces _____ NADH',choices:['3 / 3','6 / 6','2 / 2','4 / 4'],ans:0,exp:'Isocitrate dehydrogenase, α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase and malate dehydrogenase each reduce one NAD+, giving **3 NADH** per turn. The value 6 is per glucose (two turns), not per acetyl-CoA.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'Per turn (per acetyl-CoA), the citric acid cycle consumes _____ FAD and produces _____ FADH2',choices:['1 / 1','2 / 2','3 / 3','1 / 2'],ans:0,exp:'Only one step uses FAD: **succinate dehydrogenase** oxidizes succinate to fumarate, making **1 FADH2** per turn. Succinate dehydrogenase is also Complex II of the ETC.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'Per turn (per acetyl-CoA), the citric acid cycle consumes _____ GDP (or ADP) and produces _____ GTP (or ATP)',choices:['1 / 1','2 / 2','3 / 3','1 / 2'],ans:0,exp:'**Succinyl-CoA synthetase** uses the energy of the thioester bond to make **1 GTP** (or ATP in some tissues) per turn by substrate-level phosphorylation. Most of the cycle\'s energy is instead captured as NADH and FADH2 for the ETC.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'The citric acid cycle sends electrons to the electron transport chain within the electron carriers _____ and _____',choices:['NADH / FADH2','NADPH / FADH2','NAD+ / FAD','NADH / NADPH'],ans:0,exp:'The cycle\'s dehydrogenases load electrons onto **NADH** (to Complex I) and **FADH2** (to Complex II via ubiquinone). NADPH is a biosynthetic reductant made mainly by the pentose phosphate pathway, not by the TCA cycle, and NAD+ and FAD are the oxidized, electron-poor forms.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'The ETC creates the _____ across the inner mitochondrial membrane that is used to power ATP synthase',choices:['proton (H+) gradient','sodium (Na+) gradient','potassium (K+) gradient','calcium (Ca2+) gradient'],ans:0,exp:'Complexes I, III and IV pump **protons** into the intermembrane space, creating an electrochemical gradient (the proton-motive force). Protons flow back through ATP synthase (F0 channel) to drive ATP formation; this is chemiosmotic coupling.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'Complex I in the ETC is also known as _____',choices:["NADH dehydrogenase","Cytochrome oxidase","Succinate dehydrogenase","Cytochrome reductase"],ans:0,exp:'**NADH dehydrogenase** (NADH:ubiquinone oxidoreductase) accepts electrons from NADH via FMN and Fe-S clusters, passes them to ubiquinone and pumps 4 H+. Succinate dehydrogenase is Complex II, which takes electrons from FADH2 instead.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'Complex II in the ETC is also known as _____',choices:['cytochrome reductase','cytochrome oxidase','NADH dehydrogenase','succinate dehydrogenase'],ans:3,exp:'**Succinate dehydrogenase** oxidizes succinate to fumarate, reducing its bound FAD to FADH2 and handing the electrons to ubiquinone. It is the only enzyme that belongs to both the TCA cycle and the ETC, and it pumps no protons.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'Complex III in the ETC is also known as _____',choices:['succinate dehydrogenase','cytochrome reductase','NADH dehydrogenase','cytochrome oxidase'],ans:1,exp:'**Cytochrome reductase** (ubiquinol–cytochrome c reductase, the cytochrome bc1 complex) moves electrons from ubiquinol to cytochrome c via the Q cycle. Cytochrome oxidase is Complex IV, which passes electrons on to O2.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'Complex IV in the ETC is also known as _____',choices:['cytochrome oxidase','succinate dehydrogenase','cytochrome reductase','NADH dehydrogenase'],ans:0,exp:'**Cytochrome oxidase** (cytochrome c oxidase) takes electrons from cytochrome c and uses copper and heme a/a3 centers to reduce O2 to water. It is the target of cyanide and carbon monoxide poisoning.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'_____ uses a proton gradient to power the conversion of ADP to ATP',choices:['ATP synthase','NADH dehydrogenase','Succinate dehydrogenase','Cytochrome oxidase'],ans:0,exp:'**ATP synthase** (Complex V) lets protons flow back into the matrix through its F0 channel, and the resulting rotation drives ATP synthesis in the F1 head. The other complexes build the gradient by moving electrons; they do not phosphorylate ADP.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'Each NADH oxidized by the ETC yields about _____ ATP',choices:['2.5','1.5','1.0','3.5'],ans:0,exp:'NADH enters at Complex I, so its electrons drive proton pumping at Complexes I, III and IV (about 10 H+), giving about **2.5 ATP**. FADH2 bypasses Complex I and yields only about 1.5 ATP.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'Each FADH2 oxidized by the ETC yields about _____ ATP',choices:['1.5','2.5','1.0','0.5'],ans:0,exp:'FADH2 passes its electrons to ubiquinone through Complex II, which pumps no protons, so only Complexes III and IV pump (about 6 H+), giving about **1.5 ATP**. The value 2.5 applies to NADH, which enters at Complex I.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'Complex I in the ETC oxidizes _____ to form _____',choices:['NADH / NAD+','NAD+ / NADH','FADH2 / FAD','NADPH / NADP+'],ans:0,exp:'Complex I removes two electrons from **NADH**, regenerating **NAD+** for glycolysis and the TCA cycle, and passes the electrons to ubiquinone. Oxidizing means losing electrons, so the reduced carrier (NADH) is the substrate and NAD+ the product.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'Complex II in the ETC oxidizes _____ to form _____',choices:['succinate / fumarate','fumarate / succinate','malate / oxaloacetate','succinyl-CoA / succinate'],ans:0,exp:'Complex II is succinate dehydrogenase, which oxidizes **succinate** to **fumarate** in the TCA cycle. Malate to oxaloacetate is the malate dehydrogenase step (makes NADH), and succinyl-CoA to succinate is the substrate-level phosphorylation step.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'Complex II pumps _____ protons into the intermembrane space for each pair of electrons it transfers',choices:['0','2','4','10'],ans:0,exp:'Complex II **pumps no protons**; it only feeds electrons from succinate (via FADH2) into the ubiquinone pool. Complexes I and III each pump 4 H+ and Complex IV pumps 2 H+, which is why FADH2 yields less ATP than NADH.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'ETC Complex II removes 2 hydrogens from _____ and adds them to _____',choices:['succinate / FAD','fumarate / FAD','succinate / NAD+','malate / NAD+'],ans:0,exp:'Succinate dehydrogenase removes two hydrogens from **succinate** (giving fumarate) and transfers them to its covalently bound **FAD**, forming FADH2; the electrons then pass via Fe-S clusters to ubiquinone. NAD+ is the acceptor for the malate, isocitrate and α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenases, not for Complex II.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'Complex II is the only ETC complex that is also an enzyme of the _____',choices:['citric acid cycle','urea cycle','pentose phosphate pathway','beta oxidation pathway'],ans:0,exp:'Complex II is **succinate dehydrogenase**, which catalyzes the succinate → fumarate step of the **citric acid cycle** while embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane. Beta oxidation also makes FADH2, but its acyl-CoA dehydrogenase passes electrons to ubiquinone through a separate carrier (ETF), not Complex II.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'Complex III in the ETC is the site of the _____',choices:['Q cycle','Cori cycle','urea cycle','Calvin cycle'],ans:0,exp:'In the **Q cycle**, Complex III oxidizes ubiquinol (QH2) in two steps, passing electrons one at a time to cytochrome c and moving 4 H+ into the intermembrane space per 2 electrons. The Cori cycle shuttles lactate and glucose between muscle and liver.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'The Q cycle is the process by which electrons travel from _____ to _____',choices:['QH2 (ubiquinol) / cytochrome c','cytochrome c / O2','NADH / ubiquinone','FADH2 / cytochrome c'],ans:0,exp:'At Complex III, the two-electron carrier **QH2** hands electrons, one at a time, to the one-electron carrier **cytochrome c**, and the cycle doubles the protons released into the intermembrane space. Cytochrome c to O2 is the job of Complex IV.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'Complex IV in the ETC transfers electrons from _____ to _____',choices:['cytochrome c / O2','O2 / cytochrome c','ubiquinol / cytochrome c','NADH / O2'],ans:0,exp:'Cytochrome c oxidase takes electrons from reduced **cytochrome c** and gives them to **O2**, which picks up protons to form water; it also pumps 2 H+ per electron pair. Ubiquinol to cytochrome c is Complex III.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'_____ is the final acceptor of electrons in the ETC during aerobic respiration',choices:['Oxygen','NAD+','Ubiquinone','Cytochrome c'],ans:0,exp:'**Oxygen** has the highest reduction potential in the chain, so it accepts electrons at Complex IV and is reduced to water. Without O2, electrons back up, NADH cannot be reoxidized and oxidative phosphorylation stops; ubiquinone and cytochrome c are only intermediate carriers.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'In order, the five steps of each fatty acid synthesis cycle are 1. _____ 2. _____ 3. _____ 4. _____ 5. _____',choices:['attach / condense / reduce / dehydrate / reduce','oxidize / hydrate / oxidize / thiolyze / attach','attach / reduce / condense / reduce / dehydrate','condense / attach / dehydrate / reduce / oxidize'],ans:0,exp:'Fatty acid synthase **attaches** acetyl and malonyl groups to ACP, **condenses** them (releasing CO2), **reduces** the β-keto group with NADPH, **dehydrates** to a double bond, then **reduces** the double bond with NADPH. Mnemonic: "ACRDR"; beta oxidation runs in the opposite sense (oxidize, hydrate, oxidize, thiolyze).'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'Fatty acid synthesis begins with the transfer of _____ from the mitochondria to the cytosol via the _____',choices:['acetyl-CoA / citrate shuttle','acetyl-CoA / carnitine shuttle','malonyl-CoA / citrate shuttle','acyl-CoA / carnitine shuttle'],ans:0,exp:'**Acetyl-CoA** cannot cross the inner mitochondrial membrane, so it condenses with oxaloacetate to form citrate, which leaves via the **citrate shuttle** and is cleaved by citrate lyase in the cytosol. The carnitine shuttle moves fatty acyl groups the other way, into the matrix for beta oxidation.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'Fatty acid synthesis begins with the transfer of acetyl-CoA from the _____ to the _____ via the citrate shuttle',choices:['mitochondria / cytosol','cytosol / mitochondria','mitochondria / nucleus','smooth ER / cytosol'],ans:0,exp:'Acetyl-CoA is made in the **mitochondrial** matrix (by PDH and beta oxidation), but fatty acid synthase works in the **cytosol**, so acetyl units are exported as citrate. The reverse direction describes fatty acids entering mitochondria for beta oxidation.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'During the initiation of fatty acid synthesis, acetyl-CoA is activated through the synthesis of _____',choices:['propionyl-CoA','acetoacetyl-CoA','malonyl-CoA','succinyl-CoA'],ans:2,exp:'Acetyl-CoA carboxylase uses biotin, ATP and CO2 to make **malonyl-CoA**, the activated 2-carbon donor for fatty acid synthase. Malonyl-CoA also inhibits carnitine acyltransferase I, so new fatty acids are not immediately oxidized.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'The rate-limiting, regulated enzyme of fatty acid synthesis is _____',choices:['acetyl-CoA carboxylase','HMG-CoA reductase','fatty acid synthase','pyruvate carboxylase'],ans:0,exp:'**Acetyl-CoA carboxylase** makes malonyl-CoA; it is activated by insulin and citrate and inhibited by glucagon, epinephrine and palmitoyl-CoA. HMG-CoA reductase is the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis instead.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'In the five-step fatty acid synthesis cycle, step 1 is to _____',choices:['attach','reduce','condense','dehydrate'],ans:0,exp:'First, acetyl-CoA and malonyl-CoA are **attached** to the acyl carrier protein (ACP) of fatty acid synthase as thioesters. Condensation cannot happen until both groups are loaded.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'In the five-step fatty acid synthesis cycle, step 2 is to _____',choices:['dehydrate','condense','reduce','attach'],ans:1,exp:'In step 2 the acetyl (or growing acyl) group **condenses** with malonyl-ACP, releasing CO2 and forming a β-ketoacyl-ACP. Loss of the CO2 that acetyl-CoA carboxylase added drives this reaction forward.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'In the five-step fatty acid synthesis cycle, step 3 is to _____',choices:['dehydrate','condense','reduce','attach'],ans:2,exp:'In step 3 NADPH **reduces** the β-keto group to a β-hydroxyl group. This is the first of two NADPH-dependent reductions per cycle; the PPP supplies much of that NADPH.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'In the five-step fatty acid synthesis cycle, step 4 is to _____',choices:['hydrate','condense','reduce','dehydrate'],ans:3,exp:'In step 4 the β-hydroxyacyl-ACP is **dehydrated** (loses water) to form a trans C=C double bond. Hydration is the mirror-image step in beta oxidation, which adds water across that double bond.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'In the five-step fatty acid synthesis cycle, step 5 is to _____',choices:['dehydrate','reduce','condense','attach'],ans:1,exp:'In step 5 NADPH **reduces** the double bond to give a saturated acyl chain 2 carbons longer. The cycle repeats until palmitate (C16) is released.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'Fatty acid synthesis occurs in the _____',choices:['mitochondrial matrix','nucleus','cytosol','smooth ER'],ans:2,exp:'Fatty acid synthase is a **cytosolic** enzyme, which is why acetyl-CoA must be exported from mitochondria as citrate. Its opposite, beta oxidation, runs in the mitochondrial matrix, and the smooth ER handles chain elongation beyond C16 and desaturation.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Acetyl-CoA',q:'Acetyl-CoA uses the _____ to exit the mitochondria',choices:['glycerol-3-phosphate shuttle','carnitine shuttle','citrate shuttle','malate-aspartate shuttle'],ans:2,exp:'In the **citrate shuttle**, citrate synthase joins acetyl-CoA and oxaloacetate in the matrix, citrate is exported, and citrate lyase regenerates acetyl-CoA in the cytosol. The glycerol-3-phosphate and malate-aspartate shuttles carry NADH electrons, not acetyl groups.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'_____ is the process by which fatty acids are oxidized and broken down into 2-carbon units',choices:['Beta oxidation','Lipolysis','Ketogenesis','Lipogenesis'],ans:0,exp:'**Beta oxidation** removes 2-carbon acetyl-CoA units from fatty acyl-CoA in the mitochondrial matrix. Lipolysis is the step before it: hormone-sensitive lipase releases free fatty acids and glycerol from triacylglycerols.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Fatty Acids',q:'Beta oxidation of most fatty acids occurs in the _____',choices:['cytosol','intermembrane space','inner mitochondrial membrane','mitochondrial matrix'],ans:3,exp:'The beta oxidation enzymes are in the **mitochondrial matrix**, next to the TCA cycle that consumes its acetyl-CoA. Very-long-chain fatty acids are first shortened in peroxisomes.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Acetyl-CoA',q:'Acyl-CoA must use the _____ to pass through the inner mitochondrial membrane',choices:['citrate shuttle','carnitine shuttle','malate-aspartate shuttle','glycerol-3-phosphate shuttle'],ans:1,exp:'Carnitine acyltransferase I swaps CoA for carnitine, acylcarnitine crosses the inner membrane, and carnitine acyltransferase II restores acyl-CoA in the matrix; this is the **carnitine shuttle**. Carnitine acyltransferase I is the rate-limiting step of beta oxidation and is inhibited by malonyl-CoA.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Fatty Acids',q:'Beta oxidation starts with the oxidation of _____',choices:['propionyl-CoA','acetyl-CoA','malonyl-CoA','acyl-CoA'],ans:3,exp:'Acyl-CoA dehydrogenase oxidizes the fatty **acyl-CoA** between its α and β carbons, forming a trans double bond and FADH2. Acetyl-CoA is the product of beta oxidation, and propionyl-CoA is the 3-carbon leftover from odd-chain fatty acids.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Fatty Acids',idStem:"The first step of beta oxidation is _____", q:"The first reaction in the mitochondrial fatty-acid degradation cycle is _____",choices:['oxidation','reduction','thiolysis','hydration'],ans:0,exp:'Acyl-CoA dehydrogenase performs an **oxidation**, removing hydrogens to make a trans-Δ2 double bond and reducing FAD to FADH2. Beta oxidation never uses a reduction step; reductions belong to fatty acid synthesis.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Fatty Acids',q:'The second step of beta oxidation is _____',choices:['thiolysis','reduction','oxidation','hydration'],ans:3,exp:'Enoyl-CoA hydratase adds water across the new double bond (**hydration**), producing a β-hydroxyacyl-CoA. This reverses the dehydration step of fatty acid synthesis.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Fatty Acids',idStem:"The third step of beta oxidation is _____", q:"The third reaction in the mitochondrial fatty-acid degradation cycle is _____",choices:['oxidation','reduction','thiolysis','hydration'],ans:0,exp:'3-Hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase oxidizes the β-hydroxyl to a β-keto group (**oxidation**), reducing NAD+ to NADH. Each round therefore yields 1 FADH2 (step 1) and 1 NADH (step 3).'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Fatty Acids',q:'The fourth step of beta oxidation is _____',choices:['oxidation','reduction','hydration','thiolysis'],ans:3,exp:'β-Ketothiolase uses a new CoA-SH to split off acetyl-CoA (**thiolysis**), leaving an acyl-CoA 2 carbons shorter that re-enters the cycle. Mnemonic for the four steps: oxidize, hydrate, oxidize, thiolyze.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Fatty Acids',q:'The two main products of beta oxidation are _____ and the reduced electron carriers _____',choices:['acetyl-CoA / NADH and FADH2','malonyl-CoA / NADPH and FADH2','acetyl-CoA / NADPH and NADH','propionyl-CoA / NAD+ and FAD'],ans:0,exp:'Each round releases one **acetyl-CoA** (to the TCA cycle or ketogenesis) and makes one **NADH** and one **FADH2** (to the ETC). NADPH is consumed by fatty acid synthesis, not produced by beta oxidation.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'If a C16 fatty acid is completely processed by beta oxidation, _____ acetyl-CoA molecules will be produced',choices:['9','8','7','6'],ans:1,exp:'Palmitate has 16 carbons, and each acetyl-CoA carries 2, so complete oxidation gives 16/2 = **8 acetyl-CoA**. Only 7 rounds are needed, because the last round splits a 4-carbon unit into two acetyl-CoA, which is why 7 is the tempting wrong answer.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'A C16 fatty acid will undergo a maximum of _____ rounds of beta oxidation',choices:['6','8','7','9'],ans:2,exp:'For an even-chain fatty acid, rounds = (carbons/2) − 1 = 8 − 1 = **7**, because the final round produces two acetyl-CoA at once. Eight is the number of acetyl-CoA formed, not the number of rounds.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'A C16 fatty acid that undergoes complete beta oxidation will produce _____ NADH and _____ FADH2',choices:['7 / 7','8 / 8','7 / 8','8 / 7'],ans:0,exp:'Each of the **7 rounds** makes 1 NADH and 1 FADH2, giving 7 of each (not counting what the 8 acetyl-CoA later produce in the TCA cycle). Using 8 confuses the number of acetyl-CoA with the number of rounds.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: PPP',q:'The pentose phosphate pathway produces _____ and _____',choices:['NADPH / ribose 5-phosphate','NADH / ribose 5-phosphate','NADPH / glucose 6-phosphate','ATP / deoxyribose'],ans:0,exp:'The oxidative phase makes **NADPH** (for fatty acid and cholesterol synthesis and for keeping glutathione reduced), and the non-oxidative phase makes **ribose 5-phosphate** for nucleotides. The PPP makes NADPH, not NADH, and it makes no ATP.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: PPP',q:'The oxidative phase of the pentose phosphate pathway converts _____ to _____',choices:['NADP+ / NADPH','NADPH / NADP+','NAD+ / NADH','FAD / FADH2'],ans:0,exp:'Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (the rate-limiting enzyme) and 6-phosphogluconate dehydrogenase each reduce **NADP+** to **NADPH**, giving 2 NADPH per glucose-6-phosphate. G6PD deficiency leaves red cells short of NADPH and prone to oxidative hemolysis.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: PPP',q:'The pentose phosphate pathway produces _____, the sugar phosphate used for nucleotide synthesis',choices:['ribose 5-phosphate','glucose 6-phosphate','fructose 6-phosphate','ribulose 1,5-bisphosphate'],ans:0,exp:'**Ribose 5-phosphate** is activated to PRPP, the backbone on which purine and pyrimidine nucleotides are built. Glucose 6-phosphate is the PPP\'s starting substrate, and ribulose 1,5-bisphosphate is the CO2 acceptor of the plant Calvin cycle.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: PPP',q:'The oxidative branch of the pentose phosphate pathway begins with the glycolytic intermediate _____',choices:['glucose 6-phosphate','pyruvate','ribose 5-phosphate','fructose 1,6-bisphosphate'],ans:0,exp:'Hexokinase makes **glucose 6-phosphate**, which can enter glycolysis or be oxidized by glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase in the PPP. Ribose 5-phosphate is a PPP product, not something glycolysis supplies.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Urea Cycle',q:'The urea cycle in the liver converts toxic _____ to urea, which is then excreted in urine',choices:['ammonia','creatinine','uric acid','glutamine'],ans:0,exp:'Free **ammonia** (as carbamoyl phosphate) and the amino group of aspartate supply the two nitrogens of urea. Uric acid is the end product of purine breakdown, creatinine comes from muscle creatine, and glutamine is a non-toxic carrier that delivers ammonia to the liver.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Urea Cycle',q:'The urea cycle begins in the _____ and finishes in the _____ of _____ cells',choices:['mitochondrial matrix / cytosol / liver','cytosol / mitochondrial matrix / liver','mitochondrial matrix / cytosol / kidney','smooth ER / cytosol / liver'],ans:0,exp:'The urea cycle is split between compartments of **hepatocytes**: carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I and ornithine transcarbamoylase make carbamoyl phosphate and citrulline in the **mitochondrial matrix**, and the remaining steps (argininosuccinate synthetase, argininosuccinate lyase, arginase) run in the **cytosol**. The kidney excretes urea but does not run the full cycle.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Urea Cycle',q:'The urea cycle connects to the citric acid cycle through _____, released by argininosuccinate lyase, and _____, which donates the second nitrogen of urea',choices:['aspartate / fumarate','fumarate / aspartate','succinate / glutamate','malate / glutamate'],ans:1,exp:'Argininosuccinate lyase releases **fumarate**, which the TCA cycle converts via malate to oxaloacetate; oxaloacetate is transaminated to **aspartate**, which condenses with citrulline to bring in urea\'s second nitrogen (the "Krebs bicycle"). Glutamate supplies nitrogen indirectly (via NH₃ and transamination) but does not enter the cycle itself.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'The net equation of glycolysis is glucose + 2 NAD+ + 2 ADP + 2 Pi → _____',choices:['2 lactate + 2 NAD+ + 2 ATP + 2 H₂O','2 pyruvate + 2 NADH + 2 H+ + 4 ATP + 2 H₂O','2 pyruvate + 2 NADH + 2 H+ + 2 ATP + 2 H₂O','2 acetyl-CoA + 2 NADH + 2 CO₂ + 2 ATP'],ans:2,exp:'Glycolysis splits one glucose into **2 pyruvate**, reducing 2 NAD+ to 2 NADH and making a **net 2 ATP** (4 made in the payoff phase minus 2 spent in the investment phase). Lactate is formed only when fermentation regenerates NAD+, and acetyl-CoA plus CO₂ come from the later pyruvate dehydrogenase step.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Hexokinase is allosterically inhibited by its own product, _____',choices:['ribose 5-phosphate','glucose 6-phosphate','fructose 1,6-bisphosphate','pyruvate'],ans:1,exp:'Hexokinase phosphorylates glucose to **glucose 6-phosphate** and is inhibited by that product (feedback inhibition), so glucose uptake slows when G6P accumulates. Liver glucokinase is not inhibited by G6P, which lets the liver keep trapping glucose after a meal.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'_____ is the enzyme that catalyzes the committed, rate-limiting step of glycolysis',choices:["Pyruvate kinase (PK)","Phosphofructokinase-1 (PFK-1)","Hexokinase (HK)","Fructose-bisphosphate aldolase"],ans:1,exp:'**PFK-1** converts fructose 6-phosphate to fructose 1,6-bisphosphate; once this irreversible step occurs, the sugar is committed to glycolysis. Hexokinase also catalyzes an irreversible step, but its product (glucose 6-phosphate) can still enter glycogen synthesis or the pentose phosphate pathway.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'The storage form of glucose in animals is _____',choices:['starch','cellulose','chitin','glycogen'],ans:3,exp:'Animals store glucose as **glycogen**, a highly branched α-1,4/α-1,6 polymer found mainly in liver and skeletal muscle. Starch is the plant storage form, and cellulose (β-1,4) and chitin are structural polymers.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Phosphofructokinase-1 is allosterically inhibited by _____ and _____',choices:['AMP / citrate','ATP / citrate','ATP / fructose 2,6-bisphosphate','ADP / acetyl-CoA'],ans:1,exp:'High **ATP** (energy is plentiful) and **citrate** (TCA intermediates are abundant) inhibit PFK-1, slowing glycolysis. AMP and fructose 2,6-bisphosphate do the opposite and are its key activators.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Phosphofructokinase-1 is allosterically activated by _____',choices:['AMP','ATP','citrate','glucose 6-phosphate'],ans:0,exp:'**AMP** signals a low energy charge and activates PFK-1, speeding glycolysis to make more ATP. Fructose 2,6-bisphosphate (made by PFK-2 in response to insulin) is the other major activator, whereas ATP and citrate inhibit the enzyme.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Pyruvate kinase is allosterically inhibited by _____',choices:['ATP','AMP','ADP','fructose 1,6-bisphosphate'],ans:0,exp:'**ATP** (and alanine in the liver) inhibits pyruvate kinase, so the last step of glycolysis slows when energy is plentiful. Fructose 1,6-bisphosphate is the opposite: it is a feed-forward activator.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Pyruvate kinase is activated in a feed-forward manner by _____',choices:['fructose 6-phosphate','glucose 6-phosphate','pyruvate','fructose 1,6-bisphosphate'],ans:3,exp:'**Fructose 1,6-bisphosphate**, the product of PFK-1, activates pyruvate kinase downstream (feed-forward activation), so the end of glycolysis keeps pace with its committed step. In the liver, glucagon-driven phosphorylation inactivates pyruvate kinase during fasting.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Acetyl-CoA',q:'Pyruvate dehydrogenase converts _____ to _____',choices:['acetyl-CoA / pyruvate','pyruvate / oxaloacetate','pyruvate / acetyl-CoA','pyruvate / lactate'],ans:2,exp:'The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex irreversibly decarboxylates **pyruvate** to **acetyl-CoA** in the mitochondrial matrix, releasing CO₂ and making NADH. Pyruvate carboxylase makes oxaloacetate, and lactate dehydrogenase makes lactate.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycogen',q:'_____ is the synthesis of glycogen from glucose',choices:['Gluconeogenesis','Glycogenolysis','Glycogenesis','Glycolysis'],ans:2,exp:'**Glycogenesis** builds glycogen from UDP-glucose using glycogen synthase and branching enzyme, mainly in the fed state under insulin. Gluconeogenesis makes new glucose from non-carbohydrate precursors, and glycogenolysis breaks glycogen down.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycogen',q:'The two main enzymes of glycogenesis are _____ and _____',choices:['glycogen phosphorylase / debranching enzyme','glycogen synthase / debranching enzyme','glycogen phosphorylase / branching enzyme','glycogen synthase / branching enzyme'],ans:3,exp:'**Glycogen synthase** (rate-limiting) adds UDP-glucose units by α-1,4 bonds, and **branching enzyme** moves segments to create α-1,6 branch points. Glycogen phosphorylase and debranching enzyme are the opposing glycogenolysis enzymes.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycogen',q:'Glycogen synthase creates _____ between glucose residues',choices:['β-1,4 glycosidic bonds','α-1,4 glycosidic bonds','α-1,6 glycosidic bonds','β-1,6 glycosidic bonds'],ans:1,exp:'Glycogen synthase extends linear chains by forming **α-1,4 glycosidic bonds** from UDP-glucose. The α-1,6 bonds at branch points are made by branching enzyme, and β linkages are found in cellulose and lactose, not glycogen.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycogen',q:'Branching enzyme creates glycogen branch points with _____',choices:['α-1,4 glycosidic bonds','β-1,6 glycosidic bonds','α-1,6 glycosidic bonds','β-1,4 glycosidic bonds'],ans:2,exp:'Branching enzyme transfers a short α-1,4-linked segment and reattaches it by an **α-1,6 glycosidic bond**. Branching makes glycogen more soluble and creates many nonreducing ends for fast synthesis and breakdown.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycogen',q:'_____ is the breakdown of glycogen',choices:['Glycogenesis','Glycogenolysis','Gluconeogenesis','Glycolysis'],ans:1,exp:'**Glycogenolysis** releases glucose 1-phosphate from glycogen and is stimulated by glucagon and epinephrine. Glycogenesis is the opposite (synthesis), and gluconeogenesis makes glucose from non-carbohydrate precursors.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycogen',q:'The two main enzymes of glycogenolysis are _____ and _____',choices:['glycogen synthase / debranching enzyme','glycogen phosphorylase / debranching enzyme','glycogen phosphorylase / branching enzyme','glycogen synthase / branching enzyme'],ans:1,exp:'**Glycogen phosphorylase** (rate-limiting) cleaves α-1,4 bonds by phosphorolysis to release glucose 1-phosphate, and **debranching enzyme** moves short oligosaccharides and hydrolyzes the α-1,6 branch points. Glycogen synthase and branching enzyme belong to glycogenesis.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycogen',q:'In glycogenolysis, glycogen phosphorylase releases glucose 1-phosphate by breaking _____',choices:['β-1,6 glycosidic bonds','α-1,4 glycosidic bonds','α-1,6 glycosidic bonds','β-1,4 glycosidic bonds'],ans:1,exp:'Glycogen phosphorylase uses inorganic phosphate (phosphorolysis, not hydrolysis) to cleave **α-1,4 glycosidic bonds** from the nonreducing ends. It stops about four residues from a branch point, where debranching enzyme takes over the α-1,6 bond.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycogen',q:'In glycogenolysis, debranching enzyme removes the last glucose at a branch point by hydrolyzing _____',choices:['α-1,4 glycosidic bonds','α-1,6 glycosidic bonds','β-1,6 glycosidic bonds','β-1,4 glycosidic bonds'],ans:1,exp:'Debranching enzyme first moves a block of three residues to a nearby chain (transferase activity), then hydrolyzes the remaining **α-1,6 glycosidic bond**, releasing free glucose. Glycogen phosphorylase handles the α-1,4 bonds.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycogen',q:'The glycolysis intermediate _____ feeds into glycogenesis',choices:['pyruvate','ribose 5-phosphate','fructose 6-phosphate','glucose 6-phosphate'],ans:3,exp:'**Glucose 6-phosphate** is a branch point: phosphoglucomutase converts it to glucose 1-phosphate, which becomes UDP-glucose for glycogen synthase. G6P can also enter glycolysis or the pentose phosphate pathway.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Gluconeogenesis',q:'_____ is the process in which pyruvate is converted to glucose',choices:['Glycolysis','Glycogenolysis','Gluconeogenesis','Glycogenesis'],ans:2,exp:'**Gluconeogenesis** rebuilds glucose from pyruvate and other precursors (lactate, glycerol, glucogenic amino acids), using bypass enzymes for the three irreversible steps of glycolysis. Glycogenolysis also raises blood glucose but only by releasing stored glycogen.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Gluconeogenesis',q:'Gluconeogenesis occurs mainly in the _____, with smaller contributions from the _____ and _____',choices:['skeletal muscle / kidney / intestine','liver / kidney / intestine','liver / skeletal muscle / kidney','liver / kidney / adipose tissue'],ans:1,exp:'The **liver** performs most gluconeogenesis, with the **kidney** cortex (especially in prolonged fasting) and **intestinal** epithelium contributing. Skeletal muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase, so it cannot release free glucose into the blood.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Gluconeogenesis',q:'Because the pyruvate kinase step is irreversible, in gluconeogenesis pyruvate must first be converted to _____ before becoming phosphoenolpyruvate',choices:['citrate','lactate','oxaloacetate','acetyl-CoA'],ans:2,exp:'Pyruvate carboxylase (mitochondrial, biotin-dependent, activated by acetyl-CoA) makes **oxaloacetate**, and PEPCK then converts it to phosphoenolpyruvate using GTP. Acetyl-CoA cannot be used for net glucose synthesis because the PDH step is irreversible.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Gluconeogenesis',q:'_____ maintains blood glucose by synthesizing new glucose during fasting, starvation, low-carbohydrate diets, or intense exercise',choices:['Gluconeogenesis','Glycogenolysis','Glycogenesis','Glycolysis'],ans:0,exp:'**Gluconeogenesis** makes new glucose, driven by glucagon and cortisol when carbohydrate intake is low. Glycogenolysis also raises blood glucose early in a fast, but it only releases stored glucose, and liver glycogen runs out within about a day.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: PPP',q:'The pentose phosphate pathway is also known as the _____',choices:['Cori cycle','glyoxylate cycle','Embden-Meyerhof pathway','hexose monophosphate shunt'],ans:3,exp:'The PPP is called the **hexose monophosphate (HMP) shunt** because it diverts glucose 6-phosphate from glycolysis to make NADPH and ribose 5-phosphate. The Embden-Meyerhof pathway is glycolysis, and the Cori cycle shuttles lactate and glucose between muscle and liver.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: PPP',q:'The rate-limiting enzyme of the pentose phosphate pathway is _____',choices:["6-phosphogluconate dehydrogenase","Ribulose-5-phosphate epimerase","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase","Transketolase (TPP-dependent enzyme)"],ans:2,exp:"**Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD)** catalyzes the first oxidative step of the pentose phosphate pathway and controls its flux through the NADP+/NADPH balance. 6-Phosphogluconate dehydrogenase catalyzes a later oxidative step. Ribulose-5-phosphate epimerase and the TPP-dependent transketolase act in the non-oxidative phase."},
  {s:'Metabolism: PPP',q:'The pentose phosphate pathway occurs in the _____',choices:['smooth ER','mitochondrial matrix','cytosol','nucleus'],ans:2,exp:'The PPP runs in the **cytosol**, alongside glycolysis and fatty acid synthesis, which is where its NADPH is used for biosynthesis and glutathione reduction. Mitochondrial pathways include the TCA cycle and beta oxidation.'},
  {s:'Metabolism',q:'Glycolysis, the pentose phosphate pathway, glycogenolysis, and fatty acid synthesis occur in the _____; pyruvate oxidation, the citric acid cycle, and beta oxidation occur in the _____',choices:['mitochondrial matrix / cytosol','cytosol / mitochondrial matrix','cytosol / intermembrane space','smooth ER / mitochondrial matrix'],ans:1,exp:'Glycolysis, the PPP, glycogen metabolism, and fatty acid synthesis are **cytosolic**; pyruvate dehydrogenase, the TCA cycle, and beta oxidation run in the **mitochondrial matrix**. Oxidative phosphorylation is on the inner mitochondrial membrane, and the intermembrane space only holds the pumped protons.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Monosaccharides',q:'Dietary galactose comes mainly from digestion of _____',choices:['lactose','sucrose','maltose','trehalose'],ans:0,exp:'Lactase splits **lactose** (milk sugar) into galactose + glucose. Sucrose yields glucose + fructose, while maltose and trehalose each yield two glucose.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Monosaccharides',q:'Dietary fructose comes from fruit, honey, and digestion of _____',choices:['lactose','maltose','trehalose','sucrose'],ans:3,exp:'Sucrase splits **sucrose** (table sugar) into glucose + fructose, and fructose is also found free in fruit and honey. Lactose yields galactose + glucose, while maltose and trehalose yield only glucose.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Monosaccharides',q:'Which monosaccharide is the primary fuel for most cells and is released by the breakdown of glycogen and starch?',choices:['Fructose','Galactose','Glucose','Ribose'],ans:2,exp:'**Glucose** (C₆H₁₂O₆), a D-aldohexose, is the principal metabolic fuel and the only monomer of glycogen and starch. Amylase digests starch, and glycogenolysis releases glucose 1-phosphate that the liver converts to free glucose.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Monosaccharides',q:'Which monosaccharide is found in honey and fruit, bypasses PFK-1 regulation in the liver, and is a ketohexose?',choices:['Glucose','Galactose','Ribose','Fructose'],ans:3,exp:'**Fructose** is a ketohexose. In the liver, fructokinase makes fructose 1-phosphate, which aldolase B splits into trioses that enter glycolysis below PFK-1, bypassing its regulation. Glucose and galactose are aldoses.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Monosaccharides',q:'Which monosaccharide is derived from lactose in milk and enters glycolysis via the Leloir pathway?',choices:['Fructose','Glucose','Mannose','Galactose'],ans:3,exp:'**Galactose**, the C-4 epimer of glucose, is converted to galactose 1-phosphate and then (via GALT and UDP-glucose) to glucose 1-phosphate in the Leloir pathway. GALT deficiency causes classic galactosemia.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Acetyl-CoA',q:'Acetyl-CoA contains a high-energy _____ bond that can be used to drive other reactions',choices:['ester','amide','anhydride','thioester'],ans:3,exp:'The acetyl group is attached to the sulfur of coenzyme A by a **thioester** bond, whose hydrolysis is highly exergonic (about −31 kJ/mol). Thioesters are less resonance-stabilized than oxygen esters, which makes them good acyl donors.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Acetyl-CoA',q:'Acetyl-CoA can be formed from _____, _____, and _____',choices:['pyruvate / fatty acids / cholesterol','pyruvate / urea / ketogenic amino acids','pyruvate / fatty acids / ketogenic amino acids','carbon dioxide / fatty acids / ketogenic amino acids'],ans:2,exp:'Acetyl-CoA comes from **pyruvate** (carbohydrates, via PDH), **fatty acids** (beta oxidation), and **ketogenic amino acids** such as leucine and lysine. Cholesterol is made from acetyl-CoA but cannot be broken back down to it in humans.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Acetyl-CoA',q:'As the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex converts pyruvate into acetyl-CoA, it releases _____ and reduces NAD+ to _____',choices:['H₂O / NADH','CO₂ / FADH₂','CO₂ / NADH','O₂ / NADPH'],ans:2,exp:'PDH (E1 with thiamine pyrophosphate, E2 with lipoate and CoA, E3 with FAD) catalyzes pyruvate + CoA + NAD+ → acetyl-CoA + **CO₂** + **NADH**. FAD on E3 is only an intermediate electron carrier; the electrons leave as NADH.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Acetyl-CoA',q:'The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex is turned off when it is _____',choices:['phosphorylated','dephosphorylated','acetylated','ubiquitinated'],ans:0,exp:'**PDH kinase**, activated by ATP, NADH, and acetyl-CoA, phosphorylates and inactivates PDH when energy is plentiful. PDH phosphatase (stimulated by insulin and Ca²⁺) removes the phosphate to turn it back on.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Acetyl-CoA',q:'The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex is turned on when it is _____',choices:['phosphorylated','acetylated','dephosphorylated','ubiquitinated'],ans:2,exp:'**PDH phosphatase** removes the inhibitory phosphate, activating PDH; insulin in the fed state and Ca²⁺ in contracting muscle stimulate it. Phosphorylation by PDH kinase does the opposite, switching the complex off.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'Per acetyl-CoA, one turn of the citric acid cycle produces _____ NADH, _____ FADH₂, _____ GTP, and _____ CO₂',choices:['2 / 2 / 1 / 2','3 / 1 / 2 / 2','3 / 1 / 1 / 2','4 / 1 / 1 / 3'],ans:2,exp:'Each turn yields **3 NADH** (isocitrate DH, α-ketoglutarate DH, malate DH), **1 FADH₂** (succinate DH), **1 GTP** (succinyl-CoA synthetase), and **2 CO₂**. Double these per glucose, since one glucose gives two acetyl-CoA.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'Isocitrate dehydrogenase, the rate-limiting enzyme of the citric acid cycle, is activated by _____ and inhibited by _____',choices:['ATP / ADP','ADP / ATP','NADH / NAD+','ATP / NAD+'],ans:1,exp:'Isocitrate dehydrogenase senses energy charge: **ADP** (and NAD+, Ca²⁺) signals low energy and activates it, while **ATP** (and NADH) signals high energy and inhibits it. Reversing the pair is the common mistake.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'The citric acid cycle is also known as the _____ or the _____',choices:['Cori cycle / tricarboxylic acid cycle','Krebs cycle / glyoxylate cycle','Calvin cycle / Cori cycle','Krebs cycle / tricarboxylic acid cycle'],ans:3,exp:'It is called the **Krebs cycle** after Hans Krebs and the **tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle** because citrate has three carboxyl groups. The Cori cycle links muscle lactate to liver gluconeogenesis, and the Calvin cycle fixes CO₂ in plants.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'Isocitrate dehydrogenase, the rate-limiting enzyme of the citric acid cycle, is activated by _____ or _____',choices:['ADP / NAD+','ATP / NAD+','ADP / NADH','GTP / NADH'],ans:0,exp:'**ADP** and **NAD+** accumulate when energy is low and activate isocitrate dehydrogenase, speeding the cycle to make more NADH for the ETC. ATP and NADH, their high-energy counterparts, are inhibitors.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'Isocitrate dehydrogenase, the rate-limiting enzyme of the citric acid cycle, is inhibited by _____ or _____',choices:['ADP / NADH','ATP / NAD+','ATP / NADH','ADP / NAD+'],ans:2,exp:'**ATP** and **NADH** signal high energy and inhibit isocitrate dehydrogenase, slowing the cycle when energy is plentiful. ADP and NAD+ are activators.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'The citric acid cycle occurs in the _____ in eukaryotes and in the _____ in prokaryotes',choices:['cytosol / mitochondrial matrix','mitochondrial matrix / cytosol','inner mitochondrial membrane / plasma membrane','intermembrane space / cytosol'],ans:1,exp:'Eukaryotes run the TCA cycle in the **mitochondrial matrix** (only succinate dehydrogenase is embedded in the inner membrane). Prokaryotes have no mitochondria, so the cycle runs in the **cytosol** and their ETC sits in the plasma membrane.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'_____ is a series of complexes that transfer electrons from electron donors to electron acceptors via redox reactions',choices:['Electron transport chain','Citric acid cycle','Malate-aspartate shuttle','Glycolysis'],ans:0,exp:'The **electron transport chain** (Complexes I–IV with ubiquinone and cytochrome c) passes electrons from NADH and FADH₂ to O₂, using the energy to pump protons. The malate-aspartate shuttle only moves reducing equivalents across the inner membrane.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'_____ is the movement of protons down their electrochemical gradient across a membrane, coupled to ATP synthesis by ATP synthase',choices:['Osmosis','Substrate-level phosphorylation','Chemiosmosis','Active transport'],ans:2,exp:'In **chemiosmosis** (Mitchell), protons pumped by the ETC flow back through ATP synthase (F₀F₁), and that flow drives ATP synthesis. Substrate-level phosphorylation makes ATP directly from a high-energy substrate, without a gradient.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'The _____ is the proton electrochemical gradient generated by the electron transport chain across the inner mitochondrial membrane',choices:['proton-motive force','redox potential','resting membrane potential','osmotic pressure'],ans:0,exp:'The **proton-motive force** combines the pH gradient (ΔpH) and the electrical potential (Δψ) across the inner membrane, and it powers ATP synthase. Redox potential describes a molecule\'s tendency to gain electrons, not a proton gradient.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'A _____ sense RNA strand has a nucleotide sequence complementary to the mRNA that it encodes',choices:['positive','negative','ambisense','coding'],ans:1,exp:'**Negative-sense** (antisense) RNA is complementary to mRNA, so it cannot be translated directly; the virus must carry an RNA-dependent RNA polymerase to make + strand mRNA (e.g., influenza, rabies). Positive-sense RNA, like the coding strand of DNA, has the same sequence as mRNA.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'At a pH well above all of its pKa values, an amino acid is _____',choices:['fully deprotonated','fully protonated','zwitterionic','positively charged'],ans:0,exp:'When pH > pKa, a group loses its proton, so at very high pH every ionizable group is **fully deprotonated** (–COO⁻ and –NH₂), giving a net negative charge. Low pH gives the fully protonated cation, and the zwitterion dominates near the pI.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'When pH = pI, an amino acid exists predominantly in the _____ form',choices:['cationic','anionic','un-ionized','zwitterionic'],ans:3,exp:'At the isoelectric point the net charge is zero because the molecule is a **zwitterion** (–NH₃⁺ and –COO⁻), so it does not migrate in an electric field. The un-ionized form with no charges at all is negligible in water at any pH.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'The amino acid sequence of a peptide chain is written from the _____-terminus to the _____-terminus',choices:['C / N','N / C','5′ / 3′','3′ / 5′'],ans:1,exp:'Peptides are written and synthesized from the free amino (**N**) terminus to the free carboxyl (**C**) terminus, matching the 5′→3′ reading of the mRNA. The 5′/3′ labels apply to nucleic acids, not peptides.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'At physiological pH, the N-terminus of a peptide chain carries a _____ charge',choices:['negative','partial positive','partial negative','positive'],ans:3,exp:'The terminal amino group has a pKa of about 9, so at pH 7.4 it is protonated (–NH₃⁺) and carries a full **positive** charge. The C-terminal carboxyl (pKa about 2) is deprotonated and negative.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'The 1° structure of proteins is stabilized by _____',choices:['hydrogen bonds','disulfide bonds','ionic bonds','peptide bonds'],ans:3,exp:'Primary structure is the amino acid sequence, held together by covalent **peptide bonds** between residues. Disulfide bonds, ionic bonds, and side-chain hydrogen bonds stabilize higher-order (mainly tertiary) structure.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'The 2° structure of proteins is stabilized by _____',choices:['hydrophobic interactions','disulfide bonds','hydrogen bonds','peptide bonds'],ans:2,exp:'α-Helices and β-sheets are held together by **hydrogen bonds** between backbone carbonyl oxygens and amide hydrogens. Hydrophobic interactions and disulfide bonds involve side chains and shape tertiary structure.'},
  {s:'Proteins',q:'The 3° structure of proteins is driven primarily by _____',choices:['hydrogen bonds','disulfide bonds','ionic bonds','hydrophobic interactions'],ans:3,exp:'Burying nonpolar side chains away from water (**hydrophobic interactions**) raises solvent entropy and is the main driving force for folding. Disulfide bonds, salt bridges, and hydrogen bonds also stabilize tertiary structure but contribute less overall.'},
  {s:'Enzymes',q:'Enzymes _____ the ΔG or ΔH of a reaction, or its final equilibrium position',choices:['lower','raise','do not alter','reverse the sign of'],ans:2,exp:'Enzymes lower the activation energy and speed both forward and reverse reactions equally, so they **do not alter** ΔG, ΔH, or K_eq; they only help equilibrium arrive faster. Lowering the activation energy is often confused with lowering ΔG.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'Triacylglycerols contain a glycerol attached to 3 fatty acids by _____ bonds',choices:['phosphodiester','ester','glycosidic','peptide'],ans:1,exp:'Each glycerol –OH condenses with a fatty acid carboxyl group to form an **ester** bond, releasing water. Phosphodiester bonds link nucleotides, glycosidic bonds link sugars, and peptide bonds link amino acids.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'_____ is a mixture of the sodium or potassium salts of naturally occurring fatty acids',choices:['Wax','Detergent','Soap','Lecithin'],ans:2,exp:'**Soap** forms by saponification, the base hydrolysis of triacylglycerols, giving amphipathic fatty acid carboxylate salts that form micelles. Detergents are synthetic surfactants (e.g., sulfonates), and waxes are esters of fatty acids with long-chain alcohols.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'DNA polymerase reads the template strand in the _____ direction and synthesizes the new strand in the _____ direction',choices:['3\'→5\' / 5\'→3\'','5\'→3\' / 3\'→5\'','5\'→3\' / 5\'→3\'','3\'→5\' / 3\'→5\''],ans:0,exp:'DNA polymerase can add nucleotides only to a free 3\'-OH, so the new strand always grows **5\'→3\'**. Because the strands are antiparallel, the template is therefore read **3\'→5\'**; this is why the lagging strand must be made discontinuously as Okazaki fragments.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'In eukaryotic translation initiation, the 40S ribosomal subunit binds the _____ of the mRNA and then scans for the start codon',choices:['TATA box','5\' cap','Shine-Dalgarno sequence','poly-A tail'],ans:1,exp:'The eukaryotic 40S subunit, with initiation factors, binds the **7-methylguanosine 5\' cap** and scans downstream to the first AUG, often within a Kozak sequence. The Shine-Dalgarno sequence is the prokaryotic ribosome-binding site, and the TATA box is a promoter element used in transcription, not translation.'},
  {s:'Cell Membrane',q:'During osmosis, water moves from an area of _____ solute concentration to an area of _____ solute concentration',choices:['high / low','low / high','high / high','low / low'],ans:1,exp:'Water moves down its own concentration gradient: from **low solute** (high water) concentration to **high solute** (low water) concentration. In other words, water flows toward the hypertonic side, which is why cells in a hypertonic solution shrink.'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'Polysaccharides are built from monosaccharides joined by repeated _____ bonds',choices:['ester','peptide','glycosidic','phosphodiester'],ans:2,exp:'A **glycosidic bond** forms by dehydration between the anomeric carbon of one sugar and an -OH of another, as in the alpha-1,4 links of starch and the beta-1,4 links of cellulose. Peptide bonds join amino acids and phosphodiester bonds join nucleotides.'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'_____ is the main structural polysaccharide of plant cell walls',choices:['Chitin','Starch','Glycogen','Cellulose'],ans:3,exp:'**Cellulose** is a linear polymer of glucose joined by beta-1,4 glycosidic bonds; the straight chains hydrogen-bond into strong fibers, and humans lack an enzyme to digest them. Chitin is the structural polysaccharide of fungal cell walls and arthropod exoskeletons, not plants.'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates',q:'In plants, glucose is stored for energy mainly as _____',choices:['starch','glycogen','cellulose','chitin'],ans:0,exp:'Plants store glucose as **starch** (amylose plus branched amylopectin, both alpha-linked), while animals store it as the more highly branched glycogen. Cellulose is also a plant glucose polymer, but its beta-1,4 links make it structural rather than a fuel store.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA: Transcription',q:'The 5\' cap of eukaryotic mRNA is made of _____, and the 3\' poly-A tail is made of _____',choices:['7-methyladenosine / adenine nucleotides','7-methylguanosine / uracil nucleotides','7-methylguanosine / adenine nucleotides','7-methyladenosine / guanine nucleotides'],ans:2,exp:'The cap is a **7-methylguanosine** joined to the mRNA by an unusual 5\'-5\' triphosphate bridge, and the tail is a string of about 200 **adenine nucleotides** added by poly-A polymerase. Both protect the transcript from exonucleases and help with nuclear export and translation.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'The bulk of chemical lipid digestion occurs in the _____ due to pancreatic lipase',choices:['stomach','mouth','small intestine','large intestine'],ans:2,exp:'Pancreatic lipase is secreted into the duodenum, so most triacylglycerol hydrolysis happens in the **small intestine**, after bile salts emulsify the fat. Lingual and gastric lipases start a small amount of digestion in the mouth and stomach.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'When fat enters the duodenum, it undergoes _____, the mixing of two normally immiscible liquids (here, fat and water) into small droplets',choices:['saponification','emulsification','esterification','hydrolysis'],ans:1,exp:'**Emulsification** breaks large fat globules into small droplets, greatly increasing the surface area on which lipase can act. It is a physical change, unlike hydrolysis or saponification, which break ester bonds chemically.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'Emulsification of dietary lipids in the small intestine is aided mainly by _____',choices:['bile salts','pancreatic lipase','gastric acid','cholecystokinin'],ans:0,exp:'**Bile salts**, made from cholesterol in the liver and stored in the gallbladder, are amphipathic and coat fat droplets to emulsify them. Cholecystokinin only triggers gallbladder contraction, and pancreatic lipase digests the fat after it has been emulsified.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'In intestinal cells, absorbed long-chain fatty acids are re-esterified to triacylglycerols and packaged into _____ for release into the lymphatic system',choices:['VLDL','HDL','LDL','chylomicrons'],ans:3,exp:'Enterocytes package dietary triacylglycerols, cholesterol and apolipoprotein B-48 into **chylomicrons**, which are too large for capillaries and enter lacteals instead. VLDL is the similar particle made by the liver for endogenous triacylglycerols.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'Newly made chylomicrons leave the intestine by first entering the _____',choices:['hepatic portal vein','lymphatic system','intestinal capillaries','bile ducts'],ans:1,exp:'Chylomicrons enter lacteals and travel through the **lymphatic system** to the thoracic duct, which empties into the left subclavian vein, so dietary fat bypasses the liver at first. Monosaccharides, amino acids and short-chain fatty acids instead enter capillaries and the hepatic portal vein.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'_____ are particles of lipid and protein that transport triacylglycerols and cholesterol through the blood and lymph',choices:['Micelles','Glycoproteins','Lipoproteins','Proteoglycans'],ans:2,exp:'**Lipoproteins** have a hydrophobic core of triacylglycerols and cholesterol esters wrapped in a shell of phospholipids, cholesterol and apolipoproteins. Micelles are bile-salt aggregates that ferry lipids only within the intestinal lumen.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'Lipoproteins are named and classified by their overall _____',choices:['size','density','charge','apolipoprotein content'],ans:1,exp:'Lipoproteins are classed by **density**: the more lipid relative to protein, the lower the density. Chylomicrons are the least dense, followed by VLDL, IDL and LDL, while protein-rich HDL is the densest.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'Low-density lipoproteins (LDL) are considered _____ cholesterol because they _____',choices:['good / deliver cholesterol to tissues, including artery walls','bad / deliver cholesterol to tissues, including artery walls','bad / carry excess cholesterol back to the liver','good / carry excess cholesterol back to the liver'],ans:1,exp:'LDL delivers cholesterol to peripheral tissues, and when LDL levels are high the excess deposits in arterial walls and drives atherosclerosis, hence **bad** cholesterol. Carrying excess cholesterol back to the liver is the job of HDL, the good cholesterol.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'High-density lipoproteins (HDL) are considered _____ cholesterol because they _____',choices:['good / carry excess cholesterol back to the liver','bad / carry excess cholesterol back to the liver','good / deliver cholesterol to tissues, including artery walls','bad / deliver cholesterol to tissues, including artery walls'],ans:0,exp:'HDL performs **reverse cholesterol transport**, picking up excess cholesterol from tissues and vessel walls and returning it to the liver for excretion in bile, hence **good** cholesterol. Delivering cholesterol to tissues is the role of LDL.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'The main function of very-low-density lipoproteins (VLDL) is to carry newly made triacylglycerols from _____ to _____',choices:['the small intestine / peripheral tissues','peripheral tissues / the liver','the liver / peripheral tissues','the small intestine / the liver'],ans:2,exp:'VLDL is assembled in **the liver** and carries endogenously made triacylglycerols to **peripheral tissues** such as muscle and adipose. Carrying dietary fat from the small intestine to tissues is the job of chylomicrons.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'As lipoprotein lipase removes triacylglycerols, VLDL is converted first to _____ and then to _____',choices:['LDL / IDL','IDL / LDL','IDL / HDL','chylomicron remnants / LDL'],ans:1,exp:'Losing triacylglycerol makes the particle smaller and denser: VLDL becomes **intermediate-density lipoprotein (IDL)** and then cholesterol-rich **LDL**. HDL is made separately by the liver and intestine, and chylomicron remnants come from chylomicrons, not VLDL.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'The main function of low-density lipoproteins (LDL) is to _____',choices:['return excess cholesterol to the liver','carry dietary triacylglycerols from the intestine','carry liver-made triacylglycerols to tissues','deliver cholesterol to peripheral tissues'],ans:3,exp:'LDL is the main **cholesterol delivery** particle; cells take it up by receptor-mediated endocytosis through the LDL receptor. Returning cholesterol to the liver is HDL, carrying dietary fat is chylomicrons, and carrying liver-made triacylglycerols is VLDL.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'_____ are the protein components of lipoproteins that bind cell-surface receptors, activate enzymes such as lipoprotein lipase, and control lipoprotein interactions',choices:['Apolipoproteins','Albumins','Scavenger receptors','Lipoprotein lipases'],ans:0,exp:'**Apolipoproteins** (e.g., apoB-100 binds the LDL receptor, apoC-II activates lipoprotein lipase, apoA-I activates LCAT on HDL) give each lipoprotein its identity and targeting. Lipoprotein lipase is the endothelial enzyme they activate, not a structural part of the particle.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'Cholesterol may be obtained from the diet or through _____ in the liver',choices:['de novo synthesis','beta oxidation','ketogenesis','gluconeogenesis'],ans:0,exp:'The liver makes cholesterol by **de novo synthesis** from acetyl-CoA via HMG-CoA and mevalonate, using NADPH and ATP. Beta oxidation and ketogenesis also occur in the liver but break down or reuse fat carbons rather than build sterols.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'_____ is the rate-limiting enzyme of the mevalonate pathway, which produces cholesterol',choices:['HMG-CoA synthase','Acetyl-CoA carboxylase','Squalene synthase','HMG-CoA reductase'],ans:3,exp:'**HMG-CoA reductase** converts HMG-CoA to mevalonate in the smooth ER; it is the main control point and the target of statin drugs. HMG-CoA synthase forms HMG-CoA (and in mitochondria serves ketogenesis), and acetyl-CoA carboxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of fatty acid synthesis.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'The omega end of a fatty acid is the _____ end',choices:['carboxyl','methyl','hydroxyl','ester'],ans:1,exp:'Omega is the last Greek letter, so the omega carbon is the terminal **methyl** carbon, farthest from the carboxyl group. The carboxyl carbon is C-1, the start of the delta (Δ) numbering system.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'Omega fatty acids (e.g., omega-3, omega-6) are classified according to the _____',choices:['total number of double bonds','position of the first double bond from the carboxyl end','total number of carbons','position of the first double bond from the methyl end'],ans:3,exp:'In omega-n naming, n is the **position of the first double bond counted from the methyl (omega) end**; for example, alpha-linolenic acid is omega-3. Counting from the carboxyl end is the separate delta (Δ) system.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'Ketogenesis turns acetyl-CoA into acetoacetate, which can then be reduced to _____ or spontaneously decarboxylated to acetone',choices:['lactate','malonate','3-hydroxybutyrate','propionate'],ans:2,exp:'The three ketone bodies are acetoacetate, acetone and **3-hydroxybutyrate** (beta-hydroxybutyrate), which forms when acetoacetate is reduced by NADH. Strictly, 3-hydroxybutyrate is not a ketone, but it is counted as a ketone body; lactate is the product of pyruvate reduction, not ketogenesis.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'During prolonged starvation, ketone bodies form in the liver because of excess _____',choices:['acetyl-CoA','oxaloacetate','pyruvate','malonyl-CoA'],ans:0,exp:'Beta oxidation floods liver mitochondria with **acetyl-CoA**, while oxaloacetate is drawn off for gluconeogenesis, so acetyl-CoA cannot enter the citric acid cycle and is diverted to ketone bodies. Oxaloacetate is the tempting distractor, but it is depleted, not in excess.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'_____ is the breakdown of ketone bodies in peripheral tissues such as brain and muscle to regenerate acetyl-CoA for the citric acid cycle',choices:['Ketogenesis','Lipolysis','Glycogenolysis','Ketolysis'],ans:3,exp:'In **ketolysis**, 3-hydroxybutyrate is oxidized to acetoacetate, which is activated by succinyl-CoA acetoacetyl-CoA transferase (thiophorase) and split into two acetyl-CoA. The liver lacks thiophorase, so it makes ketone bodies (ketogenesis) but cannot use them.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'_____ are made from acetyl-CoA, mainly in the mitochondrial matrix of liver cells, when carbohydrate is so scarce that energy must come from fatty acid breakdown',choices:['Ketone bodies','Fatty acids','Glucose molecules','Lactate molecules'],ans:0,exp:'When glucose is scarce, hepatic acetyl-CoA from beta oxidation is converted to **ketone bodies** (acetoacetate, 3-hydroxybutyrate, acetone), which are exported as water-soluble fuel. Glucose is also made in fasting, but by gluconeogenesis, and never from acetyl-CoA.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'_____ occurs when insulin deficiency causes very high blood glucose together with a dangerous buildup of acidic ketone bodies',choices:['Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state','Diabetic ketoacidosis','Lactic acidosis','Alcoholic ketoacidosis'],ans:1,exp:'**Diabetic ketoacidosis** (DKA), typical of type 1 diabetes, combines hyperglycemia with an anion-gap metabolic acidosis from unchecked lipolysis and ketogenesis. Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (type 2) has even higher glucose but little ketosis, and alcoholic ketoacidosis has ketosis with low or normal glucose.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'During prolonged starvation, the brain can derive up to about two-thirds of its energy from _____',choices:['fatty acids','ketone bodies','glycogen','amino acids'],ans:1,exp:'After several days of fasting, the brain adapts to use **ketone bodies**, which cuts its glucose need and spares muscle protein. Fatty acids are the tempting answer, but the brain oxidizes very little fatty acid for fuel.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'Large-scale breakdown of cellular proteins for fuel occurs mainly during _____',choices:['the absorptive state','an overnight fast','prolonged starvation','moderate exercise'],ans:2,exp:'Proteins are always turning over, but net breakdown of functional and structural protein for fuel happens mainly in **prolonged starvation**, once glycogen is gone; the amino acids supply gluconeogenesis. In shorter fasts the body relies on glycogen and fat, sparing protein.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'_____ are amino acids whose carbon skeletons can be converted into glucose through gluconeogenesis',choices:['Ketogenic amino acids','Glucogenic amino acids','Essential amino acids','Branched-chain amino acids'],ans:1,exp:'**Glucogenic amino acids** are broken down to pyruvate or citric acid cycle intermediates (e.g., oxaloacetate, alpha-ketoglutarate), which can be made into glucose. All standard amino acids except leucine and lysine are at least partly glucogenic.'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'_____ are amino acids whose carbon skeletons can be converted into acetyl-CoA or acetoacetyl-CoA and therefore into ketone bodies',choices:['Branched-chain amino acids','Glucogenic amino acids','Essential amino acids','Ketogenic amino acids'],ans:3,exp:'**Ketogenic amino acids** yield acetyl-CoA or acetoacetyl-CoA, which cannot be used for net glucose synthesis. Leucine and lysine are purely ketogenic; isoleucine, phenylalanine, tyrosine, tryptophan and threonine are both ketogenic and glucogenic.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'Under standard biochemical conditions, hydrolysis of ATP\'s terminal phosphoanhydride bond releases about _____ kJ/mol of free energy',choices:['7.3','15','30','60'],ans:2,exp:'The standard free energy of ATP hydrolysis to ADP + Pi is about **-30 kJ/mol** (-30.5 kJ/mol), which is the same as -7.3 kcal/mol; in cells it is closer to -50 kJ/mol. The 7.3 value is a common unit trap, since it is in kcal, not kJ.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'_____ are electron-carrier proteins with a tightly bound flavin nucleotide (FAD or FMN) as their prosthetic group',choices:['Cytochromes','Iron-sulfur proteins','Flavoproteins','Quinones'],ans:2,exp:'**Flavoproteins** carry FAD or FMN, which accept and donate electrons; examples are NADH dehydrogenase (FMN, Complex I) and succinate dehydrogenase (FAD, Complex II). Cytochromes carry heme groups instead, and ubiquinone is a lipid-soluble carrier, not a protein.'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'FAD and FMN, the prosthetic groups of flavoproteins, are both derived from _____',choices:['niacin (vitamin B3)','riboflavin (vitamin B2)','thiamine (vitamin B1)','pantothenic acid (vitamin B5)'],ans:1,exp:'FAD and FMN are flavin nucleotides made from **riboflavin (vitamin B2)**; they are coenzymes, not proteins themselves. Niacin is the precursor of NAD+ and NADP+, thiamine of TPP, and pantothenic acid of coenzyme A.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'_____ means occurring in the period after eating a meal',choices:['Postabsorptive','Preprandial','Postprandial','Fasting'],ans:2,exp:'**Postprandial** (post = after, prandium = meal) describes the period right after eating, which matches the absorptive or fed state, when insulin is high. Postabsorptive refers to the later period after nutrients have been absorbed, as in an overnight fast.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'The _____ follows a meal: the gastrointestinal tract is full, insulin is high, and anabolic processes dominate',choices:['postabsorptive state','absorptive state','prolonged fasting state','cephalic phase'],ans:1,exp:'In the **absorptive (fed) state**, high insulin drives glycogen, fat and protein synthesis while nutrients are being absorbed. The postabsorptive state follows once absorption is complete and glucagon begins to rise.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'The _____ begins after digestion and absorption are complete (e.g., overnight), when the body relies mainly on liver glycogen breakdown to maintain blood glucose',choices:['absorptive state','prolonged fasting state','postabsorptive state','cephalic phase'],ans:2,exp:'In the **postabsorptive (fasting) state**, glucagon and epinephrine stimulate glycogenolysis, along with some gluconeogenesis and lipolysis. In prolonged fasting, liver glycogen is exhausted, so gluconeogenesis and ketone bodies take over.'},
  {s:'Metabolism',q:'Direct calorimeters measure the _____, which can be used to calculate metabolic rate',choices:['oxygen consumed by the organism','carbon dioxide produced by the organism','heat produced by the organism','ATP produced by the organism'],ans:2,exp:'Nearly all the energy released by metabolism ends up as heat, so **direct calorimetry** measures the **heat produced** to find metabolic rate. Measuring oxygen consumed and carbon dioxide produced is indirect calorimetry, which also gives the respiratory quotient.'},
  {s:'Metabolism',q:'The _____ is the ratio of the volume of carbon dioxide produced to the volume of oxygen consumed',choices:['basal metabolic rate','respiratory quotient','P/O ratio','ventilation-perfusion ratio'],ans:1,exp:'**Respiratory quotient (RQ) = CO2 produced / O2 consumed**. It reveals the fuel being burned: about 1.0 for carbohydrate, 0.8 for protein and 0.7 for fat. The P/O ratio instead counts ATP made per oxygen atom reduced.'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Regulation',q:'_____ is a hypothalamic hormone that increases appetite and promotes wakefulness; its loss causes narcolepsy',choices:['Ghrelin','Leptin','Melatonin','Orexin'],ans:3,exp:'**Orexin** (hypocretin) is made in the lateral hypothalamus and both increases food-seeking and maintains alertness, so loss of orexin neurons causes narcolepsy. Ghrelin also increases appetite but comes from the stomach, and leptin from adipose tissue decreases appetite.'},
  {s:'Lipids',q:'Triacylglycerol stored in _____ is the principal energy reserve in mammals',choices:['skeletal muscle','adipose tissue','the liver','plasma lipoproteins'],ans:1,exp:'**Adipose tissue** holds most of the body\'s stored energy as triacylglycerol, which yields about 9 kcal/g and is stored without water. Liver and muscle glycogen give a much smaller, faster-access reserve.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'Gel electrophoresis separates molecules such as DNA, RNA and proteins according to their _____ and _____',choices:['size / charge','size / polarity','solubility / charge','hydrophobicity / polarity'],ans:0,exp:'In an electric field, molecules migrate toward the electrode opposite their **charge**, and the gel matrix slows larger molecules, separating them by **size**. Because nucleic acids have a uniform charge-to-mass ratio, they separate almost purely by size.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'In gel electrophoresis, small molecules move _____ through the gel and large molecules move _____',choices:['slower / faster','faster / at the same rate','at the same rate / faster','faster / slower'],ans:3,exp:'Small molecules slip through the gel pores easily and travel **faster** and farther, while large molecules are held back and move **slower**. So the smallest bands end up closest to the positive electrode for DNA.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'In gel electrophoresis, _____ gels have large pores and are used for nucleic acids, while _____ gels have small pores and are better for proteins',choices:['polyacrylamide / agarose','agarose / polyacrylamide','agarose / cellulose','dextran / polyacrylamide'],ans:1,exp:'**Agarose** gels have large pores suited to large DNA and RNA fragments, while **polyacrylamide** gels have smaller, tunable pores suited to proteins (as in SDS-PAGE) and very small DNA fragments. Dextran beads are used in size-exclusion chromatography columns rather than electrophoresis gels.'},
  {s:'Lab Techniques',q:'Native PAGE is a polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis method that separates proteins under _____ conditions',choices:['denaturing','reducing','non-denaturing','denaturing and reducing'],ans:2,exp:'Native PAGE uses **non-denaturing** conditions (no SDS, no reducing agent), so proteins keep their folded shape and activity and separate by charge, size and shape together. SDS-PAGE, by contrast, denatures proteins so they separate by mass alone.'},
  {s:'Lab Techniques',q:'_____ denatures proteins and coats them with a uniform negative charge',choices:['Urea','SDS','Guanidinium chloride','2-Mercaptoethanol'],ans:1,exp:'**SDS** (sodium dodecyl sulfate) is an anionic detergent that unfolds proteins and binds in proportion to their length, masking their native charge so they separate by mass. Urea and guanidinium chloride denature without adding charge, and 2-mercaptoethanol only reduces disulfide bonds.'},
  {s:'Lab Techniques',q:'SDS-PAGE denatures proteins and separates them based mainly on _____',choices:['net charge','isoelectric point','mass','charge and native shape'],ans:2,exp:'Because SDS gives every protein the same charge-to-mass ratio and destroys its shape, migration depends on **mass** (polypeptide length). Separation by charge and native shape is what native PAGE does, and separation by isoelectric point is isoelectric focusing.'},
  {s:'Lab Techniques',q:'In SDS-PAGE, the reducing agent _____ can be added to break disulfide bonds and denature the protein further',choices:['urea','2-mercaptoethanol','SDS','guanidinium chloride'],ans:1,exp:'**2-Mercaptoethanol** (beta-mercaptoethanol), like DTT, reduces disulfide bridges to free -SH groups, separating covalently linked subunits. Urea, guanidinium chloride and SDS disrupt noncovalent interactions but do not reduce disulfides.'},
  {s:'Lab Techniques',q:'In isoelectric focusing, the gel has a pH gradient and proteins migrate until they reach the pH that matches their _____',choices:['molecular weight','isoelectric point','net charge at pH 7','hydrodynamic radius'],ans:1,exp:'A protein stops migrating at its **isoelectric point (pI)**, where its net charge is zero. Below its pI a protein is positive and moves toward the cathode; above its pI it is negative and moves toward the anode.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'_____ is a method of transferring proteins, DNA, or RNA that have been separated by electrophoresis onto a nitrocellulose or PVDF membrane for probing',choices:['Sanger sequencing','PCR','Blotting','Gel electrophoresis'],ans:2,exp:'**Blotting** moves the separated molecules out of the fragile gel onto a membrane in the same pattern, where a labeled probe or antibody can detect a specific target. Gel electrophoresis is the separation step that comes before the transfer.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'_____ is used to detect a specific DNA sequence',choices:['Northern blotting','Southern blotting','Eastern blotting','Western blotting'],ans:1,exp:'**Southern blotting** uses a labeled complementary probe to detect a DNA sequence. Mnemonic SNOW DROP: Southern = DNA, Northern = RNA, Western = Protein.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'_____ is used to detect a specific RNA sequence',choices:['Western blotting','Eastern blotting','Northern blotting','Southern blotting'],ans:2,exp:'**Northern blotting** detects RNA, often to measure how much a gene is expressed. Mnemonic SNOW DROP: Southern = DNA, Northern = RNA, Western = Protein.'},
  {s:'Lab Techniques',q:'_____ is used to detect a specific protein in a sample',choices:['Southern blotting','Western blotting','Northern blotting','Eastern blotting'],ans:1,exp:'**Western blotting** separates proteins by SDS-PAGE, transfers them to a membrane, and detects the target with a specific antibody. Eastern blotting is a less common variant used to study post-translational modifications.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'_____ is a method of DNA sequencing based on the selective incorporation of chain-terminating dideoxynucleotides',choices:['Maxam-Gilbert sequencing','Pyrosequencing','Nanopore sequencing','Sanger sequencing'],ans:3,exp:'In **Sanger sequencing**, ddNTPs lack a 3\'-OH, so each one stops strand extension; the resulting fragments are sorted by length to read the sequence. Maxam-Gilbert sequencing instead cleaves DNA chemically at specific bases.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'_____ is a fast, cell-free technique that makes many copies of a specific target region of DNA through repeated heating and cooling cycles',choices:['Southern blotting','Polymerase chain reaction','Sanger sequencing','In situ hybridization'],ans:1,exp:'The **polymerase chain reaction (PCR)** uses two primers flanking the target and a heat-stable polymerase, doubling the target each cycle (about 2^n copies after n cycles). Southern blotting and in situ hybridization detect sequences but do not amplify them.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'PCR relies on a thermostable DNA polymerase called _____',choices:['Klenow fragment','T4 DNA polymerase','Taq polymerase','Phi29 polymerase'],ans:2,exp:'**Taq polymerase** comes from Thermus aquaticus, a hot-spring bacterium, so it survives the repeated denaturation steps near 95°C. The Klenow fragment and T4 polymerase come from mesophiles and would be destroyed by the heat.'},
  {s:'DNA & RNA',q:'In order, the three steps of a PCR cycle and their approximate temperatures are _____, then _____, then _____',choices:['annealing at ~95°C / denaturation at ~55°C / extension at ~72°C','denaturation at ~95°C / extension at ~55°C / annealing at ~72°C','denaturation at ~95°C / annealing at ~55°C / extension at ~72°C','denaturation at ~72°C / annealing at ~37°C / extension at ~95°C'],ans:2,exp:'**Denaturation** (about 94–98°C) separates the strands, **annealing** (about 50–65°C) lets the primers bind, and **extension** (about 72°C, the optimum for Taq polymerase) builds the new strands. Annealing must be cooler than extension so the primers can hydrogen-bond stably first.'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Glycine has pKa values of 2.34 (α-carboxyl) and 9.60 (α-amino) and no ionizable side chain. What is its isoelectric point?',choices:['7.26','5.97','4.80','3.63'],ans:1,exp:'With no ionizable side chain, pI = (pKa of α-carboxyl + pKa of α-amino)/2 = (2.34 + 9.60)/2 = **5.97**, the pH midway between the two pKa values, where the zwitterion dominates. The same formula applies to any amino acid with a neutral side chain; 7.26 is the difference between the pKa values, not their average.',src:'milesdown:1554074519575'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Lysine has pKa values of 2.18 (α-carboxyl), 8.95 (α-amino) and 10.53 (side-chain amino). What is its isoelectric point?',choices:['6.36','7.22','9.74','5.57'],ans:2,exp:'For a basic amino acid, the neutral species exists between the two highest pKa values, so pI = (8.95 + 10.53)/2 = **9.74**, the average of the α-amino and side-chain pKa values. Averaging all three pKa values (7.22) is a common error; basic amino acids always have a pI above 7.',src:'milesdown:1554074541271'},
  {s:'Amino Acids',q:'Aspartate has pKa values of 1.88 (α-carboxyl), 3.65 (side-chain carboxyl) and 9.60 (α-amino). What is its isoelectric point?',choices:['2.77','5.74','6.63','5.04'],ans:0,exp:'For an acidic amino acid, net charge is zero between the two lowest pKa values, so pI = (1.88 + 3.65)/2 = **2.77**, the average of the α-carboxyl and side-chain carboxyl pKa values. 5.74 ignores the side chain as if aspartate were neutral; acidic amino acids always have a pI well below 7.',src:'milesdown:1554074557717'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Kinetics',q:'Which expression is the Michaelis-Menten equation for the initial reaction velocity v₀?',choices:['v₀ = Vmax[S]/(Km + [S])','v₀ = Vmax·Km/(Km + [S])','v₀ = Km[S]/(Vmax + [S])','v₀ = Vmax[S]/(Km − [S])'],ans:0,exp:'Here v₀ is the initial velocity, Vmax the maximum velocity, [S] the substrate concentration and Km the [S] that gives half of Vmax. Sense check: when [S] = Km, v₀ = Vmax/2, and when [S] ≫ Km, v₀ approaches Vmax; putting Km in the numerator would make velocity fall as substrate rises.',src:'milesdown:1554089291465'},
  {s:'Enzymes: Kinetics',q:'In the Michaelis-Menten scheme E + S ⇌ ES → E + P (forward constant k₁, reverse constant k₋₁, then k₂), which rate constant describes the usually rate-limiting catalytic step that converts the complex to product?',choices:['k₁','k₋₁','k₂','k₁/k₋₁'],ans:2,exp:'**k₂** (also called kcat) governs ES → E + P, the slow step that releases product and regenerates free enzyme, so Vmax = k₂[E]total. k₁ and k₋₁ describe the fast, reversible formation and dissociation of ES, and k₁/k₋₁ is a binding constant, not a rate of catalysis.',src:'milesdown:1554094859157'},
  {s:'Biosignaling',idStem:"Epinephrine binds a G protein-coupled receptor on liver cells, activating adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and activating protein kinase A to promote glycogen breakdown. In this pathway, the first messenger is _____", q:"A circulating catecholamine binds a G protein-coupled receptor on liver cells, activating adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and activating protein kinase A to promote glycogen breakdown. Which listed component is the first messenger?",choices:['cAMP','epinephrine','protein kinase A','the Gs α subunit'],ans:1,exp:'The **first messenger** is the extracellular signal that binds the receptor, here **epinephrine**, which cannot cross the membrane. cAMP, made from ATP by adenylyl cyclase, is the second messenger that carries the signal inside the cell to PKA.',src:'milesdown:1554128547783'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Which enzyme catalyzes the first step of glycolysis in muscle and most other tissues, phosphorylating glucose to glucose 6-phosphate?',choices:['Glucose 6-phosphatase','Phosphoglucomutase','Hexokinase','Phosphoglucose isomerase'],ans:2,exp:'**Hexokinase** transfers a phosphate from ATP to C-6 of glucose, trapping it in the cell because charged glucose 6-phosphate cannot leave through GLUT transporters (liver uses the isozyme glucokinase). Glucose 6-phosphatase catalyzes the reverse reaction in gluconeogenesis, and phosphoglucomutase interconverts glucose 1-phosphate and glucose 6-phosphate.',src:'milesdown:1556595608094'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'In the hexokinase reaction, glucose + _____ → glucose 6-phosphate + _____',choices:['Pi / H₂O','ATP / ADP','ADP / ATP','GTP / GDP'],ans:1,exp:'Hexokinase is a kinase, so it moves the terminal phosphate of **ATP** onto glucose and leaves **ADP**; this is the first of glycolysis\'s two ATP-investment steps (PFK-1 is the second). Joining free Pi to glucose with loss of water is not something kinases do.',src:'milesdown:1556597012755'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Which glycolytic enzyme converts glucose 6-phosphate (an aldose) into fructose 6-phosphate (a ketose)?',choices:['Triose phosphate isomerase','Phosphoglucose isomerase','Phosphoglucomutase','Aldolase'],ans:1,exp:'**Phosphoglucose isomerase** (glucose-6-phosphate isomerase) shifts the carbonyl from C-1 to C-2, making a ketose that PFK-1 can phosphorylate at C-1 and aldolase can later split into two trioses. Triose phosphate isomerase works on the 3-carbon sugars instead, and phosphoglucomutase moves a phosphate between C-1 and C-6.',src:'milesdown:1556595678551'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'In the phosphofructokinase-1 reaction, fructose 6-phosphate + _____ → fructose 1,6-bisphosphate + _____',choices:['H₂O / Pi','ADP / ATP','ATP / ADP','NAD+ / NADH'],ans:2,exp:'PFK-1 spends **ATP**, releasing **ADP**, to phosphorylate C-1 of fructose 6-phosphate; this committed step is the second ATP-consuming step of glycolysis. Water and Pi belong to the opposite direction in gluconeogenesis, where fructose 1,6-bisphosphatase hydrolyzes off the C-1 phosphate.',src:'milesdown:1556597547054'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Which glycolytic enzyme cleaves fructose 1,6-bisphosphate into two three-carbon sugar phosphates?',choices:['Aldolase','Triose phosphate isomerase','Fructose 1,6-bisphosphatase','Phosphofructokinase-1'],ans:0,exp:'**Aldolase** catalyzes a reverse aldol reaction (it is a lyase), splitting the 6-carbon sugar between C-3 and C-4. Fructose 1,6-bisphosphatase acts on the same substrate in gluconeogenesis but only removes the C-1 phosphate, leaving a six-carbon sugar.',src:'milesdown:1556595779230'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Aldolase cleaves fructose 1,6-bisphosphate into which two products?',choices:['Two molecules of glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate','Dihydroxyacetone phosphate and glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate','Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate and 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate','Dihydroxyacetone phosphate and glycerol 3-phosphate'],ans:1,exp:'Aldolase yields one **dihydroxyacetone phosphate** (DHAP, a ketose) and one **glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate** (G3P, an aldose). Only G3P continues down glycolysis, so triose phosphate isomerase converts DHAP to G3P; each glucose ultimately gives two G3P, but aldolase itself does not make two.',src:'milesdown:1556595825462'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Which enzyme interconverts dihydroxyacetone phosphate and glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate in glycolysis?',choices:['Aldolase','Phosphoglucose isomerase','Glycerol 3-phosphate dehydrogenase','Triose phosphate isomerase'],ans:3,exp:'**Triose phosphate isomerase** converts DHAP (a ketose) into G3P (an aldose), so both halves of glucose proceed through the payoff phase. Glycerol 3-phosphate dehydrogenase also uses DHAP, but reduces it to glycerol 3-phosphate for lipid synthesis or the glycerol phosphate shuttle.',src:'milesdown:1556596049847'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Which glycolytic enzyme oxidizes glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate and adds inorganic phosphate to form 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate?',choices:['Phosphoglycerate kinase','Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate dehydrogenase','Triose phosphate isomerase','Phosphoglycerate mutase'],ans:1,exp:'**Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate dehydrogenase** catalyzes the only oxidation in glycolysis: it oxidizes the aldehyde and captures the energy in a high-energy acyl phosphate. Phosphoglycerate kinase acts next, transferring that phosphate to ADP.',src:'milesdown:1556595862320'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'The glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate dehydrogenase reaction requires _____ and produces _____ (besides 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate)',choices:['NAD+ and Pi / NADH','NAD+ and ATP / NADH','NADH and Pi / NAD+','FAD and Pi / FADH₂'],ans:0,exp:'GAPDH uses **inorganic phosphate (Pi)**, not ATP, to phosphorylate its substrate and reduces **NAD+** to **NADH**; this is why glycolysis stops when NAD+ is not regenerated by the ETC or by lactate dehydrogenase. Picking ATP as the phosphate donor confuses this step with the kinase steps.',src:'milesdown:1556597863887'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Which glycolytic enzyme converts 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate to 3-phosphoglycerate?',choices:['Phosphoglycerate mutase','Pyruvate kinase','Phosphoglycerate kinase','Enolase'],ans:2,exp:'**Phosphoglycerate kinase** transfers the high-energy C-1 phosphate to ADP, the first ATP-producing step of glycolysis; kinases are named for the reverse direction, so it runs "backward" here. Phosphoglycerate mutase acts next, moving the phosphate from C-3 to C-2.',src:'milesdown:1556595908675'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'In the phosphoglycerate kinase reaction, 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate + _____ → 3-phosphoglycerate + _____',choices:['ATP / ADP','ADP / ATP','NAD+ / NADH','GDP / GTP'],ans:1,exp:'Phosphoglycerate kinase makes **ATP** from **ADP** by **substrate-level phosphorylation**, the direct transfer of a phosphate from a high-energy substrate; it runs twice per glucose, paying back the 2 ATP invested earlier. GTP is made by substrate-level phosphorylation in the citric acid cycle (succinyl-CoA synthetase), not in glycolysis.',src:'milesdown:1556597569744'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Which glycolytic enzyme moves the phosphate of 3-phosphoglycerate from C-3 to C-2?',choices:['Phosphoglycerate mutase','Phosphoglucomutase','Phosphoglycerate kinase','Enolase'],ans:0,exp:'A **mutase** is an isomerase that shifts a functional group within one molecule; **phosphoglycerate mutase** produces 2-phosphoglycerate, setting up the dehydration by enolase. Phosphoglucomutase does a similar job for glucose 1-phosphate and glucose 6-phosphate in glycogen metabolism.',src:'milesdown:1556595950746'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Which glycolytic enzyme converts 2-phosphoglycerate into phosphoenolpyruvate?',choices:['Pyruvate kinase','Phosphoglycerate mutase','Enolase','PEP carboxykinase'],ans:2,exp:'**Enolase** dehydrates 2-phosphoglycerate, creating the enol phosphate of PEP, which has a very high phosphoryl-transfer potential. PEP carboxykinase also makes PEP, but from oxaloacetate during gluconeogenesis. Fluoride inhibits enolase, which is why fluoride tubes preserve blood glucose samples.',src:'milesdown:1556595983138'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'In glycolysis, the enolase reaction converting 2-phosphoglycerate to phosphoenolpyruvate releases _____',choices:['CO₂','Pi','H₂O','NADH'],ans:2,exp:'Enolase catalyzes a **dehydration**: removing **H₂O** forms the C=C double bond of the enol, raising the phosphate\'s transfer potential so pyruvate kinase can make ATP. No carbon is lost (so no CO₂) and no redox occurs (so no NADH) at this step.',src:'milesdown:1556597959409'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'Which enzyme catalyzes the final, irreversible step of glycolysis, converting phosphoenolpyruvate to pyruvate?',choices:['Pyruvate carboxylase','PEP carboxykinase','Pyruvate dehydrogenase','Pyruvate kinase'],ans:3,exp:'**Pyruvate kinase** transfers PEP\'s phosphate to ADP; the enol form of pyruvate then tautomerizes to the keto form, making the step strongly exergonic. Gluconeogenesis bypasses it with pyruvate carboxylase plus PEP carboxykinase, and pyruvate dehydrogenase acts afterward to make acetyl-CoA.',src:'milesdown:1556596013406'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Glycolysis',q:'In the pyruvate kinase reaction, phosphoenolpyruvate + _____ → pyruvate + _____',choices:['ADP / ATP','ATP / ADP','GDP / GTP','Pi / ATP'],ans:0,exp:'Pyruvate kinase makes **ATP** from **ADP** by substrate-level phosphorylation, the second ATP-producing step of glycolysis; together with phosphoglycerate kinase it gives 4 ATP gross and 2 net per glucose. Free Pi is not used, because the phosphate comes from PEP itself.',src:'milesdown:1556597641771'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Gluconeogenesis',q:'In gluconeogenesis, enolase runs in reverse, converting phosphoenolpyruvate to 2-phosphoglycerate by adding _____',choices:['Pi','H₂O','CO₂','ATP'],ans:1,exp:'Enolase is reversible, so in gluconeogenesis it **hydrates** PEP, adding **H₂O** across the double bond to give 2-phosphoglycerate. Only the hexokinase, PFK-1 and pyruvate kinase steps need bypass enzymes; adding CO₂ is the job of pyruvate carboxylase, earlier in the pathway.',src:'milesdown:1556644640648'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Gluconeogenesis',q:'In gluconeogenesis, which enzyme hydrolyzes fructose 1,6-bisphosphate to fructose 6-phosphate?',choices:['Fructose 1,6-bisphosphatase','Phosphofructokinase-1','Aldolase','Glucose 6-phosphatase'],ans:0,exp:'**Fructose 1,6-bisphosphatase** removes the C-1 phosphate by hydrolysis, releasing Pi and bypassing the irreversible PFK-1 step; it is the rate-limiting enzyme of gluconeogenesis and is inhibited by AMP and fructose 2,6-bisphosphate. PFK-1 cannot simply run backward because its reaction is too exergonic.',src:'milesdown:1556644847505'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Gluconeogenesis',q:'Which enzyme catalyzes the final step of gluconeogenesis, hydrolyzing glucose 6-phosphate to free glucose?',choices:['Hexokinase','Glucokinase','Glucose 6-phosphatase','Phosphoglucomutase'],ans:2,exp:'**Glucose 6-phosphatase**, in the ER of liver and kidney cells, removes the phosphate so free glucose can leave the cell and enter the blood. Muscle lacks this enzyme and cannot release glucose to the blood; hexokinase and glucokinase catalyze the opposite, phosphorylating reaction.',src:'milesdown:1556647257519'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Gluconeogenesis',q:'Pyruvate carboxylase, the first enzyme of gluconeogenesis from pyruvate, is allosterically activated by _____',choices:['acetyl-CoA','AMP','fructose 2,6-bisphosphate','ADP'],ans:0,exp:'High **acetyl-CoA** (for example from fatty acid β-oxidation during fasting) activates pyruvate carboxylase and inhibits pyruvate dehydrogenase, steering pyruvate toward oxaloacetate and glucose. AMP and fructose 2,6-bisphosphate instead favor glycolysis and inhibit fructose 1,6-bisphosphatase.',src:'milesdown:1556648136981'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'Which enzyme catalyzes the first step of the citric acid cycle, condensing acetyl-CoA with oxaloacetate?',choices:['Aconitase','Citrate synthase','Pyruvate carboxylase','Malate dehydrogenase'],ans:1,exp:'**Citrate synthase** joins the 2-carbon acetyl group to 4-carbon oxaloacetate, giving 6-carbon citrate; hydrolysis of the thioester drives the reaction forward. Pyruvate carboxylase replenishes oxaloacetate but does not use acetyl-CoA as a substrate.',src:'milesdown:1556660285108'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'The citrate synthase reaction combines oxaloacetate and acetyl-CoA (with H₂O) to produce _____ and _____',choices:['citrate / CoA','isocitrate / CoA','citrate / acetate','succinyl-CoA / CO₂'],ans:0,exp:'The acetyl group is added to oxaloacetate, giving **citrate**, and the thioester is hydrolyzed, releasing free **coenzyme A** to be reused by pyruvate dehydrogenase or β-oxidation. Isocitrate forms only in the next step, when aconitase isomerizes citrate.',src:'milesdown:1556661825545'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'Which citric acid cycle enzyme isomerizes citrate to isocitrate by moving its hydroxyl group to a neighboring carbon?',choices:['Isocitrate dehydrogenase','Citrate synthase','Aconitase','Fumarase'],ans:2,exp:'**Aconitase**, an iron-sulfur enzyme, dehydrates and then rehydrates citrate via cis-aconitate, moving the OH from a tertiary carbon to a secondary carbon that can be oxidized next. Fluoroacetate is toxic because it is converted to fluorocitrate, which inhibits aconitase.',src:'milesdown:1556661597434'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'Which citric acid cycle enzyme converts isocitrate to α-ketoglutarate?',choices:['α-Ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex','Aconitase','Isocitrate dehydrogenase','Succinyl-CoA synthetase'],ans:2,exp:'**Isocitrate dehydrogenase** catalyzes the first oxidative decarboxylation of the cycle and is its rate-limiting step. The α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex catalyzes the second oxidative decarboxylation, acting on α-ketoglutarate.',src:'milesdown:1556661632745'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'Isocitrate dehydrogenase uses isocitrate and NAD+ and produces _____, _____ and _____',choices:['α-ketoglutarate / NADH / CO₂','succinyl-CoA / NADH / CO₂','α-ketoglutarate / FADH₂ / CO₂','oxaloacetate / NADH / H₂O'],ans:0,exp:'This oxidative decarboxylation releases the cycle\'s first **CO₂** and first **NADH**, leaving 5-carbon **α-ketoglutarate**. Succinyl-CoA comes from the next step, which also yields NADH and CO₂ but additionally needs CoA.',src:'milesdown:1556661893912'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'Which citric acid cycle enzyme converts α-ketoglutarate to succinyl-CoA?',choices:['Succinyl-CoA synthetase','α-Ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex','Isocitrate dehydrogenase','Succinate dehydrogenase'],ans:1,exp:'The **α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex** works like pyruvate dehydrogenase, with the same cofactors (thiamine pyrophosphate, lipoic acid, CoA, FAD, NAD+), and is inhibited by its products succinyl-CoA and NADH. Succinyl-CoA synthetase acts on its product in the next step.',src:'milesdown:1556661670803'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'The α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex uses α-ketoglutarate, NAD+ and _____, and produces succinyl-CoA, NADH and _____',choices:['CoA / CO₂','FAD / CO₂','CoA / H₂O','GDP / CO₂'],ans:0,exp:'**Coenzyme A** accepts the 4-carbon succinyl group as a high-energy thioester, and the lost carboxyl leaves as **CO₂**, the second and last CO₂ of each turn. FAD is a bound cofactor of the complex but is not consumed, since its electrons pass on to NAD+.',src:'milesdown:1556661931848'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'Which citric acid cycle enzyme converts succinyl-CoA to succinate?',choices:['Succinate dehydrogenase','Succinyl-CoA synthetase','α-Ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex','Fumarase'],ans:1,exp:'**Succinyl-CoA synthetase** (succinate thiokinase) is named for the reverse reaction; in the cycle it cleaves the thioester and uses the energy to make GTP (or ATP), the cycle\'s only substrate-level phosphorylation. Succinate dehydrogenase acts next, oxidizing succinate to fumarate.',src:'milesdown:1556661702632'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'Succinyl-CoA synthetase uses succinyl-CoA, GDP and Pi and produces _____, _____ and _____',choices:['succinate / GTP / CoA','fumarate / GTP / CoA','succinate / GTP / acetyl-CoA','fumarate / GDP / CoA'],ans:0,exp:'Cleaving the thioester of succinyl-CoA releases **succinate** and free **CoA**, and the energy drives phosphorylation of GDP to **GTP** (ATP in some tissues). Fumarate forms only in the next step, by succinate dehydrogenase.',src:'milesdown:1556661960820'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'Which citric acid cycle enzyme converts fumarate to malate?',choices:['Malate dehydrogenase','Succinate dehydrogenase','Fumarase','Aconitase'],ans:2,exp:'**Fumarase** (fumarate hydratase) adds water across the trans double bond of fumarate, giving L-malate. Malate dehydrogenase acts next, oxidizing malate to oxaloacetate with NAD+.',src:'milesdown:1556661768812'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'In the citric acid cycle, the conversion of fumarate to malate requires _____',choices:['NAD+','H₂O','FAD','CoA'],ans:1,exp:'Fumarase catalyzes a **hydration**, adding **H₂O** across the C=C bond, so no redox occurs at this step. The oxidations on either side use FAD (succinate dehydrogenase, before) and NAD+ (malate dehydrogenase, after).',src:'milesdown:1556662038881'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'Which enzyme catalyzes the final step of the citric acid cycle, regenerating oxaloacetate?',choices:['Citrate synthase','Malate dehydrogenase','Fumarase','Pyruvate carboxylase'],ans:1,exp:'**Malate dehydrogenase** oxidizes malate to oxaloacetate, ready to condense with the next acetyl-CoA; the step is uphill (ΔG°′ ≈ +30 kJ/mol) and is pulled forward because citrate synthase quickly consumes oxaloacetate. Pyruvate carboxylase also makes oxaloacetate, but from pyruvate as an anaplerotic reaction.',src:'milesdown:1556661794558'},
  {s:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle',q:'Malate dehydrogenase uses malate and NAD+ and produces _____, _____ and _____',choices:['oxaloacetate / NADH / H+','fumarate / NADH / H+','oxaloacetate / FADH₂ / H₂O','citrate / NADH / CO₂'],ans:0,exp:'Oxidizing malate\'s hydroxyl to a ketone gives **oxaloacetate**, and the removed hydrogens become **NADH** + **H+**, the third NADH of each turn. FADH₂ is made only by succinate dehydrogenase.',src:'milesdown:1556662098201'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Monosaccharides',q:'Which monosaccharide is a D-aldohexose whose Fischer projection has hydroxyl groups on the right at C-2, on the left at C-3, and on the right at C-4 and C-5?',choices:['D-galactose','D-mannose','D-glucose','D-allose'],ans:2,exp:'This is **D-glucose**; remember its Fischer projection as "right, left, right, right" from C-2 down. D-mannose differs only at C-2, D-allose only at C-3 and D-galactose only at C-4; in β-D-glucopyranose every bulky substituent is equatorial, which makes it especially stable.',src:'milesdown:1556906209812'},
  {s:'Metabolism: Monosaccharides',q:'Which aldohexose differs from D-glucose only in the configuration at C-4?',choices:['D-mannose','D-galactose','D-talose','D-allose'],ans:1,exp:'**D-galactose** is the C-4 epimer of glucose: its C-4 hydroxyl points left in the Fischer projection and is axial in the pyranose ring. D-mannose is the C-2 epimer, D-allose the C-3 epimer, and D-talose differs at both C-2 and C-4; epimers are diastereomers that differ at exactly one stereocenter.',src:'milesdown:1556906235876'},
  {s:'Lipid Metabolism',q:'α-Linolenic acid is an 18-carbon fatty acid with cis double bonds beginning at C-9, C-12 and C-15, counted from the carboxyl carbon. What is its omega classification?',choices:['Omega-3','Omega-6','Omega-9','Omega-15'],ans:0,exp:'Omega numbering counts from the methyl end: the last double bond begins at C-15, which is 18 − 15 = **3** carbons from the omega carbon, so it is an **omega-3** fatty acid. Omega-9 comes from wrongly using the first Δ position; linoleic acid (Δ9,12) is the classic omega-6 essential fatty acid.',src:'milesdown:1560051077423'},
  {s:'Metabolism: ETC',q:'Adenosine triphosphate is built from which three components?',choices:['Adenine, deoxyribose and three phosphates','Adenine, ribose and three phosphates','Adenine, ribose and two phosphates','Guanine, ribose and three phosphates'],ans:1,exp:'ATP is a nucleotide: the purine **adenine** is linked by an N-glycosidic bond to C-1′ of **ribose**, which carries a chain of **three phosphates** on C-5′; its two phosphoanhydride bonds store usable energy. The deoxyribose version (dATP) is a DNA building block, ADP has only two phosphates, and GTP has guanine instead.',src:'milesdown:1560087105499'},
  {s:'Metabolism',q:'Which expression gives a person\'s body mass index (BMI)?',choices:['mass in kg / (height in m)²','mass in kg / height in m','(height in m)² / mass in kg','mass in kg / (height in cm)²'],ans:0,exp:'BMI = **mass (kg) / height (m)²**, in kg/m². Sense check: a 70 kg adult who is 1.75 m tall has BMI = 70/3.06 ≈ 22.9, in the normal range of 18.5–24.9 (25–29.9 is overweight and 30 or more is obese); using centimeters would give a value 10,000 times too small.',src:'milesdown:1560092882402'},
];
